name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3b281cf-c846-54e8-b9fb-50c47c57c04a', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3b281cf-c846-54e8-b9fb-50c47c57c04a'}, {'abstract': 'The agency will have to go much further than apologies for breaking into the Senate’s computers. Its culture needs an overhaul, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/01/opinion/The-CIAs-Reckless-Breach-of-Trust.html', 'snippet': 'The agency will have to go much further than apologies for breaking into the Senate’s computers. Its culture needs an overhaul, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March, John Brennan, the C.I.A. director, was indignant when Senator Dianne Feinstein charged that the agency had broken into computers used by staff investigators from the Senate Intelligence Committee, which she leads. “As far as the allegations of C.I.A. hacking into Senate computers,” he said, “nothing could be further from the truth. I mean, we wouldn’t do that. I mean, that’s just beyond the scope of reason.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The C.I.A.’s Reckless Breach of Trust', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The C.I.A.’s Reckless Breach of Trust', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on Intelligence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics and Official Misconduct', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brennan, John O', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinstein, Dianne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Uni