Congress, child casualties in Gaza and more.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/01/morning-views-49/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on C.I.A. hacking in the Senate, medical marijuana in California, the immigration crisis and Congress, child casualties in Gaza and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Central Intelligence Agency needs “a thorough overhaul,” at “its very core” for breaking into the computers of the Senate Intelligence Committee, says an editorial in The New York Times. C.I.A. director John Brennan apologized privately to the committee’s leadership and promised to determine who was responsible for the hacking, but the accountability and the apologies should go “much further,” the editorial says. “It’s not just two senators that the C.I.A. has offended by this shocking action. It is all of Congress and, by extension, the American public, which is paying for an intelligence agency that does not seem to understand the most fundamental concept of separation of powers.” The committee was working on a classified report detailing the agency’s controversial antiterror program when the hacking occurred. “Congress is supposed to oversee the intelligence community and rein in its excesses. It cannot possibly do so effectively if it is being spied on by the spy agency, which is supposed to be directing its efforts against foreign terrorists and other threats to national security.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-01T10:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hanna Kozlowska', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozlowska', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', 