nt_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Wolfers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolfers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df52844b-f118-5ce0-b3d0-e9ad88e416f1', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df52844b-f118-5ce0-b3d0-e9ad88e416f1'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: The Kaiser addressed the nation on the day Germany declared war on Russia in 1914.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/01/1914-vociferous-crowds-cheer-kaiser/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: The Kaiser addressed the nation on the day Germany declared war on Russia in 1914.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — Fifty thousand persons assembled yesterday [Aug. 1] afternoon before the Imperial Palace and cheered the Kaiser and the Empire vociferously and continuously. At 6.15 p.m. the Kaiser appeared at one of the windows of the palace, and the enthusiasm of the crowd then became almost frantic. Addressing the great throng in a loud clear voice, the Kaiser said: ‘‘This is a dark day and a sombre hour for Germany. Envious people on every side have forced us to a just defence. The sword is placed in our hands by force. I hope that, if at the last moment my efforts to bring about an understanding between ourselves and our adversaries and to maintain the peace do not succeed, we may, by the help of God, so use our swords that when all is ended we can replace them in their scabbards with honor. A war will ask from us enormous sacrifices of men and of money, but we shall show our enemies what it means to provoke Germany. And now I recommend you all to God. Go to church, kneel before Him and pray that He may sustain our brave army.’’ — The New York Herald, European Edition, August 2, 1914', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/blogs/27iht-old28-emperor/27iht-old28-emperor-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 405, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/blogs/27iht-old28-emperor/27iht-old28-emperor-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 405, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/blogs/27iht-old28-emperor/27iht-old28-emperor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/07/27/blogs/27iht-old28-emperor/27iht-old28-emperor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/blogs/27iht-old28-emperor/27iht-old28-emperor-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1914: Vociferous Crowds Cheer Kaiser', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-01T14:32:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d91bfb1-f079-59c5-9c9c-4b9b4abf1563', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d91bfb1-f079-59c5-9c9c-4b9b4abf1563'}, {'abstract': 'More Moore.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/01/facts-still-have-well-you-know/', 'snippet': 'More Moore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Columbia Journalism Review has more about Stephen Moore’s fact-challenged attempt to demonstrate the virtues of tax cuts, which I wrote about the other day. We learn a bit more about the story — the piece was published because Moore demanded the opportunity to reply to my column about Kansas — which makes it even funnier and more remarkable that he couldn’t be bothered to get the numbers right. Also, my favorite line from the piece:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Facts Still Have, Well, You Know', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-01T14:38:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf4da5bf-ed26-5893-9abf-fd951ae2a176', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf4da5bf-ed26-5893-9abf-fd951ae2a176'}, {'abstract': 'Violence in Gaza, Iraq, Syria and Ukraine has dominated the news this summer, but is drawing little reaction from candidates in the midterm elections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/02/us/foreign-crises-fade-on-2014-midterm-congressional-campaign-trail.html', 'snippet': 'Violence in Gaza, Iraq, Syria and Ukraine has dominated the news this summer, but is drawing little reaction from candidates in the midterm elections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Crises in Gaza, Iraq, Syria and Ukraine have dominated the news this summer and even grabbed the attention of many Americans who are usually indifferent to international affairs. Yet the events might as well be in a parallel universe for all the notice they are getting in this year’s congressional campaigns.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/02/us/DISCONNECT/DISCONNECT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/02/us/DISCONNECT/DISCONNECT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/02/us/DISCONNECT/DISCONNECT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/02/us/DISCONNECT/DISCONNECT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/02/us/DISCONNECT/DISCONNECT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/02/us/DISCONNECT/DISCONNECT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/02/us/DISCONNECT/DISCONNECT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 