{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19823e2e-0ce5-53df-b696-42624e245e61', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19823e2e-0ce5-53df-b696-42624e245e61'}, {'abstract': 'In 1986, the U.S.-Soviet meeting in Reykjavik seemed anything but a Cold War breakthrough.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/books/review/reagan-at-reykjavik-by-ken-adelman.html', 'snippet': 'In 1986, the U.S.-Soviet meeting in Reykjavik seemed anything but a Cold War breakthrough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Failure is in fashion these days. We read about failing fast and failing well, about grit incubated by repeated failure in school and innovation by repeated failure in business. So it may be a good time to consider the hidden virtues of failure in foreign policy. And who better to demonstrate those virtues than one of modern America’s great optimists?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03kurtzphelan/03kurtzphelan-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03kurtzphelan/03kurtzphelan-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03kurtzphelan/03kurtzphelan-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03kurtzphelan/03kurtzphelan-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1296, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03kurtzphelan/03kurtzphelan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03kurtzphelan/03kurtzphelan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03kurtzphelan/03kurtzphelan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Thawing in Iceland', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Thawing in Iceland', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kurtz-Phelan, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adelman, Ken', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Reagan at Reykjavik: Forty-Eight Hours That Ended the Cold War (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-01T15:08:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Kurtz-Phelan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurtz-Phelan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc55a63c-b523-5619-81c5-8795858d4ad3', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc55a63c-b523-5619-81c5-8795858d4ad3'}, {'abstract': 'In many of Jane Gardam’s stories, a figure from the past suddenly looms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/books/review/the-stories-by-jane-gardam.html', 'snippet': 'In many of Jane Gardam’s stories, a figure from the past suddenly looms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A novel can afford to unveil its secrets slowly. Not so a short story. With its constricted circuitry, it has to be a stairway of surprise (in Emerson’s phrase) or the reader will stop climbing at the second or third step. The most incisive stories by Jane Gardam, a writer well known in Britain for children’s books and a host of novels (including the “Old Filth” trilogy that has recently won her new readers in America), are constructed, more often than not, around a double take. From colonial postings abroad, from buried love affairs, from the lists of the presumed dead (or “near-dead,” as they are called in one of Gardam’s ghost-ridden stories), a once-familiar figure suddenly looms. The unsettling appearance of such revenants — as in Proust or Anthony Powell’s “A Dance to the Music of Time” — brings about, in these deftly written tales, a shift, a re-evaluation, a fresh sense of what really happened in the past and an opening into a possibly altered future.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03benfey/03benfey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 905, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03benfey/03benfey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 905}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03benfey/03benfey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03benfey/03benfey-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1358, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03benfey/03benfey-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03benfey/03benfey-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03benfey/03benfey-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Music of Time', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Music of Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benfey, Christopher', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardam, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-01T15:09:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Benfey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benfey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1622e21-e8b4-58ab-9597-5257100545a4', 'word_count': 1148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1622e21-e8b4-58ab-9597-5257100545a4'}, {'abstract': 'The poet and novelist Stephen Crane “raced through his life,” documenting war and poverty until his death at 28.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/books/review/stephen-crane-a-life-of-fire-by-paul-sorrentino.html', 'snippet': 'The poet and novelist Stephen Crane “raced through his life,” documenting war and poverty until his death at 28.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stephen Crane, whose likeness appears on the album cover of “Sgt. Pepper’s Lonely Hearts Club Band,” was America’s first rock-star writer. He self-published his realist novella “Maggie: A Girl of the Streets” at 21, losing so much money that he took to starving himself and wearing rubber boots because he couldn’t afford shoes. By the time he was 23, he had imagined “a creature, naked, bestial, / Who, squatting upon the ground, / Held his heart in his hands, / And ate of it. / I said, ‘Is it good, friend?’ / ‘It is bitter — bitter,’ he answered; / ‘But I like it / Because it is bitter, / And because it is my heart.’ ” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03phillips/03phillips-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 745, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03phillips/03phillips-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 745}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03phillips/03phillips-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 825, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03phillips/03phillips-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1650, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03phillips/03phillips-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03phillips/03phillips-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/03phillips/03phillips-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mind Ablaze', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mind Ablaze', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sorrentino, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crane, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Stephen Crane: A Life of Fire (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-01T15:11:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jayne Anne Phillips', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jayne', 'middlename': 'Anne', 'lastname': 'Phillips', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/51168bee-5d1c-5c1b-a355-f36fc2d1a61d', 'word_count': 1309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51168bee-5d1c-5c1b-a355-f36fc2d1a61d'}, {'abstract': 'An exiled magician tries a risky heist in Lev Grossman’s novel, the final installment of a trilogy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/books/review/the-magicians-land-by-lev-grossman.html', 'snippet': 'An exiled magician tries a risky heist in Lev Grossman’s novel, the final installment of a trilogy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Lev Grossman’s “The Magicians” was like “The Secret History” crossed with “Harry Potter,” and if its sequel, “The Magician King,” was a descendant of “The Chronicles of Narnia” (with a touch of the 1990s flick “The Craft” thrown in), then what cultural mash-up does Lev Grossman conjure in “The Magician’s Land,” the trilogy’s final book? I can’t tell you, because I was too thoroughly swept away by this richly imagined and continually surprising novel to be concerned with cute comparisons. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/0803-bks-LEPUCKI/0803-bks-LEPUCKI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/0803-bks-LEPUCKI/0803-bks-LEPUCKI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 639}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/0803-bks-LEPUCKI/0803-bks-LEPUCKI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/0803-bks-LEPUCKI/0803-bks-LEPUCKI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/0803-bks-LEPUCKI/0803-bks-LEPUCKI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/0803-bks-LEPUCKI/0803-bks-LEPUCKI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/books/review/0803-bks-LEPUCKI/0803-bks-LEPUCKI-th