ce of choreography when when its twists and turns are done right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the outside, Bounden may look like two people arduously inspecting the same iPhone: twisting and turning while grasping opposite ends of the device, eyes fixed on the screen. And essentially that’s what it is, though, if done right, those twists and turns have the elegance of choreography. Created by the Dutch design studio Game Oven, this recently released app steers users through entwining duets choreographed by Ernst Meisner of the Dutch National Ballet. To replicate the swinging, circular moves, you and your partner maneuver an icon along arcing, ever-shifting pathways, inevitably getting tangled. If we can’t put down our phones, Bounden exploits that isolating tendency to bring us closer — physically, at least. It’s fun, especially at early levels, where good dancing isn’t the point. As the game advances, it becomes trickier, monitoring your accuracy and flashing “Hurry up!” if you fall behind the music, like a good-natured ballet master. Videos, featuring dancers from Dutch National Ballet’s junior company, offer something to aspire to. (gameovenstudios.com.) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03GUIDE4/03GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03GUIDE4/03GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03GUIDE4/03GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03GUIDE4/03GUIDE4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03GUIDE4/03GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03GUIDE4/03GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03GUIDE4/03GUIDE4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hold On to Your Phone!', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance: Hold on to Your Phone!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dutch National Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bounden (Video Game)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-01T16:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb849288-7a82-58db-803c-b2da9fdd2388', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb849288-7a82-58db-803c-b2da9fdd2388'}, {'abstract': 'Ishmael Butler and Tendai Maraire — who compose the creative center of Shabazz Palaces — are shaking up Seattle’s diverse, idiosyncratic and growing hip-hop scene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/03/arts/music/shabazz-palaces-shake-up-seattles-hip-hop-scene.html', 'snippet': 'Ishmael Butler and Tendai Maraire — who compose the creative center of Shabazz Palaces — are shaking up Seattle’s diverse, idiosyncratic and growing hip-hop scene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — It was a bright summer day here, but inside Shabazz Palaces’ studio, located in the Old Rainier Brewery building, it was dark as midnight. There were no windows, the walls were painted black, and the womblike interior was broken up only by a leopard-print rug. “Hip-hop should only take place at night,” said Palaceer Lazaro (real name: Ishmael Butler), 45, the mastermind of Shabazz Palaces, the eccentric experimental hip-hop act. “The parties, this, everything.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03SHABAZZ1/03SHABAZZ1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03SHABAZZ1/03SHABAZZ1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03SHABAZZ1/03SHABAZZ1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03SHABAZZ1/03SHABAZZ1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1188, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/arts/03SHABAZZ1/03SHABAZZ1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/