}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Metals and Minerals', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-02T04:56:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c8fa534-d139-56c8-a462-206155b5b086', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c8fa534-d139-56c8-a462-206155b5b086'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/02/arts/television/whats-on-tv-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:35 A.M. (Encore) THROW MOMMA FROM THE TRAIN (1987) Danny DeVito directs and stars as Owen, a downtrodden writer who dreams of killing his dominating and trollish mother (Anne Ramsey, above, with Mr. DeVito), but can’t bring himself to finish the deed. Larry (Billy Crystal) is Owen’s writing instructor and is similarly plagued by a famous ex-wife who is airing their dirty laundry to the entire country. When Larry suggests that Owen watch Alfred Hitchcock’s “Strangers on a Train” to help his writing, it instead gives Owen a different kind of inspiration: a murder swap. “Most inspired, however, is the teamwork between Mr. Crystal and Mr. DeVito, who make one of the oddest pairs imaginable but have a rapport that’s a delight,” Janet Maslin wrote in her review for The New York Times. Speaking of Mr. DeVito’s performance, she said, “He’s irresistible even when playing someone only a mother could love.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'key