ae30747-1e48-5158-a1a7-8b7f1cf8cfec', 'word_count': 981, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cae30747-1e48-5158-a1a7-8b7f1cf8cfec'}, {'abstract': 'Why Harriet Jacobs, a slave turned journalist, urged black soldiers to keep fighting.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/04/the-meaning-of-emancipation-day/', 'snippet': 'Why Harriet Jacobs, a slave turned journalist, urged black soldiers to keep fighting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By August 1864, the abolitionist writer and former slave Harriet Jacobs had spent more than a year and a half providing relief for the refugees flooding the contraband camps of Alexandria, Va. – some 6,000 people, she reported that spring to her employer, the New York Yearly Meeting of Friends. By Jacobs’s count that March, the freedmen had put up 700 small houses in just eight months. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Meaning of Emancipation Day', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEXANDRIA (VA)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-04T16:07:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott M. Korb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a5c3391-bc7b-5b6a-b173-95eff7b0472c', 'word_count': 1280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a5c3391-bc7b-5b6a-b173-95eff7b0472c'}, {'abstract': 'This spicy, juicy meal, perfect for the summer, is one of a variety of Tunisian grilled salads. The couscous can be served warm or at room temperature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/04/health/tunisian-grilled-peppers-and-tomatoes-with-couscous.html', 'snippet': 'This spicy, juicy meal, perfect for the summer, is one of a variety of Tunisian grilled salads. The couscous can be served warm or at room temperature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This makes a heavenly summer meal. Reconstitute some couscous and top with this spicy, juicy mix of grilled pepper and tomato salad. You can serve the couscous warm or at room temperature. The pepper salad is typical of Tunisian grilled salads (mechwya), of which there are many versions (eggplant can be included in the mix). In Tunisia, a spice mix called tabil would be used to season the peppers. I keep the mix on hand, without the dried garlic, but here I’ve given you a mix of spices to use. You can substitute tabil if you have some; the mix will be a little hotter because there is cayenne in the tabil.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/05/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1207, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/05/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tunisian Grilled Peppers and Tomatoes with Couscous', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couscous', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Peppers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tomatoes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-04T16:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae314bbd-7cb9-5331-a03c-1e9d1caab3ae', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae314bbd-7cb9-5331-a03c-1e9d1caab3ae'}, {'abstract': 'Losing the battle with Burmese pythons, Florida wildlife biologists are fighting back against the tegu, the latest nonnative species to find a comfortable home out in the Everglades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/05/science/a-lizard-interloper-presents-challenge-in-florida.html', 'snippet': 'Losing the battle with Burmese pythons, Florida wildlife biologists are fighting back against the tegu, the latest nonnative species to find a comfortable home out in the Everglades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLORIDA CITY, Fla. — Deer flies swarmed around Frank Mazzotti and Joy Vinci as they stooped to get a closer look at the creature thrashing around in a metal trap they had laid off a weedy dirt road. Inside it was what some biologists consider the most troublesome invasive species in the Everglades: not a Burmese python, but a 24-inch lizard, the Argentine black and white tegu. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/05LIZA/05LIZA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/05/science/05LIZA/05LIZA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 320}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/05LIZA/05LIZA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 546, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/05LIZA/05LIZA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 1597, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/science/05LIZA/05LIZA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/05/science/05LIZA/05LIZA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '