aption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/05/business/dbpix-subprime2/dbpix-subprime2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Focusing on G.M. Unit, U.S. Starts Civil Inquiry of Subprime Car Lending', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Starts Civil Inquiry of Subprime Car Lending', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Financing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Insurance and Liability', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bharara, Preet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-05T01:23:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Corkery and Jessica Silver-Greenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corkery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver-Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d6d5780-76c3-5990-8cc9-1815790bc81a', 'word_count': 1029, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d6d5780-76c3-5990-8cc9-1815790bc81a'}, {'abstract': 'State Senator Chris McDaniel filed a formal challenge on Monday to the outcome of the Republican United States Senate primary, citing evidence he said would show “a pattern of conduct” resulting in an improper victory for his rival, Senator Thad Cochran.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/05/us/mississippi-cochran-victory-is-challenged.html', 'snippet': 'State Senator Chris McDaniel filed a formal challenge on Monday to the outcome of the Republican United States Senate primary, citing evidence he said would show “a pattern of conduct” resulting in an improper victory for his rival, Senator Thad C...', 'lead_paragraph': 'State Senator Chris McDaniel filed a formal challenge on Monday to the outcome of the Republican United States Senate primary, citing evidence he said would show “a pattern of conduct” resulting in an improper victory for his rival, Senator Thad Cochran. Mitch Tyner, a lawyer for the McDaniel campaign, said the evidence would show that thousands of votes in the June 24 runoff were invalid, some of them cast by people who may have violated state election law by voting in the Republican runoff after having voted in the Democratic primary (there is no party registration in Mississippi, though so-called crossover voting is illegal). Mr. Tyner said he was not seeking a new election but a declaration by the state Republican executive committee that Mr. McDaniel was the winner, and he asked for a hearing within eight days. Mr. Cochran won the runoff by roughly 7,700 votes, a victory that was helped by turning out black voters who rarely participate in Republican primaries. Mr. Tyner said the McDaniel campaign’s research would show that Mr. McDaniel had won by 25,000 votes. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mississippi: Cochran Victory Is Challenged', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mississippi: Cochran Victory Is Challenged', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cochran, Thad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDaniel, Chris (1972- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-05T01:27:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Campbell Robertson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Campbell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robertson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ac64be7-8773-5aee-80bb-233a72389fd0', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ac64be7-8773-5aee-80bb-233a72389fd0'}, {'abstract': 'With support, a new style of emerging market hero can lead African nations to a democratic rather than autocratic future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/05/opinion/david-brooks-the-battle-of-the-regimes.html', 'snippet': 'With support, a new style of emerging market hero can lead African nations to a democratic rather than autocratic future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Mwangi grew up on the slopes of the Aberdare Mountains in central Kenya. His father lost his life during the Mau Mau uprising against the colonial authorities. His mother raised seven children, making sure both the girls and the boys were well educated. Everybody in the family worked at a series of street businesses to pay the bills.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/01/op