tml', 'snippet': 'The ideal solution would be for all countries to agree to end the advantages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Evidence of excessive corporate tax avoidance keeps piling up. And so do Congress’s excuses, mainly from Republicans, for not doing anything to curb it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tax Dodge Goes On', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Tax Dodge Goes On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Shelters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-05T10:21:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/edad976b-01db-5cf5-abf4-d7b08b046aed', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edad976b-01db-5cf5-abf4-d7b08b046aed'}, {'abstract': 'Since coming to office last year Premier Li Keqiang has stepped into the footsteps of his predecessor, Wen Jiabao, who was known as China’s “consoler in chief.”', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/05/at-quake-scene-leaders-dip-in-the-mud-gets-close-attention/', 'snippet': 'Since coming to office last year Premier Li Keqiang has stepped into the footsteps of his predecessor, Wen Jiabao, who was known as China’s “consoler in chief.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 12:09 a.m. ET| Of the images that have emerged from the aftermath of the deadly earthquake that hit southwestern China on Sunday, one of the most popular has been that of a man washing his hands. The man is Premier Li Keqiang, and he isn’t washing his hands just anywhere. He’s doing it in a puddle oozing with red mud.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Quake Scene, Leader’s Dip in the Mud Gets Close Attention', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earthquakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rescues', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Li Keqiang', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wen Jiabao', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APPLE DAILY', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yunnan Province (China)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-05T10:23:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Austin Ramzy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Austin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ramzy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5018a6c6-2674-5593-a0ea-8a7b9bd51d39', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5018a6c6-2674-5593-a0ea-8a7b9bd51d39'}, {'abstract': 'Turkey’s experience addressing terrorism succeeded because concessions amenable to both sides were finally made.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/05/opinion/the-lessons-from-turkey.html', 'snippet': 'Turkey’s experience addressing terrorism succeeded because concessions amenable to both sides were finally made.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Re “What Turks can teach Israel” (Opinion, July 24): Mustafa Akyol misrepresents how Turkey dealt with the threat of the Kurdistan Workers’ Party and the lessons to be drawn from that conflict’s resolution. He argues that the P.K.K. curbed violence when the Turkish state began making concessions to its demands related to Kurdish identity and autonomy. He omits that the state negotiated from a position of strength, and that its concessions came after years of responding to the group with unremitting force. The P.K.K. capitulated because it saw it could not win through violence and because it lacked sufficient backing from other countries or nonstate actors in the region.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lessons From Turkey', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-05T10:33:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/eddeb9a5-50f9-5729-b555-54116518b23f', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eddeb9a5-50f9-5729-b555-54116518b23f'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on regulating marijuana, a flatter health care curve, the legacy of James S. Brady and Lake Michigan’s tasty water.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/05/morning-views-51/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on regulating marijuana, a flatter health care curve, the legacy of James S. Brady and Lake Michigan’s tasty water.', 'lead_paragraph': '“As voters and lawmakers in more states decide to legalize marijuana, policy makers will have to answer a fresh and difficult question,”\xa0writes Vikas Bajaj for The New York Times. “How should governments regulate the production and sale of the drug?” A functional regulatory system “has to limit the increase in drug abuse that is likely to accompany lower prices and greater availability” in a post-legalization America. First, lawmakers must institute a viable method of taxation — “policies that allow licensed businesses to undercut the illegal market but keep prices high enough so dependence on the drug does not increase a lot.” Second, distributors must not be permitted to market to minors: “Controls should emulate the restrictions on targeting young people, banning outdoor advertising and product placements that the tobacco industry accepted as part of its settlement with the state attorney general in 1998.” And finally, streams of cultivation and retail should be segregated — to avoid a similar system of juggernauts that dominates the tobacco industry. Ultimately, “whatever states decide to do, it is important that they stand ready to modify policies as legal marijuana markets evolve.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-05T10:37:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4331290d-f368-5d40-ada8-56cbb2e33f8b', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4331290d-f368-5d40-ada8-56cbb2e33f8b'}, {'abstract': 'Can you choose the best word or phrase for each blank?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/05/test-yourself-emoji/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose the best word or phrase for each blank?', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re experimenting with a new, slightly more elaborate version of our long-running English language-arts Test Yourself feature. Tell us what you think.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/fashion/27SUBEMOJIS-LN/27SUBEMOJIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/07/27/fashion/27SUBEMOJIS-LN/27SUBEMOJIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 568}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/fashion/27SUBEMOJIS-LN/27SUBEMOJIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 969, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/fashion/27SUBEMOJIS-LN/27SUBEMOJIS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1939, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/fashion/27SUBEMOJIS-LN/27SUBEMOJIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/07/27/fashion/27SUBEMOJIS-LN/27SUBEMOJIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/27/fashion/27SUBEMOJIS-LN/27SUBEMOJIS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Emoji', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-05T10:40:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92182290-0061-5c23-9a6c-34574e8e22fe', 'word_count': 78, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92182290-0061-5c23-9a6c-34574e8e22fe'}, {'abstract': 'The German auto giant said officials from an agency that enforces antimonopoly and pricing rules had raided its offices in Shanghai.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/06/business/international/chinese-regulators-search-daimler-offices.html', 'snippet': 'The German auto giant said officials from an agency that enforces antimonopoly and pricing rules had raided its offices in Shanghai.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The German automobile giant Daimler has become the latest multinational company to bear the brunt of a Chinese regulatory inquiry, and an official from the agency that visited the company’s offices in Shanghai said on Wednesday that it was under investigation for “monopolistic behavior.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/06/business/06daimler-pic1/06daimler-pic1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/06/business/06daimler-pic1/06daimler-pic1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/06/business/06daimler-pic1/06daimler-pic1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/06/business/