'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'Emily B. Hager', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/cad2e666-5e24-5780-bbc5-e4ec41ca5b90', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/cad2e666-5e24-5780-bbc5-e4ec41ca5b90'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama vowed a long-term partnership that extended beyond exploiting the mineral wealth of the countries for the benefit of the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/06/world/africa/african-leaders-sit-down-with-american-investors.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama vowed a long-term partnership that extended beyond exploiting the mineral wealth of the countries for the benefit of the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama convened a giant game of “Let’s Make a Deal” between the United States and Africa on Tuesday, bringing together nearly 50 African leaders with American investors for what he promised would be a long-term partnership that went beyond extracting “minerals from the ground for our growth.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'African Leaders Sit Down With American Investors', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Meeting Teams African Leaders and American Investors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Landler, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commerce Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T04:15:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/353f5478-c8e8-5095-aa61-b0502a8e8e97', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/353f5478-c8e8-5095-aa61-b0502a8e8e97'}, {'abstract': 'As he vies for the presidency of the governing body of world chess, Garry Kasparov can’t escape his fiercest opponent: the president of Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/magazine/garry-kasparov.html', 'snippet': 'As he vies for the presidency of the governing body of world chess, Garry Kasparov can’t escape his fiercest opponent: the president of Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Garry Kasparov, the former world chess champion, put his head down, hunched his shoulders and walked hurriedly down the carpeted corridor of the Sands Cotai Central, a labyrinthine new complex on reclaimed land in Macau. Downstairs were two of the world’s most lucrative casinos and retailers like Saint Laurent Paris and Fabio Caviglia. There were restaurants, spas and an array of theme-park attractions from DreamWorks, including a daily parade through the lobby with characters from “Shrek,” “Madagascar” and “Kung Fu Panda.” Here on the fourth floor, though, hundreds of boys and girls from across Asia had just wrapped up their matches in a chess tournament. Spilling into the corridor, many of them gaped at Kasparov barreling by, as if Shrek himself had wandered up to the wrong floor. That was why he was rushing, he explained, when I managed to catch up to him as he turned toward a bank of elevators. “If you stop,” he said, “you’ll be there for 30 minutes.” He did not sound angry, just matter of fact. “Everyone circles around. They want pictures.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/magazine/10kasparov1/mag-10Kasparov-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/10/magazine/10kasparov1/mag-10Kasparov-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/magazine/10kasparov1/mag-10Kasparov-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/magazine/10kasparov1/mag-10Kasparov-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1268, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/magazine/10kasparov1/mag-10Kasparov-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/10/magazine/10kasparov1/mag-10Kasparov-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/magazine/10kasparov1/mag-10Kasparov-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vladimir Putin’s Chess-Master Nemesis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Man Who Would be King', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasparov, Garry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ilyumzhinov, Kirsan N', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T04:15:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Lee Myers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Myers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b89a0ee5-f85b-5569-985a-5221d87f2ad6', 'word_count': 5336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b89a0ee5-f85b-5569-985a-5221d87f2ad6'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/06/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (GSN) SKIN WARS Body painters put their creativity and technique to the test — think of the Sports Illustrated swimsuit issue, only with pasties and G-strings — for a prize package comprising $100,000, a year’s supply of paint and a stint as a guest artist at Imats, a trade show in New York. The series begins as the contestants (like the artist Gear Boxxx, above) embark on a tour of Hollywood before being given two hours to transfer their impressions onto the torsos of their models. Then they’re given five more hours to take judges on a virtual tour of their hometowns via a full-body painting. In each episode the contestant with the least compelling creation must watch as the living canvas washes herself off onstage. Rebecca Romijn hosts; RuPaul Charles is joined by the body painters Craig Tracy and Robin Slonina at the judges’ table. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T04:21:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea1fdc08-d5b2-5677-a648-9eb9ffafc1f8', 'word_count': 705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea1fdc08-d5b2-5677-a648-9eb9ffafc1f8'}, {'abstract': 'President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi’s plan to have the overhaul completed in a year was met with skepticism by some.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/06/world/middleeast/egypt-has-ambitious-plan-for-suez-canal-expansion.html', 'snippet': 'President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi’s plan to have the overhaul completed in a year was met with skepticism by some.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — When the Suez Canal opened in 1869, it had taken hundreds of thousands of Egyptian workers 10 years to complete it. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/06/world/EGYPT/EGYPT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/06/world/EGYPT/EGYPT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 767}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jum