ons', 'value': 'Walgreen Company', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T10:27:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Stevenson and Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d3f3312-11b2-54e2-a170-e10867a69613', 'word_count': 1063, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d3f3312-11b2-54e2-a170-e10867a69613'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on violence at Rikers Island, fines rather than guilt, pride and disappointment on the Israeli right, and the dangers of Ecstasy.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/06/morning-views-52/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on violence at Rikers Island, fines rather than guilt, pride and disappointment on the Israeli right, and the dangers of Ecstasy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“A much-anticipated investigation by the Justice Department has uncovered what amounted to a chamber of horrors at New York City’s Rikers Island jail, where teenagers were beaten and battered for minor infractions by correction officers who acted without fear of discovery or punishment by senior officials,” says an editorial in The New York Times. The report reveals “‘a deep-seated culture of violence’ that demands immediate remedial action by New York City.” Adolescents on Rikers “were in constant danger of physical harm even when they present no risk to the system or safety of the staff” and “force was routinely used not so much to keep order but for the express purpose of ‘inflicting injuries and pain.’” This scandalous behavior went on for so long because of “a departmental code of silence” that “aided and abetted this brutal regime and shielded its practitioners from discovery and official rebuke.” The report recommends the removal of all teenagers from Rikers and improvement in officer training.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T10:40:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9717a275-e793-50a7-b11b-403d95db291e', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9717a275-e793-50a7-b11b-403d95db291e'}, {'abstract': 'The Rosetta probe has arrived at its destination for the first extended, close examination of a comet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/07/science/space/spacecraft-pulls-alongside-comet.html', 'snippet': 'The Rosetta probe has arrived at its destination for the first extended, close examination of a comet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After 10 years and a journey of four billion miles, the European Space Agency’s Rosetta spacecraft arrived at its destination on Wednesday for the first extended, close examination of a comet.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/07/science/space/07comet-cnd/07comet-cnd-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/07/science/space/07co