han the Earth, on the solar system’s most photogenic planet. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s always something strange going on with Saturn, the solar system’s most photogenic planet – from the famous rings that baffled Galileo 400 years ago to the methane dunes and lakes of its smoggy moon Titan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Storm Chasing on Saturn', 'kicker': 'Out There', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Saturn (Planet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enceladus (Saturn Moon)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voyager Project', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Aeronautics and Space Administration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T14:09:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Space & Cosmos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Overbye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Overbye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f7d06b9-56b9-5630-beec-f1c03c9f8dec', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f7d06b9-56b9-5630-beec-f1c03c9f8dec'}, {'abstract': 'The sun is slowly rising over Saturn’s North Pole, exposing an immense six-sided hurricane. The storm, big enough to swallow four Earths, was first spotted by the Voyager missions in the early 1980s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/science/space/100000003031795/storm-chasing-on-saturn.html', 'snippet': 'The sun is slowly rising over Saturn’s North Pole, exposing an immense six-sided hurricane. The storm, big enough to swallow four Earths, was first spotted by the Voyager missions in the early 1980s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sun is slowly rising over 