'After months of stiff resistance, the bank has abruptly offered to pay $16 billion. The turning point came in an unexpected ruling from a federal judge known to be hard on big banks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 9:58 p.m. | After months of lowball offers and heels dug in, it took only 24 hours for Bank of America to suddenly cave in to the government, agreeing to the largest single federal settlement in the history of corporate America.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/07/business/dbpix-bofa1/dbpix-bofa1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/07/business/dbpix-bofa1/dbpix-bofa1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/07/business/dbpix-bofa1/dbpix-bofa1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/07/business/dbpix-bofa1/dbpix-bofa1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1424, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/07/business/dbpix-bofa1/dbpix-bofa1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/07/business/dbpix-bofa1/dbpix-bofa1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/07/business/dbpix-bofa1/dbpix-bofa1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bank of America Offers U.S. Biggest Settlement in History Over Toxic Mortgage Loans', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bank Offers U.S. Biggest Settlement in History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holder, Eric H Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moynihan, Brian T', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Countrywide Financial Corp', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Merrill Lynch & Co', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T20:27:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess and Michael Corkery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corkery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb13d8d5-8211-520c-bd19-c318c0afb4b6', 'word_count': 1212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb13d8d5-8211-520c-bd19-c318c0afb4b6'}, {'abstract': 'The artist Swoon blurs the line between where her artworks end and her activism begins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/arts/design/swoon-blurs-the-line-between-art-and-activism.html', 'snippet': 'The artist Swoon blurs the line between where her artworks end and her activism begins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a glowing paper cutout pinned over her heart, the artist known as Swoon led a procession through the Brooklyn Museum early one summer night to her installation “Submerged Motherlands,” a site-specific jumble that includes two cantilevered rafts, seemingly cobbled out of junk; a tree, of fabric and wire, that reaches to the rotunda; and nooks of stenciled portraits.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life of Wonderment', 'kicker': 'A Cultural Leap ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life of Wonderment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swoon (1978- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T20:29:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44202cae-98d5-5784-b205-0efc7e7db9ac', 'word_count': 1828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44202cae-98d5-5784-b205-0efc7e7db9ac'}, {'abstract': 'In 2009, Michael Sokolove profiled Allonzo Treir, a young basketball player who comes from the same overheated world of ‘‘elite grassroots basketball’’ that Reeves Wiedeman wrote about in last weekend’s issue.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/06/is-allonzo-trier-still-in-the-game/', 'snippet': 'In 2009, Michael Sokolove profiled Allonzo Treir, a young basketball player who comes from the same overheated world of ‘‘elite grassroots basketball’’ that Reeves Wiedeman wrote about in last weekend’s issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve found that a downside of being a magazine writer is that I don’t get to do follow-up stories, as they’re called in the newspaper business. I generally write about a subject and then, professionally speaking, move on. But I do maintain a personal curiosity, and I have a long list of people I once wrote about whom I occasionally do a little research on to see what they’re up to.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/04/magazine/Trier1/Trier1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/04/magazine/Trier1/Trier1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/04/magazine/Trier1/Trier1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/04/magazine/Trier1/Trier1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1346, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/04/magazine/Trier1/Trier1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/04/magazine/Trier1/Trier1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/04/magazine/Trier1/Trier1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Allonzo Trier Still Have Game?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-06T20:32:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By MICHAEL SOKOLOVE', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SOKOLOVE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a2d00e3-da76-5b91-81a9-6390a2bdbd75', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a2d00e3-da76-5b91-81a9-6390a2bdbd75'}, {'abstract': 'The retailer and designer shares snapshots and travel suggestions from a recent vacation spent visiting family.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/06/hong-kong-travel-diary-jade-lai-creatures-of-comfort/', 'snippet': 'The retailer and designer shares snapshots and travel suggestions from a recent vacation spent visiting family.', 'lead_paragraph': "“Most people, when they talk about Hong Kong, they’re like, ‘Oh, the parties, the bars, the shopping,'” says Jade Lai (top row, center), the Hong Kong-raised owner of Creatures of Comfort\xa0— a boutique with locations in New York and Los Angeles — and the designer of its in-house clothing and accessories line. “That’s all nice, but for me it’s about spending time with family — hiking, going to the beach, food.”", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/06/t-magazine/06hartman-lai/06hartman-lai-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/06/t-magazine/06hartman-lai/06hartman-lai-article