ne}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19a45de1-1238-57a3-8aa3-465555e31346', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19a45de1-1238-57a3-8aa3-465555e31346'}, {'abstract': 'The clashes between the Lebanese Army and Sunni extremist fighters from the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria, who occupied a Lebanese border town last weekend, showed some signs of easing on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/07/world/middleeast/lebanon-border-standoff-eases.html', 'snippet': 'The clashes between the Lebanese Army and Sunni extremist fighters from the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria, who occupied a Lebanese border town last weekend, showed some signs of easing on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The clashes between the Lebanese Army and Sunni extremist fighters from the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria, who occupied a Lebanese border town last weekend, showed some signs of easing on Wednesday, as a cease-fire that had collapsed the day before was revived for 24 hours and the militant group, known as ISIS, released three of at least 13 hostages its fighters had seized. At least some of the fighters were reported to have withdrawn from the town, Arsal, and emergency aid workers were able to reach civilians trapped by five days of fighting. But sporadic shelling interrupted the cease-fire throughout the day as Lebanese troops tightened a cordon around the town. In a new sign of Arab concern about Lebanon’s stability, the army received $1 billion in assistance on Wednesday from Saudi Arabia, which has supported the insurgency against the Syrian government but has joined other nations in condemning ISIS as a destabilizing terrorist group. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lebanon: Border Standoff Eases', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lebanon: Border Standoff Eases', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 's