ect on the forces that led Europe sleepwalking to self-destruction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The centenary of the epic carnage we know as World War I has generated a tide of analyses, commentaries, exhibitions and ceremonies. Many people have been drawn to visit the battlefields, memorials and cemeteries of Europe, trying to comprehend how civilized nations could have been reduced to a slaughter in which as many as 20 million soldiers and civilians suffered horrifying deaths. When confronted with so cataclysmic an event, it is difficult not to seek a moral, a lesson that can guide us away from a repetition of such horrors.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Guns of August', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Guns of August', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hollande, Francois', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-07T01:09:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Serge Schmemann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fcb3242-bb8e-55c7-9b38-87a19b46ba72', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fcb3242-bb8e-55c7-9b38-87a19b46ba72'}, {'abstract': '“Every civilian casualty is a 