rganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f321fe19-c5b9-5057-a712-e85ea73cb2ff', 'word_count': 646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f321fe19-c5b9-5057-a712-e85ea73cb2ff'}, {'abstract': 'The long-delayed “May the Circle Remain Unbroken” is finally out this month. The photographer’s close friend Tara St. Hill talks T through some of the tome’s intimate images.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/07/corinne-day-photographs-may-the-circle-remain-unbroken-posthumously-published-book/', 'snippet': 'The long-delayed “May the Circle Remain Unbroken” is finally out this month. The photographer’s close friend Tara St. Hill talks T through some of the tome’s intimate images.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Throughout her career, and especially during the 1990s, the photographer Corinne Day often escaped the fashion scene in which she had forged her reputation with a controversial Kate Moss shoot and other projects for glossies like Vogue. Instead, she documented her friends: models, stylists and musicians caught in a drug- and sex-addled haze. Day, a relentless diarist who died in 2010 at age 48, was unapologetic in her gaze. A portion of her ’90s-era snapshots appeared in “Diary” (2000), a stark, dark book that helped cement her position in the mainstream fashion industry as a grunge-era pioneer. Now, more than 150 additional images have been carefully documented in “May the Circle Remain Unbroken,” a photo book from the British publisher Mörel (the first since Day’s death in 2010).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Insider’s Perspective on Corinne Day’s Posthumously Published Book of Photographs', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Day, Corinne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-07T13:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Moshakis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moshakis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c473424f-f6ef-5b66-a56f-35932355c40e', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c473424f-f6ef-5b66-a56f-35932355c40e'}, {'abstract': 'Once, the Trapper Keeper promised to solve your organizational woes while simultaneously elevating you to cool-kid status. Today’s organizational systems should be even simpler (no gadgets required).', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/07/simple-solutions-for-back-to-school-student-organization-sorry-no-trapper-keeper/', 'snippet': 'Once, the Trapper Keeper promised to solve your organizational woes while simultaneously elevating you to cool-kid status. Today’s organizational systems should be even simpler (no gadgets required).', 'lead_paragraph': 'While updating my calendar today, I realized that my older son returns to school in less than a week. The end of summer always takes me by surprise, but it has been particularly swift and stealthy in its approach this year. Just as I was wrapping my head around the hideous reality of back-to-school shopping, I spotted a sweet tweet that made me yearn for my long-lost adolescence: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/29/us/motherlode-trapper-keeper/motherlode-trapper-keeper-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 218, 'width': 303, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/29/us/motherlode-tra