k Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T15:15:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/092be57d-dcbe-5e67-9605-c448c5d163e7', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/092be57d-dcbe-5e67-9605-c448c5d163e7'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE INVISIBLE BRIDGE: The Fall of Nixon and the Rise of Reagan,  by Rick Perlstein. (Simon & Schuster, $37.50.) Engrossing and at times mordantly funny, Perlstein’s book treats the years 1973-76 as a Rosetta stone for American politics today.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T15:16:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dd8d16b-98ac-53d3-9cbb-d85b8bf8b010', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dd8d16b-98ac-53d3-9cbb-d85b8bf8b010'}, {'abstract': 'Liane Moriarty, whose “Big Little Lies” is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, realized something while reading “Fifty Shades of Grey”: “I couldn’t write a sexy spanking scene to save my life.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Liane Moriarty, whose “Big Little Lies” is No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, realized something while reading “Fifty Shades of Grey”: “I couldn’t write a sexy spanking scene to save my life.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Coming Clean: The Australian novelist Liane Moriarty, like a lot of writers, is ambivalent about modern technology. “I have social media anxiety,” she confessed on her blog last year, and she wasn’t kidding — that post, dated May 6, 2013, remains her most recent as this column goes to press, and the blog as a whole has averaged just a handful of entries per year since it began in 2010. Too bad, since Moriarty turns out to be a natural blogger, droll and daffy and confiding. Here she is, for instance, on reading “Fifty Shades of Grey”: “Why didn’t any of you lovely readers tell me that you wanted more spanking? (Ha.) . . . Maybe you realized that I couldn’t write a sexy spanking scene to save my life. (Well, I don’t know. Perhaps I could to save my life. Except I’d be quite nervous and distracted by the person trying to kill me.)” Moriarty does maintain an active presence on Facebook, where, to promote her latest novel, “Big Little Lies” — new at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list — she recently asked readers to reveal little lies of their own. “My publishers asked me to get in the spirit of things by also making a confession,” she wrote, “so here’s one: When my son’s kindergarten class was putting together a recipe book of ‘favorite easy recipes’ I sent in a nutritious-sounding avocado muffin recipe that I had never made in my life just so I would look like a good mother.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10insidelist/10insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10insidelist/10insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10insidelist/10insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10insidelist/10insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10insidelist/10insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T15:16:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c94a291-7cc5-5ab0-985f-1e37c88a6e10', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c94a291-7cc5-5ab0-985f-1e37c88a6e10'}, {'abstract': 'In this brief biography, Adam Phillips focuses on the discovery of the unconscious.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/books/review/becoming-freud-by-adam-phillips.html', 'snippet': 'In this brief biography, Adam Phillips focuses on the discovery of the unconscious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This book is a biography not of Freud and psychoanalysis but of Freud as psychoanalysis. Adam Phillips explains that what the reader does not need in any biographer’s attempt to write “that impossible thing, a Freudian life of the young Freud, is the always fanciful (i.e. wishful), novelettish setting of scenes, and thumbnail sketches of characters, with their suppositions about what people were thinking and feeling and doing.” What is needed, he believes, is what he supplies: a sketchy chronology of Freud’s first 50 years threaded through the step-by-step story, richly told and richly interpreted, of how psychoanalysis came to be.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10GORNICK/10gornick-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10GORNICK/10gornick-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 960}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10GORNICK/10gornick-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10GORNICK/10gornick-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1280, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 