/fb0fe471-4c4e-5184-a035-51fd57bc1b73'}, {'abstract': 'The heroine of Anya Ulinich’s graphic novel goes on a campaign to rectify her woeful romantic inexperience.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/books/review/lena-finkles-magic-barrel-by-anya-ulinich.html', 'snippet': 'The heroine of Anya Ulinich’s graphic novel goes on a campaign to rectify her woeful romantic inexperience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most compelling panels in “Lena Finkle’s Magic Barrel,” Anya Ulinich’s engrossing graphic novel about the vicissitudes of love, family, immigration and art, is a full-page depiction of the title character staring into a mirror. With resolve in her deep-set eyes, she hacks away at her hair with a fearsome pair of scissors. This is Lena Finkle, and Ulinich renders her in a naturalistic style, with sophisticated use of perspective, shadowing and ink-wash technique. As Lena lops off her hair, she recalls an argument with her ex-husband. This memory is presented cartoonishly; the remembered Lena’s frown is a slash, her curly hair a furious scribble.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/29/books/review/0810-bks-WALDMAN/0810-bks-WALDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 867, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/07/29/books/review/0810-bks-WALDMAN/0810-bks-WALDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 867}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/29/books/review/0810-bks-WALDMAN/0810-bks-WALDMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/29/books/review/0810-bks-WALDMAN/0810-bks-WALDMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1418, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/29/books/review/0810-bks-WALDMAN/0810-bks-WALDMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/07/29/books/review/0810-bks-WALDMAN/0810-bks-WALDMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/29/books/review/0810-bks-WALDMAN/0810-bks-WALDMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unmasters of Sex', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unmasters of Sex', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldman, Ayelet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ulinich, Anya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Lena Finkle's Magic Barrel (Book)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T15:25:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ayelet Waldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ayelet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3e3074d-5292-5911-926e-0ef24061587d', 'word_count': 1110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3e3074d-5292-5911-926e-0ef24061587d'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Schmidt surveys 700 years of the novel, from Austen to Zola.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/books/review/the-novel-by-michael-schmidt.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Schmidt surveys 700 years of the novel, from Austen to Zola.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Schmidt’s 1,172 pages encompass similarly big numbers. Seven hundred years of fiction are chronicled, hundreds of novelists looked at, and even more novels summarized. The biggest number of all, which can only be guessed at, wincingly, is how many hours of reading this intrepid book distills between its straining covers. Schmidt reveres Cervantes as one of the fathers of modern fiction, and there is something quixotic about Schmidt’s own enterprise. As the French general Bosquet said, watching the charge of the Light Brigade into the valley of death, “C’est magnifique.” But, to paraphrase the second half of his tribute, “Ce n’est pas la critique littéraire.” It is not literary criticism — at least not the currently approved kind that gets you a Ph.D., tenure and an endowed chair. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/0810-bks-Sutherland/0810-bks-Sutherland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/0810-bks-Sutherland/0810-bks-Sutherland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 618}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/0810-bks-Sutherland/0810-bks-Sutherland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 995, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/0810-bks-Sutherland/0810-bks-Sutherland-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1990, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/0810-bks-Sutherland/0810-bks-Sutherland-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/0810-bks-Sutherland/0810-bks-Sutherland-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/0810-bks-Sutherland/0810-bks-Sutherland-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'You Mean It’s Not Dead?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You Mean It’s Not Dead?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Michael (1947- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Novel: A Biography (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T15:25:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Sutherland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sutherland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e317b909-efe3-56f7-b1bf-6cf0c8802c23', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e317b909-efe3-56f7-b1bf-6cf0c8802c23'}, {'abstract': 'In Bret Anthony Johnston’s debut novel, the rescue of a missing boy is only the beginning of his family’s healing process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/books/review/remember-me-like-this-by-bret-anthony-johnston.html', 'snippet': 'In Bret Anthony Johnston’s debut novel, the rescue of a missing boy is only the beginning of his family’s healing process.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I am convinced,” Wayne C. Booth wrote in “The Rhetoric of Fiction” in 1961, “that most novelists today . . . feel an inseparable connection between art and morality, quite apart from what it is popular to say about morality.” He argued that a novelist’s control of narrative distance — his manipulation of the relationships among author, narrator, character and reader — has everything to do with the novel’s moral life. “And yet we go on talking as if technical triumphs had no relation to the value of what they achieve.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10SUBHENDERSON/10SUBHENDERSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10SUBHENDERSON/10SUBHENDERSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10SUBHENDERSON/10SUBHENDERSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10SUBHENDERSON/10SUBHENDERSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10SUBHENDERSON/10SUBHENDERSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy':