ews', '_id': 'nyt://article/d39e54dc-3c8f-5b04-b4d5-4bfd22d46ee3', 'word_count': 1217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d39e54dc-3c8f-5b04-b4d5-4bfd22d46ee3'}, {'abstract': 'From Mexico to Chicago, from past to present, Maria Venegas confronts her gun-toting father’s violent legacy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/books/review/bulletproof-vest-by-maria-venegas.html', 'snippet': 'From Mexico to Chicago, from past to present, Maria Venegas confronts her gun-toting father’s violent legacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jose Venegas, the father at the center of Maria Venegas’s vividly evocative memoir, “Bulletproof Vest,” is an outlaw in the grand tradition. He is, in other words, a moving target — sometimes mythic, sometimes depressingly real. We romanticize outlaws, which is our romantic word for “criminals.” I suppose it has something to do with their vitality, an extraordinary life force manifested by brilliant clothes, flamboyant sexuality, bullets and dead bodies. Even victims can be susceptible to a form of awe. And an outlaw’s children, of course, can be prone to a vexed and powerful love.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10LAZARSUB/0810-bks-LAZAR-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10LAZARSUB/0810-bks-LAZAR-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 657}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10LAZARSUB/0810-bks-LAZAR-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10LAZARSUB/0810-bks-LAZAR-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1336, 'width': 1221, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10LAZARSUB/10LAZARSUB-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10LAZARSUB/10LAZARSUB-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/books/review/10LAZARSUB/10LAZARSUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Distance Between', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Distance Between', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lazar, Zachary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Venegas, Maria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bulletproof Vest: The Ballad of an Outlaw and His Daughter (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T15:31:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Lazar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lazar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/204189d7-f59f-5860-86af-fc1af6e84c4b', 'word_count': 1170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/204189d7-f59f-5860-86af-fc1af6e84c4b'}, {'abstract': 'The experiment to discourage overconsumption would have a greater chance of success if it were bolder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/09/opinion/sunday/britains-drinking-problem.html', 'snippet': 'The experiment to discourage overconsumption would have a greater chance of success if it were bolder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The British relationship with strong drink, which might be even more tangled than the American one, has taken a new twist: London is to experiment with the “sobriety tags” for which much success has been claimed in the United States, where the bracelets are known by a more snappy acronym, SCRAM, or Secure Continuous Remote Alcohol Monitoring. Those committing minor crimes in which alcohol has played a part will be barred from drinking for up to 120 days in a pilot program employing the alcohol-detecting anklets, which are made by a Colorado company and have been modeled by celebrities like Lindsay Lohan, Tracy Morgan and Jayson Williams.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/opinion/sunday/09nevin-main/09nevin-main-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 358, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/09/opinion/sunday/09nevin-main/09nevin-main-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 358}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/opinion/sunday/09nevin-main/09nevin-main-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/opinion/sunday/09nevin-main/09nevin-main-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1222, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/opinion/s