e': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7300028-608f-5bc9-855a-5a02a24263af', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7300028-608f-5bc9-855a-5a02a24263af'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: France reclaims Alsace, August 8, 1914.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/08/1914-france-reclaims-alsace/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: France reclaims Alsace, August 8, 1914.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Official despatches made public by the French Ministry of War at 11.30 last night [Aug. 8] show that the French troops are advancing rapidly in Upper Alsace, driving the Germans before them at the point of the bayonet. Mulhouse has been occupied, and French cavalry is pursuing the fleeing Germans further to the north. The occupation of Mulhouse followed upon that of Altkirch, reported earlier in the day. The entrance of the French troops into Alsace is a historic event, which will arouse the enthusiasm of the whole French nation. The advance guard of the French army reached Altkirch at nightfall on Friday. The town was defended by very strong field works and was occupied by a German brigade. The French advance guard also consisted of a brigade. The French troops dashed to the assault with magnificent ardor. An infantry regiment, in a furious bayonet charge, carried the German trenches after a hot engagement in front of the lines. Once again the bayonet charges put the Germans to flight.\xa0— The New York Herald, European Edition, August 9, 1914', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/blogs/09iht-retrospective-Alsace-map/09iht-retrospective-Alsace-map-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'image