d_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aca0f14c-49b5-586f-93c7-c023c085392f'}, {'abstract': 'In June, a Marine Corps veteran who was a judge advocate during the Vietnam War wrote to President Obama, expressing concern that militants’ advances in northern Iraq might prompt the United States to send troops back there. The response he received seemed prescient.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/08/a-letter-to-the-president-and-a-well-timed-response/', 'snippet': 'In June, a Marine Corps veteran who was a judge advocate during the Vietnam War wrote to President Obama, expressing concern that militants’ advances in northern Iraq might prompt the United States to send troops back there. The response he receiv...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In June, a Marine Corps veteran named David Nelson wrote a letter to President Obama expressing concern that the rapid advances of Islamist militants in northern Iraq might prompt the United States to send troops back to bolster the shaky government in Baghdad. In his letter, Mr. Nelson, who was a judge advocate during the Vietnam War, wondered whether the Obama administration might use the successes of the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria, or ISIS, much the way President Lyndon B. Johnson used confrontations between North Vietnamese torpedo boats and an American destroyer in August 1964 to justify expanded military action in Vietnam. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Letter to the President, and a Well-Timed Response', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T21:39:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cd484f0-3223-503c-83ec-742edcac1e07', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cd484f0-3223-503c-83ec-742edcac1e07'}, {'abstract': 'John Doswell and Jean Preece, who became “life partners” in the early 1970s, married on a decommissioned fireboat on the Hudson River.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/a-voyage-that-is-finally-complete.html', 'snippet': 'John Doswell and Jean Preece, who became “life partners” in the early 1970s, married on a decommissioned fireboat on the Hudson River.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a girl in St. Petersburg, Fla., in the 1940s and ’50s, Jean Preece lived for her dance lessons and the 50-cent double feature at the local movie hall, where she sat transfixed as Gene Kelly and Debbie Reynolds cavorted in “Singin’ in the Rain.” She was so devoted to the film that years later, when she was working with a New York-based Spanish dance troupe visiting Madrid, she saw that the movie was playing in a local theater, in Spanish, and went. It did not matter that she spoke almost no Spanish; it was about the songs, the dancing. She knew what she wanted in a movie.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/10VOWS1/10VOWS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/10VOWS1/10VOWS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/10VOWS1/10VOWS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/10VOWS1/10VOWS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1397, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/10VOWS1/10VOWS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/10VOWS1/10VOWS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/10/fashion/weddings/10VOWS1/10VOWS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Voyage That Is Finally Complete', 'kicker': 'Vows', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Voyage That Is Finally Complete', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vows (Times Column)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Julien, Jane Gordon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T21:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Gordon Julien', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': 'Gordon', 'lastname': 'Julien', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a0224fa-df1e-5e0d-b961-d8010f65dd27', 'word_count': 1404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a0224fa-df1e-5e0d-b961-d8010f65dd27'}, {'abstract': 'This week, the Book Review cover features a review of the latest novel from Haruki Murakami, who has a cult following that includes the review’s author, Patti Smith.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/08/08/the-murakami-effect/', 'snippet': 'This week, the Book Review cover features a review of the latest novel from Haruki Murakami, who has a cult following that includes the review’s author, Patti Smith.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week our cover features Patti Smith’s review of the new Haruki Murakami novel, “Colorless Tsukuru Tazaki and His Years of Pilgrimage.” Murakami is, of course, an internationally best-selling author with a huge following here and in Japan, where this most recent novel sold one million copies in its first week.Murakami always merits attention in the Book Review. His last book, “1Q84,” was reviewed on our cover by Kathryn Schulz, now the book critic at New York magazine. Even before we had our review assigned, we were pretty sure this new novel, which was unusually — for a major literary novel — released in the summer rather than the fall, would merit a cover review. (Even in a year when Murakami doesn’t have a new book, he can wind up in the Book Review, as he did in this delightful Sketchbook Grant Snider did for us two years ago, “Haruki Murakami Bingo.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/08/blogs/bookreview/bookreview-articleLarge.png', 'height': 713, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/08/blogs/bookreview/bookreview-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 713}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/08/blogs/bookreview/bookreview-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 862, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/08/blogs/bookreview/bookreview-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1536, 'width': 1292, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/08/blogs/bookreview/bookreview-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/08/blogs/bookreview/bookreview-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/08/blogs/bookreview/bookreview-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Murakami Effect', 'kicker': 'Times Insider', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murakami, Haruki', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T21:43:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3d2605e-2af0-5d9a-8c90-c2c7dfa0a344', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3d2605e-2af0-5d9a-8c90-c2c7dfa0a344'}, {'abstract': 'Stars dotted an evening at the Vail International Dance Festival, where the ticket prices matched the year: $20.14 and $14.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/09/arts/dance/vail-international-festival-with-tiler-peck-and-balletx.html', 'snippet': 'Stars dotted an evening at the Vail International Dance Festival, where the ticket prices matched the year: $20.14 and $14.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VAIL, Colo. — Every summer, the Vail International Dance Festival presents one evening when the prices match the year — sort of. So in 2013, all seats in the Gerald R. Ford Amphitheater were $20.13, with all places on the lawn going for $13; this year they’re $20.14 and $14. (Children 12 or under sit free on the lawn.) Obviously, this system won’t last beyond 2020, but while it does, it has extraordinary value.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09CORNEJO2/09CORNEJO2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09CORNEJO2/09CORNEJO2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 464}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09CORNEJO2/09CORNEJO2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09CORNEJO2/09CORNEJO2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1585, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09CORNEJO2/09CORNEJO2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09CORNEJO2/09CORNEJO2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09CORNEJO2/09CORNEJO2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Big Stars and Little Stars Shine, for a Bargain', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Big Stars and Little Stars Shine, for a Bargain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vail International Dance Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BalletX', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peck, Tiler', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vail (Colo)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T21:43:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2c3c5c1-1be7-5c8e-aee4-636db6935b4e', 'word_count': 1022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2c3c5c1-1be7-5c8e-aee4-636db6935b4e'}, {'abstract': 'A wedding invitation initiates a terrifying journey to New York with two parents in their 80s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/fashion/chaperoning-elderly-parents-in-manhattan.html', 'snippet': 'A wedding invitation initiates a terrifying journey to New York with two parents in their 80s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I knew what I was up against the moment I opened the “Save the Date” email. It was from a childhood friend, begging me to bring my parents to her summer wedding in New York City. “It would mean so much to me to see your folks sitting in the church,” she wrote. “It would be as if my own parents were there,” she added of her now deceased mother and father.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/fashion/06REVERSEPARENTING/0706-REVERSEPARENTING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 539, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/fashion/06REVERSEPARENTING/0706-REVERSEPARENTING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 539, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/fashion/06REVERSEPARENTING/0706-REVERSEPARENTING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/07/06/fashion/06REVERSEPARENTING/0706-REVERSEPARENTING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/06/fashion/06REVERSEPARENTING/0706-REVERSEPARENTING-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Trip to the Big City', 'kicker': 'Reverse Parenting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Trip to the Big City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T21:48:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Conlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Conlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/695227f1-c913-5343-b38f-0836ee230659', 'word_count': 1101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/695227f1-c913-5343-b38f-0836ee230659'}, {'abstract': 'Voters rejected a malodorous effort to oust three capable sitting state Supreme Court justices.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/08/tennessee-ignores-the-koch-brothers/', 'snippet': 'Voters rejected a malodorous effort to oust three capable sitting state Supreme Court justices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Participants in Tennessee’s judicial elections on Thursday rejected a malodorous effort to oust three capable sitting state Supreme Court justices: Chief Justice Gary Wade and Justices Cornelia Clark and Sharon Lee.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tennessee Ignores the Koch Brothers', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Cornelia A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Sharon Gail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wade, Gary R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-08T21:51:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dorothy J. Samuels', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dorothy', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Samuels', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08d96bf6-4744-5e7b-b27e-7b599174f506', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08d96bf6-4744-5e7b-b27e-7b599174f506'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Morris’s ballet version of Ovid’s love story of the shepherd Acis and the sea nymph Galatea arrives in New York with some important details adjusted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/09/arts/dance/mark-morris-dance-performs-acis-and-galatea.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Morris’s ballet version of Ovid’s love story of the shepherd Acis and the sea nymph Galatea arrives in New York with some important details adjusted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his two-act staging of “Acis and Galatea” at the David H. Koch Theater, Mark Morris plunges his audience into the heart of the pastoral genre. He sets the love story of the shepherd Acis and the sea nymph Galatea within a landscape chorally filled with lovers. Though, on the one hand, these other people are anonymous, it’s as if they were Daphnis and Chloe, Phyllis and Corydon, and all the amorous couples created by Theocritus, Virgil, Longus and other classical authors. On the other, it’s as if they were nature itself. And so Acis and Galatea — whose story is found in the “Metamorphoses” of Ovid — become part of a long tradition of the classical idyll in which love is interrupted by death. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09MORRIS/09MORRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09MORRIS/09MORRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/09/arts/09MORRIS/09MORRIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ