alifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9586971-fe39-5484-959d-c0453100bf78', 'word_count': 930, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9586971-fe39-5484-959d-c0453100bf78'}, {'abstract': 'After losing the Republican primary in June, the former House majority leader is set to become Eric Cantor Inc. and make lots of money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/opinion/sunday/eric-cantors-big-payoff.html', 'snippet': 'After losing the Republican primary in June, the former House majority leader is set to become Eric Cantor Inc. and make lots of money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Residents of Virginia’s Seventh Congressional District won’t be able to meet their representative at a town hall during the August recess because they won’t have one as of Aug. 18. Their congressman, Eric Cantor, announced his resignation a few days ago after losing the Republican primary in June to someone further to the right. Ostensibly, he did so to give his successor slightly higher seniority (which might mean nothing more than a better office), but the real reasons are more venal.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eric Cantor’s Big Payoff', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eric Cantor’s Big Payoff', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-09T20:07:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1065d57f-02cb-5a10-a93b-ebd06b871ae4', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1065d57f-02cb-5a10-a93b-ebd06b871ae4'}, {'abstract': 'In a tournament of upsets, the big news is the World Chess Federation’s presidential election between Kirsan Ilyumzhinov and Garry Kasparov.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/10/us/olympiad-is-shadowed-by-leadership-showdown.html', 'snippet': 'In a tournament of upsets, the big news is the World Chess Federation’s presidential election between Kirsan Ilyumzhinov and Garry Kasparov.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is election season at the World Chess Federation, with a vote at the Chess Olympiad on Monday that pits the incumbent president against a former world champion.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Olympiad Is Shadowed by Leadership Showdown', 'kicker': 'Chess', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Olympiad Is Shadowed by Leadership Showdown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Chess Federation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasparov, Garry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ilyumzhinov, Kirsan N', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-09T20:11:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Chess', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Loeb McClain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'mid