nice Bejo, above, with Mr. Mosaffa), the divorce she seeks — and in the process opens a Pandora’s box of secrets and lies — in this drama written and directed by Asghar Farhadi. Tahar Rahim plays the man Marie has taken up with, whose wife is in a coma. Elyes Aguis is their son, whose presence in Marie’s house means a playmate for her younger daughter (Jeanne Jestin). And Pauline Burlet is Marie’s older daughter, who finds the situation nearly intolerable. “The narrative complications can be distracting, at times exasperating, but they’re finally irrelevant because Mr. Farhadi’s filmmaking is so fluid, and the performers, Ms. Bejo, in particular, are so attractive,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times. “As in ‘A Separation,’ Mr. Farhadi shows a masterly gift for moving his characters and camera through rooms that — with a raised voice, a violent exit — become stages in a ferocious domestic drama. But here, keep your eye on the littlest players, the small ones trembling in the wings.” In French and Persian, with English subtitles.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-11T05:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/eebf8f4b-45a0-592e-9b36-11dbbc31a6ac', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eebf8f4b-45a0-592e-9b36-11dbbc31a6ac'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in pring on August 11, 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/11/pageoneplus/corrections-august-11-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in pring on August 11, 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INTERNATIONAL', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: August 11, 2014', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-11T06:06:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2835ccc5-35e7-58cb-8b65-13a2ef9d270c', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2835ccc5-35e7-58cb-8b65-13a2ef9d270c'}, {'abstract': 'Human Rights Watch is releasing a report on “widespread and systematic” killings of protesters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/12/world/middleeast/egypt-bars-entry-for-2-staff-members-of-human-rights-watch.html', 'snippet': 'Human Rights Watch is releasing a report on “widespread and systematic” killings of protesters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — A day after Egypt barred representatives of Human Rights Watch from entering the country, the group disclosed the source of the government’s alarm: a report implicating senior officials, including Egypt’s current president, Abdel Fattah el-Sisi, in what it called the “widespread and systematic” killings of protesters.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Systematic’ Killings in Egypt Are Tied to Leader, Group Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '&#8216;Systematic&#8217; Killings in Egypt Are Tied to Leader, Group Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fahim, Kareem', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subjec