lastname': 'Qin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a4c4964-f911-5752-97b2-2b58eccd5a29', 'word_count': 621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a4c4964-f911-5752-97b2-2b58eccd5a29'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on a staggering Ebola outbreak, ugly judicial elections, inequality in college sports and “shell” democracy in Turkey.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/11/morning-views-55/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on a staggering Ebola outbreak, ugly judicial elections, inequality in college sports and “shell” democracy in Turkey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Margaret Chan, the director general of the World Health Organization, has declared the Ebola outbreak\xa0in West Africa “the largest, most severe, most complex” in “the nearly four-decade history of the disease,” according to an editorial in The New York Times. “Apart from inflicting a staggering human toll, the spread of the Ebola virus in West Africa has laid bare how unprepared the United States and other advanced countries are to protect and treat thousands of Africans whose lives are threatened by an extremely dangerous virus for which there is no cure.” Because drug companies have little financial interest in developing a vaccine, “traditional public health measures, like finding and isolating patients who become sick, tracing their contacts and using stringent infection control procedures in hospitals, remain the best bet for containing the epidemic.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-11T10:36:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5341d9e3-9f5e-5891-8d05-ef07d1a317db', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5341d9e3-9f5e-5891-8d05-ef07d1a317db'}, {'abstract': 'Vietnam invited foreign reporters to embed with its Coast Guard and see what challenges it faced from China’s presence in the South China Sea.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/11/vietnam-gives-brief-show-of-openness-in-standoff-with-china/', 'snippet': 'Vietnam invited foreign reporters to embed with its Coast Guard and see what challenges it faced from China’s presence in the South China Sea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The reporters’ trip with the Vietnamese Coast Guard started with a meal, and the meal started with toasts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/11/world/11SINO-VIETNAM02/11SINO-VIETNAM02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/11/world/11SINO-VIETNAM02/11SINO-VIETNAM02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/11/world/11SINO-VIETNAM02/11SINO-VIETNAM02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/11/world/11SINO-VIETNAM02/11SINO-VIETNAM02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/11/world/11SINO-VIETNAM02/11SINO-VIETNAM02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 's