oncerns of Iran’s supreme leader about the country’s declining fertility rate, Iranian lawmakers passed legislation that prohibits all surgical operations to permanently prevent pregnancy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/12/world/middleeast/iran-permanent-contraception-through-surgery-is-outlawed.html', 'snippet': 'Moving to allay the concerns of Iran’s supreme leader about the country’s declining fertility rate, Iranian lawmakers passed legislation that prohibits all surgical operations to permanently prevent pregnancy.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Moving to allay the concerns of Iran’s supreme leader, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, about the country’s declining fertility rate, Iranian lawmakers on Monday passed legislation that prohibits all surgical operations to permanently prevent pregnancy. Passage of the legislation was part of a plan to “prevent a population decline,” the official Islamic Republic News Agency reported. Under the measure, any violation would be considered a crime, punishable by fines. Exceptions were made for surgery urgently required for a patient’s health. Ayatollah Khamenei expressed alarm last winter about the conspicuous decline in births in Iran, saying he was “shaking with fear” about demographic trends that could reduce population growth to zero. The number of children per couple has fallen to 1.3 from a peak of 3.6 in the years after the 1979 Islamic revolution. Many young couples have said they are fearful of bringing children into the world, given Iran’s weak economy and isolation from Western sanctions. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Permanent Contraception Through Surgery Is Outlawed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Permanent Contraception Through Surgery Is Outlawed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khamenei, Ali', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Sanctions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-12T02:01:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/088953a4-9ce9-5b87-8d07-45ef9a0e0204', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/088953a4-9ce9-5b87-8d07-45ef9a0e0204'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations Human Rights Council has appointed three legal experts to an international commission to look into whether violations of international humanitarian and human rights law were committed during the conflict in Gaza.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/12/world/middleeast/human-rights-council-plans-to-investigate-gaza-conflict.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations Human Rights Council has appointed three legal experts to an international commission to look into whether violations of international humanitarian and human rights law were committed during the conflict in Gaza.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United Nations Human Rights Council on Monday appointed three legal experts to an international commission to look into whether violations of international humanitarian and human rights law were committed by Israel and the militant Palestinian group Hamas during the conflict in Gaza. The council, based in Geneva, set up the inquiry last month, and Navi Pillay, the United Nations’ top human rights official, said war crimes might have been committed by both sides in the conflict. A Canadian law professor, William Schabas, will lead the commission and will be joined by Doudou Diène, a lawyer from Senegal who has worked as a United Nations expert on racism. Amal Alamuddin, a British-Lebanese international law specialist, was announced as the third member, but later in the day she said she would be unable to serve on the commission, The Associated Press reported. Israel condemned the resolution setting up the inquiry as “biased, misconceived and destructive.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Human Rights Council Plans to Investigate Gaza Conflict', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Human Rights Council Plans to Investigate Gaza Conflict', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cumming-Bruce, Nick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Human Rights Council', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-12T02:04:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Cumming-Bruce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cumming-Bruce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a89a5db-433d-5f03-bafb-1d8f00466491', 'word_count': 