. — LAST week’s Republican primary in Tennessee resulted in a comfortable win for Senator Lamar Alexander over his Tea Party-backed challenger, State Assembly Representative Joe Carr. But make no mistake: The Tea Party is on a roll across the South, having mounted major primary challenges in Texas, Mississippi and South Carolina, and knocked out Representative Eric Cantor of Virginia. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDguldig/0813OPEDguldig-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 742, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDguldig/0813OPEDguldig-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 742}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDguldig/0813OPEDguldig-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 828, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDguldig/0813OPEDguldig-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1739, 'width': 1406, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDguldig/0813OPEDguldig-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDguldig/0813OPEDguldig-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/opinion/0813OPEDguldig/0813OPEDguldig-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The South’s Lesson for the Tea Party', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The South’s Lesson for the Tea Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilkie, Curtis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Midterm Elections (2014)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation and Desegregation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-12T23:27:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Curtis Wilkie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Curtis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilkie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d18e4c3a-eefd-5a78-9899-ca3134ca6b80', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d18e4c3a-eefd-5a78-9899-ca3134ca6b80'}, {'abstract': 'Will those with the most hope get the worst treatment?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/opinion/the-schizophrenia-penalty-.html', 'snippet': 'Will those with the most hope get the worst treatment?', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHILADELPHIA — A FEW months ago, a patient came to our hospital, seeking help. One of us, Edward, was on the team that treated him. He was pleasant, if slightly withdrawn, and cogent. He was a college graduate in his 20s and had recently been fired from his job as a high school math teacher, because of unexpected absences. He had come to believe that government agents were conspiring against him, and he had taken to living out of a truck and sleeping in different parking lots. By the time he came to us, he was exhausted. A diagnosis became clear: he had schizophrenia. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perpetuating Schizophrenia’s Stigma', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perpetuating Schizophrenia’s Stigma', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Larkin, Edward', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Schizophrenia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurford, Irene', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-12T23:28:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Larkin and Irene Hurford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Larkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Irene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hurford', 'qualifier'