, arrested more than three months ago while visiting North Korea, publicly offered a “heartfelt apology” to the North Korean authorities and pleaded for his release.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The family of Jeffrey E. Fowle, an Ohio municipal worker arrested more than three months ago while visiting North Korea as a tourist, publicly offered its “heartfelt apology” on Tuesday to the North Korean authorities for whatever offense he may have committed and pleaded for his release on humanitarian grounds. In a statement issued by the family’s lawyer, Mr. Fowle’s wife and three young children also said they had received only one letter and one telephone call from him. In an Aug. 1 Associated Press interview with Mr. Fowle that had been approved by the North Korean government from an undisclosed place of confinement, he expressed contrition and said he expected to be tried soon. Neither he nor North Korean officials specified the charges against him, but he is believed to have left a Bible in his Pyongyang hotel, which could have been construed as a hostile act. Mr. Fowle is one of three Americans known to be in the custody of North Korea, which has no diplomatic relations with the United States and regards the American government as its chief enemy. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea: Captive’s Kin Apologize', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korea: Captive’s Kin Apologize', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fowle, Jeffrey Edward', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T03:25:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Saker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/31010077-d712-523d-84e4-386211cd3064', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31010077-d712-523d-84e4-386211cd3064'}, {'abstract': 'An Islamist charity plans to send a new aid flotilla to the Gaza Strip this year to challenge Israel’s naval blockade there, the group announced Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/world/europe/turkey-aid-flotilla-is-planned-for-gaza.html', 'snippet': 'An Islamist charity plans to send a new aid flotilla to the Gaza Strip this year to challenge Israel’s naval blockade there, the group announced Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' An Islamist charity plans to send a new aid flotilla to the Gaza Strip this year to challenge Israel’s naval blockade there, the\xa0group announced Tuesday. The organization — the Turkish Foundation for Human Rights and Freedoms and Humanitarian Relief, known as I.H.H. — said it would also organize demonstrations at sea and in ports worldwide over the next months, and campaign for the opening of a port in Gaza for international marine traffic. A similar effort in 2010 ended with an Israeli raid that killed nine people and strained ties between Turkey and Israel. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey: Aid Flotilla Is Planned for Gaza', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey: Aid Flotilla Is Planned for Gaza', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arsu, Sebnem', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Turkish Foundation for Human Rights and Freedoms and Humanitarian Relief (IHH)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T03:28:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/10e11d7f-44a9-571b-a502-df3862709199', 'word_count': 94, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10e11d7f-44a9-571b-a502-df3862709199'}, {'abstract': 'On a visit to Indian-administered Kashmir on Tuesday, Prime Minister Narendra Modi made his toughest statement yet on Pakistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/world/asia/india-modi-criticizes-pakistan.html', 'snippet': 'On a visit to Indian-administered Kashmir on Tuesday, Prime Minister Narendra Modi made his toughest statement yet on Pakistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a visit to Indian-administered Kashmir on Tuesday, Prime Minister Narendra Modi made his toughest statement yet on Pakistan, saying it “has lost strength to fight conventional war, but continues to engage in a proxy war through terrorism.” The two countries have fought three wars over the disputed territory of Kashmir, as well as a more limited conflict, known as the Kargil war, in 1999. On Tuesday, Mr. Modi visited the town of Kargil, near the Line of Control, to inaugurate a power plant. Top civilian officials have rarely visited Kargil since the conflict there, in which India lost more than 500 soldiers. Estimates of Pakistan’s losses vary, but are believed to be higher. After being elected in May, Mr. Modi took a relatively warm tone toward Pakistan, inviting its prime minister to attend his swearing-in and leaving the impression that the two nuclear-armed neighbors were prepared for a period of calm. Since then, however, cease-fire violations on the Line of Control have become more frequent. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'India: Modi Criticizes Pakistan', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'India: Modi Criticizes Pakistan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kumar, Hari', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Modi, Narendra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kashmir and Jammu (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T03:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/acd87923-df90-5296-af1f-19ef15338904', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acd87923-df90-5296-af1f-19ef15338904'}, {'abstract': 'Quotation of the Day for Wednesday, August 13, 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/pageoneplus/quotation-of-the-day-for-wednesday-august-13-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the Day for Wednesday, August 13, 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': '“This was not an anti-American statement.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day for Wednesday, August 13, 2014', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T03:34:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfcfbe00-4add-5d7b-b3f8-41169385c441', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfcfbe00-4add-5d7b-b3f8-41169385c441'}, {'abstract': 'Rent subsidies are part of Mayor Bill de Blasio’s plan to reduce the record number of homeless people in city shelters.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/nyregion/2-programs-aim-to-move-city-families-from-shelters.html', 'snippet': 'Rent subsidies are part of Mayor Bill de Blasio’s plan to reduce the record number of homeless people in city shelters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'State and city officials have agreed to create two rent subsidy programs to help families with children leave New York City shelters and move into their own apartments.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2 Programs Aim to Move New York Families From Shelters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '2 Programs Aim to Move City Families From Shelters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Navarro, Mireya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and