mages/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: As Social Media Studies Proliferate, Experts Weigh Ethics Guidelines', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T10:31:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3259f331-0a36-54e1-b204-36d143c474d4', 'word_count': 185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3259f331-0a36-54e1-b204-36d143c474d4'}, {'abstract': 'Opinions on our toxic racial legacy, Egypt’s dismal human-rights record, the right to vote in British prisons, verifying humanitarian aid in Ukraine and improved Iraqi governance.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/morning-views-57/', 'snippet': 'Opinions on our toxic racial legacy, Egypt’s dismal human-rights record, the right to vote in British prisons, verifying humanitarian aid in Ukraine and improved Iraqi governance.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The F.B.I. may be able to answer the many questions surrounding the death of Michael Brown, an 18-year-old black student from Ferguson, Mo., who was a few days from heading off to college when he was shot by a police officer on Saturday,” begins an editorial in The New York Times. “But it doesn’t take a federal investigation to understand the history of racial segregation, economic inequality and overbearing law enforcement that produced so much of the tension now evident on the streets.” St. Louis, the editorial says, “has long been one of the nation’s most segregated metropolitan areas,” with whites dominating the City Council and police department. For decades whites used zoning laws to keep blacks out of desirable areas; among black residents “within the city, poverty and unrest grew.” The black population has also been subjected to intervention from the largely white police force “far out of proportion to their population,” according to the editorial. “The circumstances of Mr. Brown’s death are, inevitably, in dispute,” says the editorial, but “what is not in dispute is the sense of permanent grievance held by many residents and shared in segregated urban areas around the country.” Mr. Brown’s death serves as “a reminder of a toxic racial legacy that still infects cities and suburbs across America.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Views', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'valu