zation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3602e466-bc9b-5722-8e76-d9d4eed9de7b', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3602e466-bc9b-5722-8e76-d9d4eed9de7b'}, {'abstract': 'Two new picture books help children comprehend the very small and the very big.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/books/tiny-creatures-and-if.html', 'snippet': 'Two new picture books help children comprehend the very small and the very big.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you want to find the wildly creative people now, I’m told, look in fields that have to do with science, math and technology. They say these days, that’s where an imagination can really fly. I’ve been skeptical. Growing up as a creative type, I experienced a plummeting sensation just catching a whiff of formaldehyde outside the science lab, or hearing the word “cosine,” which to me had a sinister ring. I couldn’t see science and math as anything other than earthbound and rote, and yet all too often, confusingly abstract.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/books/13childrens-1/13childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/13/books/13childrens-1/13childrens-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/books/13childrens-1/13childrens-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 595, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/books/13childrens-1/13childrens-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1189, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbna