', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chiquita Brands International Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fyffes PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Safra Group', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T15:46:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e4df910-049e-5eaa-80a6-abffa818af13', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e4df910-049e-5eaa-80a6-abffa818af13'}, {'abstract': 'The organizers of the Avant Music Festival will offer a series of monthly concerts, starting in September.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/13/a-monthly-series-of-new-music-from-avant-media/', 'snippet': 'The organizers of the Avant Music Festival will offer a series of monthly concerts, starting in September.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Avant Media, the organization founded by the composer Randy Gibson and the filmmaker Ana Baer, and best known for its multimedia Avant Music Festival, announced on Wednesday that it would expand its schedule to include a series of monthly concerts, in addition to the festival. With one exception, the performances are at Wild Project, 195 East 3rd Street.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Monthly Series of New Music From Avant Media', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Avant Media Performance Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T15:52:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6f9b51b-4f5a-5c49-ba18-71a4ee8b48f0', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6f9b51b-4f5a-5c49-ba18-71a4ee8b48f0'}, {'abstract': 'How did a Times reporter, Brian X. Chen, get information from inside a famously secretive corporate culture?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2014/08/13/getting-the-scoop-on-apple-u/', 'snippet': 'How did a Times reporter, Brian X. Chen, get information from inside a famously secretive corporate culture?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday’s front page, Brian X. Chen wrote about Apple University, the internal training program that, Mr. Chen reports, inculcates workers into Apple’s company values. How did Mr. Chen get information from inside a famously secretive corporate culture?Apple is a tricky company to cover. Its employees are told to keep everything they do at work confidential from outsiders, including friends, family members and especially journalists. So with the story on Apple University, Apple’s internal training program, I thought about how to carefully approach employees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/blogs/insider-bull/insider-bull-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 110, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/13/blogs/insider-bull/insider-bull-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 110}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non