lin-art-book-publisher/', 'snippet': 'The new offshoot, Little Gestalten, features titles that both adults and youngsters will enjoy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kids will ask for the same book, night after night, for months, even years. So parents know how critical it is that an oft-requested tale be pleasing to both adults and little ones. The Berlin-based art publisher Gestalten has just launched a new children’s book imprint, Little Gestalten, that marries thoughtful storytelling with supercool visuals. “Elsa and the Night,” by the Swedish author, illustrator and filmmaker Jöns Mellgren, tells the tale of a little girl who makes friends with both the night and an elephant named Olaf. In “The Zoo’s Grand Opening,” the German author and designer Judith Drews zips through the colorful letters of the alphabet, pairing each with an animal and witty remark (“Our skunk might look all cute and fluffy/But you should run like mad if he gets huffy”). Small hands will enjoy turning the oversize pages of the Japanese fairy tale “Issun Bôshi,” which has been reimagined by Mayumi Otero and Raphaël Urwiller, the duo behind the French design house Icinori. Even the classic “Little Red Riding Hood” seems new again in Little Gestalten’s version, with cutout images that can be held up to the light to cast shadows on the wall until it’s time to say, “My, what big, tired eyes you have.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/t-magazine/13gestalten-molvar/13gestalten-molvar-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/13/t-magazine/13gestalten-molvar/13gestalten-molvar-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/13/t-magazine/13gestalten-molvar/13gestalten-molvar-ju