084fc6f27fe2', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c20c7a58-a011-5ab1-abd3-084fc6f27fe2'}, {'abstract': '“The Kitchen Bible” offers aspirational examples as well as advice for do-it-yourselfers tackling kitchen makeovers themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/14/garden/recipes-for-kitchen-redesigns.html', 'snippet': '“The Kitchen Bible” offers aspirational examples as well as advice for do-it-yourselfers tackling kitchen makeovers themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kitchens tend to be busy, messy places thanks to the role they’ve assumed as the hub of the home. And apartment kitchens are often cramped and lacking in natural light and views. These are not the sorts of kitchens you will find in “The Kitchen Bible: Designing the Perfect Culinary Space” (Images Publishing; $45), by Barbara Ballinger, Margaret Crane and Jennifer Gilmer, a kitchen designer of three decades. The aspirational spaces pictured here, many designed by Ms. Gilmer, are mostly amply sized, outfitted with the latest appliances and carefully propped for their close-ups. But the book also clearly lays out the steps involved in a kitchen redo. Although geared more toward those who will be working with a kitchen designer than those tackling makeovers themselves, the book offers advice on things like hanging stove hoods that is useful for do-it-yourselfers, too.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/garden/14CURRBOOKS/0814CURRENTS9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/14/garden/14CURRBOOKS/0814CURRENTS9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 592}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/garden/14CURRBOOKS/0814CURRENTS9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 101