483f3db05b06'}, {'abstract': 'Michelle A. Rhee, the divisive former schools chancellor in Washington a is stepping down as chief executive of StudentsFirst, the advocacy group she founded four years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/14/us/ex-head-of-washington-schools-steps-down-at-advocacy-group-.html', 'snippet': 'Michelle A. Rhee, the divisive former schools chancellor in Washington a is stepping down as chief executive of StudentsFirst, the advocacy group she founded four years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michelle A. Rhee, the divisive former schools chancellor in Washington and one of the most public faces of a campaign to change public education, is stepping down as chief executive of StudentsFirst, the advocacy group she founded four years ago. In a blog post on the group’s website on Wednesday, Ms. Rhee said that it was “time for my next step in life” and that she would focus on her family and support her husband, Mayor Kevin Johnson of Sacramento, as “he continues to move forward with his career.” She also has two children with her ex-husband, Kevin Huffman, the commissioner of education in Tennessee. Ms. Rhee, who raised millions of dollars from donors including the Walton Family Foundation and the Broad Foundation, pursued an aggressive agenda involving changes in state laws regarding teacher tenure, performance evaluations and testing. In her statement, she said: “While I remain 100 percent committed to the success of StudentsFirst, it’s time for a shift in the day-to-day management of the team and our advocacy work.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-Head of Washington Schools Steps Down at Advocacy Group', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Education', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ex-Head of Washington Schools Steps Down at Advocacy Group', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rhee, Michelle A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'StudentsFirst', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walton Family Charitable Support Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-13T23:59:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4d7f7bf-4d26-5535-ae76-d1f648a195f4', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4d7f7bf-4d26-5535-ae76-d1f648a195f4'}, {'abstract': 'For Lucky magazine, a spinoff finds it part of a new company, the Lucky Group, with its own multibrand shopping website, although Condé Nast will still be printing it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/14/fashion/Lucky-Magazine-Gets-an-E-Partner-to-Shop-With.html', 'snippet': 'For Lucky magazine, a spinoff finds it part of a new company, the Lucky Group, with its own multibrand shopping website, although Condé Nast will still be printing it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN LUCKY MAGAZINE arrived on newsstands almost 14 years ago, it was the most exciting thing to happen to Condé Nast since Rice Krispies Treats in the Frank Gehry-designed cafeteria at 4 Times Square. And for readers, Lucky was not unlike a Rice Krispies Treat: hard to resist; easy to accuse of lacking nutritional value; “sticky” with its decals that one could affix, with the glee of a preschooler, to pictures of items one planned to buy. Hitherto American women’s publications had cloaked their mercenary objectives in the soft batting of short fiction and horoscopes and etiquette advice; this one declared nakedly on the cover that it was the “magazine about shopping.”', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/fashion/14zROW1/14zROW1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/14/fashion/14zROW1/14zROW1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 899}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/fashion/14zROW1/14zROW1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 684, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/fashion/14zROW1/14zROW1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1368, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/fashion/14zROW1/14zROW1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/14/fashion/14zROW1/14zROW1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/fashion/14zROW1/14zROW1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lucky Magazine Gets an E-Partner to Shop With', 'kicker': 'Front Row', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lucky Magazine Gets an E-Partner to Shop With', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'majo