', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5252371d-0582-55ff-84e2-cc913a03a9ad', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5252371d-0582-55ff-84e2-cc913a03a9ad'}, {'abstract': 'As climate change alters habitats, once-disparate animals are shacking up, creating hybrids that challenge our notion of what it means to be a species.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/magazine/should-you-fear-the-pizzly-bear.html', 'snippet': 'As climate change alters habitats, once-disparate animals are shacking up, creating hybrids that challenge our notion of what it means to be a species.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In New England today, trees cover more land than they have at any time since the colonial era. Roughly 80 percent of the region is now forested, compared with just 30 percent in the late 19th century. Moose and turkey again roam the backwoods. Beavers, long ago driven from the area by trappers seeking pelts, once more dam streams. White-tailed deer are so numerous that they are often considered pests. And an unlikely predator has crept back into the woods, too: what some have called the coywolf. It is both old and new — roughly one-quarter wolf and two-thirds coyote, with the rest being dog.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Hybrid_illo-horizontal/17Hybrid_illo-horizontal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Hybrid_illo-horizontal/17Hybrid_illo-horizontal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 448}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'ca