graph': 'As the United States representative to last summer’s Venice Biennale, Sarah Sze orchestrated a masterly series of installations in the American pavilion. Insidiously undermining the pavilion’s grand Palladian architecture and its intimations of empire, her sprawling project “Triple Point” confused indoors with out: It opened up side entrances and hidden spaces, spread like kudzu along the building’s roof and crept into a storage closet in the rotunda. And like her previous works, it distorted the visitor’s sense of scale with dizzying agglomerations of tiny objects.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15SZE/15SZE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15SZE/15SZE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 648}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15SZE/15SZE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 948, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15SZE/15SZE-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1896, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15SZE/15SZE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15SZE/15SZE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15SZE/15SZE-thumbLarge