None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e7e8000-7f71-5230-b4d2-d932934faf61', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e7e8000-7f71-5230-b4d2-d932934faf61'}, {'abstract': 'Jules Feiffer’s graphic novel is a tribute to film noir and detective fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/jules-feiffers-kill-my-mother.html', 'snippet': 'Jules Feiffer’s graphic novel is a tribute to film noir and detective fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oedipus gets all the buzz, but surely a daughter’s desire to vanquish her mother is even more elemental. Not because daughters want to sleep with their daddies (although they sometimes do, as long as they’re de facto daddies; see “Mildred Pierce”), but because it’s so darn hard to become yourself with Mother hovering. You want — no, need — to dance in the Trevi Fountain and there’s Mommy, holding your galoshes. Veda Pierce, come to think of it, had a similar complaint about Mildred: “I literally can’t open my mouth in a theater, or a radio studio, or anywhere, that she isn’t there, bustling down the aisle, embarrassing me before people.” Mother is omnipresent until she’s not, and then you hate her even more for daring to leave you.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17lippman-sub/17lippman-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17lippman-sub/17lippman-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 502}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17lippman-sub/17lippman-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 586, 'width': 700, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17lippman-sub/17lippman-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 586, 'width': 700, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17lippman-sub/17lippman-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17lippman-sub/17lippman-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17lippman-sub/17lippman-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Them Dames', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Them Dames', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Kill My Mother: A Graphic Novel (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feiffer, Jules', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-14T21:02:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Lippman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lippman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c39f49bc-8b86-58a7-82ca-7c6fbb3142c7', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c39f49bc-8b86-58a7-82ca-7c6fbb3142c7'}, {'abstract': 'In 1879, backed by a wealthy American newspaper magnate, 33 men embarked on an ill-fated expedition to the North Pole.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/in-the-kingdom-of-ice-by-hampton-sides.html', 'snippet': 'In 1879, backed by a wealthy American newspaper magnate, 33 men embarked on an ill-fated expedition to the North Pole.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ice. You remember ice. The stuff that forestalled so many polar explorers back before global warming and dreams of beachfront homes along the shores of Baffin Bay. But, as Hampton Sides reminds us in this first-rate polar history and adventure narrative, the notion of mildish weather in the Arctic did not begin with climate change. Just 150 years ago many believed that the Gulf Stream and its counterpart in the Pacific, the Kuroshio, reached all the way to the North Pole. Once a ship broke through a rim of ice circling the top of the globe, the thinking went, it would encounter an open polar sea and easy sailing to the pole. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIS/17HARRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIS/17HARRIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIS/17HARRIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 740, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIS/17HARRIS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 740, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIS/17HARRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIS/17HARRIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIS/17HARRIS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Abandon Ship!', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Abandon Ship!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'In the Kingdom of Ice: The Grand and Terrible Polar Voyage of the USS Jeannette (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sides, Hampton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-14T21:02:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert R. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c87240c-7a6c-5f74-9125-87e5d77b52a2', 'word_count': 1359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c87240c-7a6c-5f74-9125-87e5d77b52a2'}, {'abstract': 'Lucinda Franks’s memoir of her marriage is a tribute to her husband.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/timeless-by-lucinda-franks.html', 'snippet': 'Lucinda Franks’s memoir of her marriage is a tribute to her husband.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The secret to a long and passionate union is to marry someone almost 30 years your senior. That seems to be the message of Lucinda Franks’s engaging memoir, “Timeless: Love, Morgenthau, and Me.” Of course it helps if that someone is Robert M. Morgenthau, scion of a historic family, on the cusp of an extraordinarily long-running career as Manhattan’s district attorney. But the real subject of this brave, highly personal account of the Morgenthaus’ shared life is the mysterious alchemy that forges an unlikely couple out of two dissimilar souls. This is also a love story in a political context: the great events in New York’s life in the last 40 years, observed by a sharp reporter from a privileged perch.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17MARTON/17MARTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 748, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17MARTON/17MARTON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 748}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17MARTON/17MARTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 822, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17MARTON/17MARTON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1643, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17MARTON/17MARTON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17MARTON/17MAR