d Monte Reel’s article in the Aug. 10 magazine, you know that Zero Freitas, a Brazilian bus magnate and record collector, has a pretty big organizational problem on his hands. In Freitas’s warehouse in São Paulo are several million records that need to be indexed and stored in some way. It’s a project that will take decades, and that’s only if Freitas stops buying records, which seems unlikely.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/magazine/Freitas1/Freitas1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/14/magazine/Freitas1/Freitas1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/magazine/Freitas1/Freitas1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/magazine/Freitas1/Freitas1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/magazine/Freitas1/Freitas1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/14/magazine/Freitas1/Freitas1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/magazine/Freitas1/Freitas1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Organize Thousands of Vinyl Records Using Seemingly Random Knick-Knacks', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-14T21:22:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By JEANNIE CHOI', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CHOI', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ebb5e5b-ec0d-577f-afce-4ac6d7f046ad', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ebb5e5b-ec0d-577f-afce-4ac6d7f046ad'}, {'abstract': 'In “Mr. X: A Vision of Leos Carax,” Tessa Louise-Salomé turns a documentary eye on a French filmmaker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/15/movies/mr-x-a-vision-of-leos-carax-burnishes-a-cinematic-myth.html', 'snippet': 'In “Mr. X: A Vision of Leos Carax,” Tessa Louise-Salomé turns a documentary eye on a French filmmaker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The mock-cliché title of “Boy Meets Girl,” Leos Carax’s debut feature, which he made in the 1980s when he was in his early 20s, belies its fresh, shooting-star romanticism and visual originality. These qualities streak throughout the work of Mr. Carax, who, as amply illustrated by “Mr. X: A Vision of Leos Carax,” gives the sense of discovering the art and romance of cinema anew with each film.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Enigma of a Cult-Favorite Film Auteur', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. X', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Louise-Salome, Tessa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carax, Leos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documenta