 '“Dinosaur 13” looks at the famous find of a T. rex skeleton in South Dakota and the legal case that followed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Life sure wasn’t easy for a dinosaur named Sue. As revealed by the skeleton, excavated in 1990 by the paleontologist Peter Larson and his team from the Black Hills Institute for Geological Research in South Dakota, Sue’s journey through the late Cretaceous period had been fraught by an unusual number of broken bones and other injuries. Almost 70 million years later, these magnificent remains — constituting the most complete Tyrannosaurus rex ever found — would embark on another journey, this time through a labyrinthine legal battle that would leave their caretakers equally scarred.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15DINOSAUR2/15DINOSAUR2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 319, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15DINOSAUR2/15DINOSAUR2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 319}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15DINOSAUR2/15DINOSAUR2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 545, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15DINOSAUR2/15DINOSAUR2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1089, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15DINOSAUR2/15DINOSAUR2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/15/arts/15DINOSAUR2/15DINOSAUR2-thumbStandard.