es to becoming tools for racial profiling?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Aug. 5, Mayor Bill de Blasio and NYC’s Economic Development Corporation President Kyle Kimball announced twenty finalists for the city’s annual Big Apps competition. Among them was SketchFactor, an app that offers users a map of their\xa0surroundings pinned with googly-eyed faces to indicate where a “sketchy” incident has been reported. A user can report an unpleasant occurrence or use the app’s directions feature to chart a non-“sketchy” route through a\xa0city.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/opinion/nyto-sketchy/nyto-sketchy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/14/opinion/nyto-sketchy/nyto-sketchy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/opinion/nyto-sketchy/nyto-sketchy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1004, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/opinion/nyto-sketchy/nyto-sketchy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2007, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/opinion/nyto-sketchy/nyto-sketchy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/14/opinion/nyto-sketchy/nyto-sketchy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/14/opinion/nyto-sketchy/nyto-sketc