he New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/business/16stewart-pic/16stewart-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 423, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/16/business/16stewart-pic/16stewart-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 423}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/business/16stewart-pic/16stewart-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/business/16stewart-pic/16stewart-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1442, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/business/16stewart-pic/16stewart-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/16/business/16stewart-pic/16stewart-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/business/16stewart-pic/16stewart-pic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fears of Renewed Instability as Fed Ends Stimulus', 'kicker': 'Common Sense', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fears of Renewed Instability as Fed Ends Stimulus', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stein, Jeremy C', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yellen, Janet L', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T13:38:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By James B. Stewart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c92cda2-7fc6-59d3-a42a-70f422733590', 'word_count': 1280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c92cda2-7fc6-59d3-a42a-70f422733590'}, {'abstract': 'Why we’re feeling (economically) depressed.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/15/secular-stagnation-the-book/', 'snippet': 'Why we’re feeling (economically) depressed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ebook, actually: the invaluable VoxEU has a new ebook with contributions from a lot of good people — and also one from me — which is must-reading for anyone trying to keep up with current debates about our economic prospects.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Secular Stagnation: The Book', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T13:52:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/432714d7-890c-528b-b4a0-6855093aef50', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/432714d7-890c-528b-b4a0-6855093aef50'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Art experts condemned a robber who retouched a painting from the Louvre in 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/15/1939-anger-over-art-restoration/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: Art experts condemned a robber who retouched a painting from the Louvre in 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'French art experts were enraged yesterday when they learned that an unknown painter, Serge Claude Bogousslavsky, had had the nerve to clean and restore Watteau’s famous painting ‘‘L’Indifferent’’ during the two months he held it after having stolen it from the Louvre. It was bad enough to steal the picture, they argued, but to return it fixed up according to his own ideas was almost a sacrilege. Yet not one of them was prepared to argue that Serge’s criticism of the work before he altered it was wrong. They only doubted that he was the man for the job. Meanwhile, the picture lies in a safe in the Louvre and in the days to come it will be studied by a group of specialists. It may be, as some believe, that Serge has harmed it to some extent, or, as others believe, that he improved it. That will take time to determine, but the Louvre publicly admits the painting is still worth its $100,000 assessment value.\xa0— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, August 16, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Anger Over Art Restoration', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louvre Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T13:54:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8680632b-efa5-5167-b071-f861472b9e24', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8680632b-efa5-5167-b071-f861472b9e24'}, {'abstract': 'After an album (“Vida”) and a tour celebrating his recovery from cancer, the Long Island-born and Puerto Rico-raised Robi Draco Rosa is facing new challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/arts/music/after-a-career-comeback-draco-rosa-goes-home.html', 'snippet': 'After an album (“Vida”) and a tour celebrating his recovery from cancer, the Long Island-born and Puerto Rico-raised Robi Draco Rosa is facing new challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Last November’s Latin Grammy Awards ceremony seemed to be the culmination of a heartwarming comeback for the Puerto Rican singer-songwriter-guitarist-producer Robi Draco Rosa.  He received the album of the year honor for “Vida”; sang the hit single “Más y Más” with his boyhood pal and former Menudo bandmate Ricky Martin; and, as far as the audience knew, was finally free of the cancer that had been diagnosed in 2011.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17DRACO/17DRACO-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 423, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17DRACO/17DRACO-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 423}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17DRACO/17DRACO-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17DRACO/17DRACO-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1442, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17DRACO/17DRACO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17DRACO/17DRACO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17DRACO/17DRACO-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life After ‘Vida’: One Man’s Time to Heal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life After ‘Vida’: One Man’s Time to Heal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosa, Robi Draco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T13:58:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a13ccbab-63b1-5d64-9fa7-d496861e6b05', 'word_count': 1687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a13ccbab-63b1-5d64-9fa7-d496861e6b05'}, {'abstract': 'The comics of the writer and artist Frank Miller have long been dark and violent, but they reached their apex in his “Sin City.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/sin-city-and-other-comics-in-frank-millers-dark-oeuvre.html', 'snippet': 'The comics of the writer and artist Frank Miller have long been dark and violent, but they reached their apex in his “Sin City.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'He saved Marvel’s Daredevil from irrelevance, reinvented DC’s Batman as the darkest of knights and even imported samurai legend into mainstream American comics. But the comic book writer and artist Frank Miller found his truest, his deepest creative home in the stark noir of “Sin City.” In Mr. Miller’s underworld, as the troubled drifter Marv observes in the first “Sin City” graphic novel, “The Hard Goodbye” (1992), it’s always “a lousy room in a lousy part of a lousy town.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17miller-1/17miller-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17miller-1/17miller-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 280}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17miller-1/17miller-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 901, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17miller-1/17miller-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 901, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17miller-1/17miller-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/books/17miller-1/17miller-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drawing Noirish Worlds in Blood and Ink', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drawing Noirish Worlds in Blood and Ink', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Frank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Jennings', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jennings', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0363ab4f-7b72-5e0e-96fa-aa67891b91be', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0363ab4f-7b72-5e0e-96fa-aa67891b91be'}, {'abstract': 'Frank Miller, an illustrator and author of “Sin City,” considers himself a cartoonist at heart, but he has also embraced Hollywood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/movies/with-a-new-sin-city-film-frank-miller-extends-his-craft.html', 'snippet': 'Frank Miller, an illustrator and author of “Sin City,” considers himself a cartoonist at heart, but he has also embraced Hollywood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world of Frank Miller’s “Sin City” is black and white in almost every sense. In these popular graphic novels and their film adaptations, Mr. Miller, the author, illustrator and filmmaker, presents a fictional metropolis as monochromatic in hue as in values: mean streets populated with trench-coated men and scantily clad women, ready to exchange bullets or bodily fluids when it is to their advantage.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17FRANK-MILLER1/17FRANK-MILLER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17FRANK-MILLER1/17FRANK-MILLER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17FRANK-MILLER1/17FRANK-MILLER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17FRANK-MILLER1/17FRANK-MILLER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1209, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17FRANK-MILLER1/17FRANK-MILLER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17FRANK-MILLER1/17FRANK-MILLER1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/arts/17FRANK-MILLER1/17FRANK-MILLER1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Purveyor of a Stylish Brutality', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Purveyor of a Stylish Brutality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Frank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbd63e3c-987f-55e3-9e86-f4cb222a75a2', 'word_count': 1470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbd63e3c-987f-55e3-9e86-f4cb222a75a2'}, {'abstract': 'The writer and director discusses his zombie romantic comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/15/jeff-baena-narrates-a-scene-from-life-after-beth/', 'snippet': 'The writer and director discusses his zombie romantic comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the comedy “Life After Beth,” the writer and director Jeff Baena sought to make a different kind of zombie movie. Rather than focusing on physical carnage, his film is more about the emotional carnage that is produced when a girl who dies mysteriously returns. In this video, he narrates a sequence featuring Dane DeHaan, Aubrey Plaza, John C. Reilly and Molly Shannon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeff Baena Narrates a Scene From ‘Life After Beth’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edab40c8-bcd2-5d4f-8c54-9377e2c31cb1', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edab40c8-bcd2-5d4f-8c54-9377e2c31cb1'}, {'abstract': 'The most frequent guest on a Sunday morning political talk show will now be there even more often.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/16/upshot/if-chuck-todd-seems-comfortable-on-on-meet-the-press-he-should.html', 'snippet': 'The most frequent guest on a Sunday morning political talk show will now be there even more often.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most frequent guest on “Meet the Press,” the venerable Sunday morning political talk show on NBC, isn’t John McCain or John Kerry. It’s the show’s new host, Chuck Todd.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15up-todd3/15up-todd3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 903, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15up-todd3/15up-todd3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 903}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15up-todd3/15up-todd3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15up-todd3/15up-todd3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1361, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15up-todd3/15up-todd3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15up-todd3/15up-todd3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15up-todd3/15up-todd3-thumbLarge.jpg',