it': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17talk1/mag-17Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1367, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17talk1/17talk1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17talk1/17talk1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17talk1/17talk1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ryan Adams: ‘Weed Helps a Lot’', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Weed Helps a Lot’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADAMS, RYAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alcohol Abuse', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:20:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Dave', 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/d60595e7-c6b4-5740-ac02-29779b1955f5', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d60595e7-c6b4-5740-ac02-29779b1955f5'}, {'abstract': 'I was 13 when a new friend joined me in the cornfield and in my home — and opened a door I hadn’t known was closed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/magazine/the-boy-of-summer.html', 'snippet': 'I was 13 when a new friend joined me in the cornfield and in my home — and opened a door I hadn’t known was closed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was the summer I detasseled corn. That dreary, dead-simple job consisted solely of walking down mile-long rows of corn each morning and evening for a period of several weeks, pulling the tassels off every plant that was supposed to produce hybrid seeds. The idea was that the ears would then be fertilized by pollen from a different type of corn in the adjoining row, thus creating a hybrid. The year I was 13 my parents said that yes, I could have a horse if I earned the money to buy it, so I took on three acres, for $30 each.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17lives1/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17lives1/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 777}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17lives1/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 695, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17lives1/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 695, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17lives1/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17lives1/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17lives1/mag-17Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Boy of Summer', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Boy of Summer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willoughby, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:20:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Willoughby', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Willoughby', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76836979-83a3-56c1-b734-bca478345813', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76836979-83a3-56c1-b734-bca478345813'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/magazine/reply-all-the-8-314-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE KIDS WHO BEAT AUTISM', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/magazine/03cover_type/03cover_type-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/03/magazine/03cover_type/03cover_type-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/magazine/03cover_type/03cover_type-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/magazine/03cover_type/03cover_type-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1684, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/magazine/03cover_type/03cover_type-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/03/magazine/03cover_type/03cover_type-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/03/magazine/03cover_type/03cover_type-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reply All: The 8.3.14 Issue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reply All: The 8.3.14 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:20:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d82ed2a0-840d-5231-bdde-bb7862433b60', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d82ed2a0-840d-5231-bdde-bb7862433b60'}, {'abstract': 'Weighing the risk of trauma against the right to know.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/magazine/should-unwitting-victims-be-informed-of-the-crime.html', 'snippet': 'Weighing the risk of trauma against the right to know.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was recently announced that Johns Hopkins Hospital will pay a $190 million settlement to more than 8,000 women who were potentially photographed and/or videotaped during gynecological exams by Dr. Nikita Levy. While it is obvious that a horrendous crime was committed against these women, I wonder what ethical obligation the investigators had to make their findings public in a case in which the victims were unaware a crime had occurred. The crime is such that knowledge of it will cause a great deal of emotional trauma. It would be hard (and probably unconstitutional) to prosecute the perpetrator while keeping the details of his crimes private. In general, should the victims be informed despite the trauma it will cause? What is the ethical obligation to the public at large? ARLO LYLE, MINNEAPOLIS', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Ethicist1/17Ethicist1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Ethicist1/17Ethicist1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Ethicist1/17Ethicist1-jumbo.png', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Ethicist1/17Ethicist1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Ethicist1/17Ethicist1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Ethicist1/17Ethicist1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17Ethicist1/17Ethicist1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Unwitting Victims Be Informed of the Crime?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "What You Don't Know", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levy, Nikita A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:20:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/64d7b235-f0f7-5d8c-b299-59a391c845e7', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64d7b235-f0f7-5d8c-b299-59a391c845e7'}, {'abstract': ' What could possibly explain the crazy popularity of strangers’ opening things on camera?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/magazine/a-mothers-journey-through-the-unnerving-universe-of-unboxing-videos.html', 'snippet': ' What could possibly explain the crazy popularity of strangers’ opening things on camera?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few months ago, I exchanged emails with a woman who told me her name was Melissa Lima. She said she was 21, a native of Brazil who now lived in Westchester County, N.Y., and that she was childless, because, after all, she was only 21.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17riff1/17riff1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 89, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17riff1/17riff1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 89}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17riff1/17riff1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17riff1/17riff1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 303, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17riff1/mag-17Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17riff1/mag-17Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/magazine/17riff1/mag-17Riff-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mother’s Journey Through the Unnerving Universe of ‘Unboxing’ Videos', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Opening Ceremony', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silcoff, Mireille', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Containers and Packaging', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:20:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireille Silcoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireille', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silcoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80c42662-f98c-55d6-bfda-768f208437a1', 'word_count': 1759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80c42662-f98c-55d6-bfda-768f208437a1'}, {'abstract': 'With the help of a 30-pound “concave easel,” Trevor and Ryan Oakes are creating a handmade version of an Edward Steichen original.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/magazine/how-a-pair-of-twins-redrew-an-iconic-photograph-with-camera-like-precision.html', 'snippet': 'With the help of a 30-pound “concave easel,” Trevor and Ryan Oakes are creating a handmade version of an Edward Steichen original.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Edward Steichen took his iconic photograph of the Flatiron Building in New York in 1904, the structure itself was only two years old, one of the tallest in the city and the very epitome of modernity. So it seemed the perfect challenge for the identical-twin artists Ryan and Trevor Oakes earlier this summer, a sidelight to the retrospective of their work I curated at the Museum of Mathematics, just up the road at 11 East 26th Street, open daily through Sept. 14. The brothers, who have been engaged in a deep colloquy on the nature of bifocal vision since toddlerhood, have recently developed one of the most intriguing breakthroughs in the depiction of physical reality since the Renaissance: They have come up with a method for tracing camera-obscura-exact renderings of the world before them onto a concave grid with no other optical equipment (no lenses, no pinholes) except their own unaided eyes. Lawrence Weschler', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Pair of Twins Redrew an Iconic Photograph With Camera-Like Precision', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Double Vision', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weschler, Lawrence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oakes, Trevor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oakes, Ryan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flatiron Building (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Mathematics', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Look (Magazine)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steichen, Edward', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Photographs by David La Spina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Photographs', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'La Spina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19b926bf-7962-5e86-b97d-0f457f51af3d', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 