nytimes.com/2014/08/15/1964-vietnam-presidency-to-khanh/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: South Vietnam gets a new head of state in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAP ST. JACQUES, South Vietnam — In a major revision of South Vietnam’s governmental structure, the military revolutionary council today [Aug. 16] ousted Maj. Gen. Duong Van Minh as chief of state and gave Premier Nguyen Khanh sweeping new powers by electing him to the newly created post of president. The council, meeting at this seaside resort about 40 miles east of Saigon, also promulgated a new national charter similar to a provisional constitution. This action clears the way for the establishment of an appointed provisional national assembly within one month. There has been no assembly or post of president since President Ngo Dinh Diem was overthrown last November. The council blocked Gen. Khanh’s bid for a one-man rule during the previously declared state of emergency and insured that final authority would rest with the council as a whole. Gen. Khanh retains the position of council chairman and commander in chief of the armed forces.\xa0— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, August 17, 1964 ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/blogs/16iht-oldaug16-vietnam/16iht-oldaug16-vietnam-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 590, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/blogs/16iht-oldaug16-vietnam/16iht-oldaug16-vietnam-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 618, 'width': 590, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/blogs/16iht-oldaug16-vietnam/16iht-oldaug16-vietnam-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'heig