'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:40:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alena Graedon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graedon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ef40b27-c651-51cf-817d-04d0fd1349ef', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ef40b27-c651-51cf-817d-04d0fd1349ef'}, {'abstract': 'A tragedy tears away at a mixed-race family in 1970s Ohio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/everything-i-never-told-you-by-celeste-ng.html', 'snippet': 'A tragedy tears away at a mixed-race family in 1970s Ohio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Celeste Ng’s debut novel, “Everything I Never Told You,” is a literary thriller that begins with some stock elements: a missing girl, a lake, a local bad boy who was one of the last to see her and won’t say what he knows. The year is 1977, the setting, a quiet all-American town in Ohio, where everyone knows one another and nothing like this has ever happened before. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17CHEE/17CHEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17CHEE/17CHEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17CHEE/17CHEE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 920, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17CHEE/17CHEE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17CHEE/17CHEE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17CHEE/17CHEE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17CHEE/17CHEE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Leftovers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Leftovers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Everything I Never Told You (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ng, Celeste', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T14:40:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Chee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/73ab4deb-a94d-5399-87f7-c5fa94c9c693', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73ab4deb-a94d-5399-87f7-c5fa94c9c693'}, {'abstract': 'James Lee Burke’s sprawling novel connects an encounter with Bonnie and Clyde to the Battle of the Bulge and the oil boom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/wayfaring-stranger-by-james-lee-burke.html', 'snippet': 'James Lee Burke’s sprawling novel connects an encounter with Bonnie and Clyde to the Battle of the Bulge and the oil boom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here in the Lone Star State, we have a subgenre of the Great American Novel we like to call the Novel as Big as Texas. A representative N.A.B.A.T. features lots of pages crowded with multiple generations of characters fighting Comanches, driving cattle, bringing in oil wells, eating Mexican food, settling ancestral grudges and brooding about the pitiless immensity of the Land. The category arguably began with Edna Ferber’s “Giant” and has proven elastic enough to encompass not just earnest cycloramic texts like James Michener’s “Texas” but also literary benchmarks as varied as Larry McMurtry’s “Lonesome Dove,” Cormac McCarthy’s Border Trilogy and Philipp Meyer’s recent epic, “The Son.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIHAN/0817-bks-Harrihan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIHAN/0817-bks-Harrihan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 604}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIHAN/0817-bks-Harrihan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1018, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17HARRIHAN/0817-bks-Harrihan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2036, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'cred