ruggles to cope after its take-charge daughter flees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mary Lawson’s story of a dysfunctional family in a northern Ontario logging town is told in scenes that are as palpably tender and surprising as they are quietly disturbing. “Road Ends,” her formidable third novel, uses the frozen landscape as a metaphor for the numb, spiritless lives her people shuffle through uneventfully, stuck in their circumstances. But there is a deeper drama that propels the book forward, and that sets up a vivid, evocative tale of rural and domestic angst laced with calamity. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17mehta/17mehta-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17mehta/17mehta-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 688}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17mehta/17mehta-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 894, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17mehta/17mehta-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1376, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17mehta/17mehta-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17mehta/17mehta-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17mehta/17mehta-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Anybody Home?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anybody Home?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Road Ends (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lawson, Mary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T15:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Mehta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mehta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c79ebc0-93a3-5ef3-8e16-9826273f65c3', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c79ebc0-93a3-5ef3-8e16-9826273f65c3'}, {'abstract': 'In Rennie Airth’s “The Reckoning,” a former Scotland Yard detective comes out of retirement to assist in the investigation of killings committed with the same pistol.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/rennie-airths-the-reckoning-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Rennie Airth’s “The Reckoning,” a former Scotland Yard detective comes out of retirement to assist in the investigation of killings committed with the same pistol.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rennie Airth opens his new police procedural, THE RECKONING (Viking, $26.95), with a thoughtful character study of Oswald Gibson, an inoffensive worm of a man, a recent widower on the threshold of old age, who has only now dared to indulge his passion for fly-fishing. After setting this pathetic soul down on the banks of a quiet stream, contentedly humming a Gilbert and Sullivan tune, Airth kills him off.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/0817-bks-CRIME/0817-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 816, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/0817-bks-CRIME/0817-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 816}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/0817-bks-CRIME/0817-bks-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 754, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/0817-bks-CRIME/0817-bks-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1507, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/0817-bks-CRIME/0817-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/0817-bks-CRIME/0817-bks-CRIME-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/0817-bks-CRIME/0817-bks-CRIME-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Safe Haven', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Safe Haven', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AIRTH, RENNIE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maron, Margaret', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mankell, Henning', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garnier, Pascal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T15:01:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20a328c0-7ebf-5f03-8bb8-45ee87ba2e2f', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20a328c0-7ebf-5f03-8bb8-45ee87ba2e2f'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COUNTDOWN: Our Last, Best Hope for a Future on Earth? by Alan Weisman.  (Back Bay/Little, Brown, $18.) In “The World Without Us” (2007), Weisman contemplated what would happen to the planet should human beings suddenly vanish. In this rousing sequel — a tour of our overpopulated, overexploited world — he interviews scientists, religious leaders and public-health experts in more than 20 countries, asking: How many humans can the planet hold without capsizing?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-15T15:01:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d8c971b-9d82-5dc8-a00a-ba427fcacb43', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d8c971b-9d82-5dc8-a00a-ba427fcacb43'}, {'abstract': 'Lev Grossman’s “The Magician’s Land,” which debuts at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, owes a debt to “Brideshead Revisited.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Lev Grossman’s “The Magician’s Land,” which debuts at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, owes a debt to “Brideshead Revisited.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not Hogwarts: With the publication of “The Magician’s Land,” which debuts at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list this week, Lev Grossman’s fantasy trilogy comes to a close — for now. In a letter to his fans (written as a mock alumni letter sent by Brakebills, the wizarding college in the series), Grossman writes, “People have been asking me whether ‘The Magician’s Land’ is really the last Magicians book, and the answer to that is a resounding probably.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17grossman/17grossman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 458, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17grossman/17grossman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 458, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/books/review/17grossman/17grossman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'hei