in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-16T00:17:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ali Khedery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ali', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khedery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cc33669-63c8-500f-a038-6f083ec02d07', 'word_count': 2148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cc33669-63c8-500f-a038-6f083ec02d07'}, {'abstract': 'How the South learned to stop worrying and embrace timed explosives.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/15/the-confederate-torpedo/', 'snippet': 'How the South learned to stop worrying and embrace timed explosives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the great points of debate among Confederate leaders during the Civil War was the use of “torpedoes” – what today we call mines – against Northern military naval forces, merchants and civilians. President Jefferson Davis, who thought they were unethical and cowardly weapons, was opposed to using them in almost every context. But many of his advisers felt otherwise, and as the Confederate war effort grew more dire in 1864, their calls became louder.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Confederate Torpedo', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Torpedoes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-16T00:20:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Grady', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe0d526d-9ea1-5ea6-ab46-64a981e6820e', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe0d526d-9ea1-5ea6-ab46-64a981e6820e'}, {'abstract': 'Salmon, once nearly extinct on part of the Columbia River, are slowly recovering, to the delight of birds. As a result, those charged with nursing the salmon back to robust health have a new plan to protect them: shoot the birds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/16/us/taking-up-arms-where-birds-feast-on-buffet-of-salmon.html', 'snippet': 'Salmon, once nearly extinct on part of the Columbia River, are slowly recovering, to the delight of birds. As a result, those charged with nursing the salmon back to robust health have a new plan to protect them: shoot the birds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ASTORIA, Ore. — The salmon here in the Columbia River, nearly driven to extinction by hydroelectric dams a quarter century ago, have been increasing in number — a fact not lost on the birds that like to eat them. These now flock by the thousands each spring to the river’s mouth, where the birds have their young, and gorge at leisure.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Up Arms Where Birds Feast on Buffet of Salmon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking Up Arms Where Birds Feast on Buffet of Salmon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barringer, Felicity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salmon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Columbia River (Pacific Northwest)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Sand Island (Ore)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Astoria (Ore)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Audubon Society, National', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-16T00:21:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66e3a0cb-f83e-5a10-8713-9f57e79b938f', 'word_count': 1084, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66e3a0cb-f83e-5a10-8713-9f57e79b938f'}, {'abstract': 'The California racetrack will host the 2017 Breeders’ Cup, but its summer meet has been troubled by a series of calamities, including the deaths of five horses on the newly installed turf course.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/16/sports/scrutiny-circles-track-at-del-mar.html', 'snippet': 'The California racetrack will host the 2017 Breeders’ Cup, but its summer meet has been troubled by a series of calamities, including the deaths of five horses on the newly installed turf course.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DEL MAR, Calif. — The summer season has been a turbulent one at Del Mar. The track has enjoyed a renaissance in recent years, its summer postrace concert series drawing a younger, more party-hearty crowd — a much-desired demographic for an industry that has been plagued by drug scandals and flagging interest. In June, Del Mar was awarded the 2017 Breeders’ Cup, the first time it will host the prestigious event.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scrutiny Circles Track at Del Mar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scrutiny Circles Track at Del Mar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Witz, Billy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Del Mar Thoroughbred Club', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Abuse, Rights and Welfare', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Breeders' Cup (Horse Races)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Horse Racing Board', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Del Mar Racetrack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Del Mar (Calif)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-16T00:27:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Billy Witz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Billy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a89d1bef-ecfe-51bf-8be5-b5d06de2d079', 'word_count': 1802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a89d1bef-ecfe-51bf-8be5-b5d06de2d079'}, {'abstract': 'Tova Weinberg, one of the country’s top Jewish matchmakers, has introduced “about 250” couples who later married, and that does not include her work through a matchmaking website.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/16/us/a-jewish-matchmaker-whose-hand-led-hundreds-down-the-aisle.html', 'snippet': 'Tova Weinberg, one of the country’s top Jewish matchmakers, has introduced “about 250” couples who later married, and that does not include her work through a matchmaking website.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PITTSBURGH — When I walked into Tova Weinberg’s large house here in the Squirrel Hill neighborhood of Pittsburgh, she looked me up and down and asked, “Are you Jewish?” I told her I was. “Are you married?” Yes, I said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/nyregion/y-BELIEFS/y-BELIEFS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/16/nyregion/y-BELIEFS/y-BELIEFS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 834}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/nyregion/y-BELIEFS/y-BELIEFS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/nyregion/y-BELIEFS/y-BELIEFS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1991, 'width': 1432, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/16/nyregion/y-BELIEFS/y-BELIEFS-th