nt': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e845783-80c1-5405-8abf-ef7aee449d2a'}, {'abstract': 'A lot once thick with garbage and weeds has become the Dred Scott Bird Sanctuary, an oasis of green space in the neighborhood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/nyregion/in-the-bronx-an-unlikely-sanctuary-for-birds-and-people.html', 'snippet': 'A lot once thick with garbage and weeds has become the Dred Scott Bird Sanctuary, an oasis of green space in the neighborhood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Troy Lancaster lives in exile. In the Bronx. His relatives won’t visit him, he says, because like a lot of folks with roots in the South, they are not keen on concrete, noise and narrow streets. He felt the same way in the 1980s when he moved to a stretch of Grant Avenue that had been reduced to an open-air drug market along a weed-choked street.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/nyregion/17SIDESTREETweb/17SIDESTREETweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/nyregion/17SIDESTREETweb/17SIDESTREETweb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/nyregion/17SIDESTREETweb/17SIDESTREETweb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/nyregion/17SIDESTREETweb/17SIDESTREETweb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/nyregion/17SIDESTREETweb/17SIDESTREETweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/nyregion/17SIDESTREETweb/17SIDESTREETweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/nyregion/17SIDESTREETweb/17SIDESTREETweb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Bronx, an Unlikely Sanctuary for Birds, and People', 'kicker': 'Side Street', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Bronx, a Sanctuary for Birds, and the Neighbors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gonzalez, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lancaster, Troy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks and Recreation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Bronx (Bronx, NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T17:11:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/190306b2-6ddc-5277-bcbd-c2aa8a760f08', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/190306b2-6ddc-5277-bcbd-c2aa8a760f08'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: In 1939, the Tate Gallery in London refuses a portrait of the Duke of Windsor as the Prince of Wales.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/1939-tate-gallery-refuses-portrait-by-john-singer-sargent/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: In 1939, the Tate Gallery in London refuses a portrait of the Duke of Windsor as the Prince of Wales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Art circles in London sought to discover today [Aug. 16] whether political motives lay behind the Tate Gallery’s refusal to accept the late John Singer Sargent’s charcoal portrait of the Duke of Windsor as the Prince of Wales, a bequest to the museum by Sir Philip Sassoon, wealthy art patron and former First Commissioner of Works, who died in June. Although the Gallery trustees turned down the Windsor picture a week ago, they accepted Sassoon’s offer of another Sargent work, a portrait in oil of Sir Philip himself. Dr. John Rothenstein, director and keeper of the Tate Gallery, stoutly maintained today that the trustees’ decision was the result of consideration of the charcoal drawing ‘‘purely on its merits as a work of art.’’ He said it was not considered ‘‘a good example of the artist’s work.’’ ‘‘Any suggestions that there was a prejudice against the personality or that there was an extraneous reason is entirely untrue,’’ he said.\xa0— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, August 17, 1939', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15iht-old18/15iht-old18-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 469, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15iht-old18/15iht-old18-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 469, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15iht-old18/15iht-old18-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15iht-old18/15iht-old18-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/blogs/15iht-old18/15iht-old18-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '1939: Tate Gallery Refuses Portrait by John Singer Sargent', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royal Family', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sargent, John Singer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tate Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T17:17:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8e09c3c-8638-5c63-8d7f-484cd0b3a761', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8e09c3c-8638-5c63-8d7f-484cd0b3a761'}, {'abstract': 'The events in Ferguson, Mo., have caused many to question what is seen as the worrisome militarization of our local police departments.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/drawing-the-line-between-soldier-and-cop/', 'snippet': 'The events in Ferguson, Mo., have caused many to question what is seen as the worrisome militarization of our local police departments.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Ferguson, Mo., has become a virtual war zone,” write Elizabeth R. Beavers and Michael Shank in an Op-Ed for The New York Times. “In the wake of the shooting of an unarmed black teenager, Michael Brown, outsize armored vehicles have lined streets and tear gas has filled the air. Officers dressed in camouflage uniforms from Ferguson’s 53-person police force have pointed M-16s at the very citizens they are sworn to protect and serve.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/opinion/nyto-ferguson/nyto-ferguson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/17/opinion/nyto-ferguson/nyto-ferguson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/opinion/nyto-ferguson/nyto-ferguson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/opinion/nyto-ferguson/nyto-ferguson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 2020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/opinion/nyto-ferguson/nyto-ferguson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/17/opinion/nyto-ferguson/nyto-ferguson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/17/opinion/nyto-ferguson/nyto-ferguson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drawing the Line Between Soldier and Cop', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCaskill, Claire', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Rand', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ferguson (Mo)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T17:52:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Flanagin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4a0b29d-cf3f-57e3-88b4-453cfae003a3', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4a0b29d-cf3f-57e3-88b4-453cfae003a3'}, {'abstract': 'Starting next year, the academy will train 42 students, using a curriculum developed by Wynton Marsalis.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/jazz-at-lincoln-center-to-open-a-summer-academy/', 'snippet': 'Starting next year, the academy will train 42 students, using a curriculum developed by Wynton Marsalis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center is expected to announce on Monday that it will establish the Jazz at Lincoln Center Summer Jazz Academy, an intensive performance institute for high school musicians. The academy will be based at the Castleton Festival, the classical music and opera festival and summer training program that the conductor Lorin Maazel and his wife, Dietlinde Turban Maazel, founded in 2009 on their farm in Castleton, Va. (Mr. Maazel died in July; Mrs. Maazel has succeeded him as artistic director.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center to Open a Summer Academy', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center Starts Summer Academy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsalis, Wynton', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jazz at Lincoln Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T18:20:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5d49f3f-3c50-5527-a485-5b5ae181395f', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5d49f3f-3c50-5527-a485-5b5ae181395f'}, {'abstract': 'Eminem and Rihanna, the megastars and regular collaborators, performed at MetLife Stadium on Saturday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/arts/music/eminem-and-rihanna-fuse-dark-energy-on-tour.html', 'snippet': 'Eminem and Rihanna, the megastars and regular collaborators, performed at MetLife Stadium on Saturday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. — It’s difficult to think of Eminem — perhaps the most transformational pop music figure of the 2000s, and still a multiplatinum star — as a supplicant in need of a handout. But in 2014, he is merely a galaxy-size cult favorite, a 41-year-old white rapper far from the center of pop culture, leaning heavily on reputation and largely failing to innovate. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/MONSTER/MONSTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/MONSTER/MONSTER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/MONSTER/MONSTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 585, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/MONSTER/MONSTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1170, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/MONSTER/MONSTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/MONSTER/MONSTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/MONSTER/MONSTER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'United by Their Rough Edges', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'United by Their Rough Edges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eminem', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rihanna', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MetLife Stadium (NJ)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T18:44:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf8b23b5-4862-51ea-b1a9-bb65414a190c', 'word_count': 987, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf8b23b5-4862-51ea-b1a9-bb65414a190c'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/17/arts/television/whats-on-tv-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:05 P.M. (Showtime 2) THE CANYONS (2013) This low-budget, famously trashy film was supposed to herald the return of Lindsay Lohan (above, with James Deen) to respectability, or at least bankability. It didn’t. But it was interesting. Written by the novelist Brett Easton Ellis (“Less Than Zero,” “American Psycho”) and directed by Paul Schrader (who wrote “Taxi Driver” and “Raging Bull” and directed “American Gigolo”), the movie is a dark, half-incoherent story about sex, movies and meaninglessness in Los Angeles. “Every so often,” wrote Manohla Dargis in The New York Times, “Mr. Schrader manages to deliver a jolt, an image, a line reading and even a scene — like a narratively decisive down-and-dirty sexual four-way — that fleetingly lifts ‘The Canyons.’ But his struggle is as palpable as his budgetary limitations.”', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T18:47:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brandon K. Thorp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brandon', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Thorp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ec683c0-634c-5eba-8b4d-87321d966bf2', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ec683c0-634c-5eba-8b4d-87321d966bf2'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas, accused of using his veto power to pressure Austin’s top prosecutor to step down, said, “I would make exactly the same decision.” He also said President Obama was responsible for a national erosion of the “rule of law.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/us/in-wake-of-indictment-perry-defends-veto-and-criticizes-obama.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas, accused of using his veto power to pressure Austin’s top prosecutor to step down, said, “I would make exactly the same decision.” He also said President Obama was responsible for a national erosion of the “rule of law.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Rick Perry of Texas remained unrepentant on Sunday about a veto he made as governor that led to his indictment on charges of abuse of power, saying that “if I had to do it again, I would make exactly the same decision.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Wake of Indictment, Perry Defends Veto (and Criticizes Obama)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocat