'nyt://article/96f9789a-7035-5925-a117-e49f8024fdfd'}, {'abstract': '“The Phantom of the Opera,” the long-running Andrew Lloyd Webber tale of obsession and murder, remains a grand, lavishly decked-out galleon of a show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/theater/the-phantom-of-the-opera-retains-its-luster.html', 'snippet': '“The Phantom of the Opera,” the long-running Andrew Lloyd Webber tale of obsession and murder, remains a grand, lavishly decked-out galleon of a show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there a truism more rarely true than the notion that absence makes the heart grow fonder? ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/jpphantom/jpphantom-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/jpphantom/jpphantom-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/jpphantom/jpphantom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/jpphantom/jpphantom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/jpphantom/jpphantom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/jpphantom/jpphantom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/arts/jpphantom/jpphantom-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Music of 10,000 Nights', 'kicker': 'Checking Back', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Music of 10,000 Nights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Phantom of the Opera (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lloyd Webber, Andrew', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boggess, Sierra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, Norm (1963- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T22:46:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5cfa98a-4a47-51a2-b9c8-4a64fade3dae', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5cfa98a-4a47-51a2-b9c8-4a64fade3dae'}, {'abstract': 'A Max Liebermann painting was wrongfully taken by the Nazis from the collection of its Jewish owner, a team of international experts has determined.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/arts/design/experts-say-a-second-work-in-munich-was-looted.html', 'snippet': 'A Max Liebermann painting was wrongfully taken by the Nazis from the collection of its Jewish owner, a team of international experts has determined.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Two Riders on the Beach,” above, a Max Liebermann painting found in the private collection of Cornelius Gurlitt, who died in early May, was wrongfully taken by the Nazis from the collection of its Jewish owner, a team of international experts has determined. Using archival material and other sources, the experts ascertained that the Liebermann painting rightfully belonged to the descendants of the industrialist David Friedmann, they said in a statement on Sunday. The work is only the second of 458 disputed paintings, prints and drawings in the collection, known as the Munich Art Trove, that the panel of experts, convened by the German government, has determined was looted. The other, a Matisse, was found in June to have been stolen from the collector Paul Rosenberg. Yet the Matisse has not been returned to Mr. Rosenberg’s heirs, and the Liebermann is sure to face a similar fate, because of the legal limbo surrounding Mr. Gurlitt’s collection following his death. Although Mr. Gurlitt, the son of an art dealer who worked for the Nazis, left his collection to the Kunstmuseum Bern, it has not yet decided whether to accept the gift. An agreement signed between Mr. Gurlitt and German authorities binds the new owner to restitute any looted works, but that cannot happen until the gift is accepted. David Toren, a great-nephew of Mr. Friedmann’s who lives in New York, filed a lawsuit against the German government in hopes of speeding the return of his family’s art work. “This is a very important first step in returning the painting to its rightful owners and we hope that the German government will not further delay returning the painting,” Mr. Toren’s attorney, August J. Matteis Jr., said in a statement.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Experts Say a Second Work in Munich Was Looted', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Experts Say a Second Work in Munich Was Looted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arts and Antiquities Looting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eddy, Melissa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gurlitt, Cornelius', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Munich (Germany)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T22:55:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Eddy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eddy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e42a36b-7381-558d-9237-7185da368138', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e42a36b-7381-558d-9237-7185da368138'}, {'abstract': 'HBO assumed its familiar role as the early leader in the Emmys race after taking home 15 Creative Arts awards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/arts/television/emmys-go-to-fallon-orange-and-yes-hbo.html', 'snippet': 'HBO assumed its familiar role as the early leader in the Emmys race after taking home 15 Creative Arts awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HBO assumed its familiar role as the early leader in the Emmys race after taking home 15 Creative Arts awards this weekend, but a few new faces garnered notable wins as well. The Academy of Television Arts & Sciences bestowed the awards in a ceremony in Los Angeles on Saturday in categories like casting, editing, and animation. The ceremony precedes the major Emmys broadcast, which airs Aug. 25 on NBC. “Game of Thrones” and “True Detective” earned four awards apiece for HBO, but the most decorated show of the night was NBC’s “Saturday Night Live,” which won half of that network’s 10 awards. An episode hosted by Jimmy Fallon, below, was responsible for four of the trophies, including a win for Mr. Fallon as guest actor in a comedy series. Other guest acting awards went to Joe Morton in ABC’s “Scandal,” Allison Janney in Showtime’s “Masters of Sex” and Uzo Aduba, who took home one of the first three trophies for Netflix’s series “Orange Is the New Black” for her breakout role as the inmate Suzanne Warren, known as Crazy Eyes. Zach Galifianakis’s “Between Two Ferns” series on the comedy website Funny or Die won best short-format, live-action entertainment program for an episode in which Mr. Galifianakis interviewed President Obama.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Emmys Go to Fallon, ‘Orange’ and, Yes, HBO', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Emmys Go to Fallon, &#8216;Orange&#8217; and, Yes, HBO', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emmy Awards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T22:56:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/75400eb8-6075-5e49-8f52-57e19b6c8d1c', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75400eb8-6075-5e49-8f52-57e19b6c8d1c'}, {'abstract': 'Symphony Space, the miniature arts center on the Upper West Side of Manhattan, is expected to announce on Monday that it has appointed Andrew Byrne as its artistic director.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/arts/carnegie-hall-executive-to-run-symphony-space.html', 'snippet': 'Symphony Space, the miniature arts center on the Upper West Side of Manhattan, is expected to announce on Monday that it has appointed Andrew Byrne as its artistic director.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Symphony Space, the miniature arts center on the Upper West Side of Manhattan, is expected to announce on Monday that it has appointed Andrew Byrne as its artistic director. The space has been without a director since January, when the composer Laura Kaminsky left the post in a disagreement over programming. Mr. Byrne takes over on Sept. 15. Mr. Byrne, a 48-year-old Australian who came to New York in 1993 on a Fulbright Scholarship to complete his doctorate in music at Columbia University, has worked at Carnegie Hall for 10 years, eight of them as the director of festivals and special projects. “It’s a different model,” Mr. Byrne said on Friday, obliquely acknowledging that Symphony Space gives him a smaller palette than Carnegie Hall. “But we have performing arts, film and education programs within the house. What attracts me to Symphony Space is the opportunity not only to shape the full season, but also to shape the profile of the institution.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Carnegie Hall Executive to Run Symphony Space', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carnegie Hall Executive To Run Symphony Space', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kozinn, Allan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Symphony Space', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrne, Andrew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-17T22:58:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alla