n_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper, Mark Landler and Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae7a42d7-429f-549f-8dfe-cffe413ff037', 'word_count': 1303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae7a42d7-429f-549f-8dfe-cffe413ff037'}, {'abstract': 'A rival bid for Family Dollar Stores could set up a bidding war. | PricewaterhouseCoopers faces a regulatory penalty. | Silicon Valley’s biggest corporate acquirers are leaning on internal teams to handle deals. | Alibaba’s deal-making is under scrutiny.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/morning-agenda-battle-for-family-dollar/', 'snippet': 'A rival bid for Family Dollar Stores could set up a bidding war. | PricewaterhouseCoopers faces a regulatory penalty. | Silicon Valley’s biggest corporate acquirers are leaning on internal teams to handle deals. | Alibaba’s deal-making is under sc...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dollar General said on Monday that it was offering $8.9 billion to buy Family Dollar Stores in an effort to break up its Family Dollar’s planned merger with Dollar Tree, DealBook’s Michael J. de la Merced writes. Under the terms of the deal, Dollar General would pay $78.50 a share in cash, significantly higher than the $74.50 a share that Dollar Tree’s original cash-and-stock bid was worth when it was announced last month. Including debt, Dollar General’s offer is valued at $9.7 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Battle for Family Dollar', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-18T11:36:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45d51486-9f87-5ad3-ae7f-99b58be76ab7', 'word_count': 1765, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45d51486-9f87-5ad3-ae7f-99b58be76ab7'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Byrne succeeds Laura Kaminsky, who resigned from the post in January.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/symphony-space-names-andrew-byrne-as-artistic-director/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Byrne succeeds Laura Kaminsky, who resigned from the post in January.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Symphony Space, the Upper West Side arts center, is expected to announce on Monday that it has appointed Andrew Byrne as its artistic director. The space has been without a director since January, when the composer Laura Kaminsky left the post in a disagreement over programming. Mr. Byrne takes over on Sept. 15.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Symphony Space Names Andrew Byrne as Artistic Director', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrne, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Symphony Space', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-18T12:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8f3e4c2-eb1e-5b5e-aac5-c44dad04791d', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8f3e4c2-eb1e-5b5e-aac5-c44dad04791d'}, {'abstract': 'The Martha Washington hotel, built over a century ago to house unmarried women, will reopen next month in New York’s NoMad neighborhood.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/martha-washington-hotel-nyc-marta-restaurant-danny-meyer/', 'snippet': 'The Martha Washington hotel, built over a century ago to house unmarried women, will reopen next month in New York’s NoMad neighborhood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Martha Washington hotel, built in 1903 to house unmarried women, will reopen in September in New York’s NoMad neighborhood, with austere new interiors by Annabelle Selldorf. “Our design for the hotel was very much influenced by the spirit of large, open spaces at the turn of the century,” says the architect, who updated the Renaissance Revival landmark with a walnut palette, blue tile floors and tons of glass to offset the space’s gothic fluted columns. Danny Meyer will also be opening Marta, a restaurant serving rustic Italian food, yet another fitting nod to the Washington’s namesake, who was known for being an expert hostess. ', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '118', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/t-magazine/24womens-look-hotel-1/24womens-look-hotel-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/t-magazine/24womens-look-hotel-1/24womens-look-hotel-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/