/081814bucks-carl-sketch/081814bucks-carl-sketch-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Go Ahead, Spice Up Your Investing Life', 'kicker': 'Sketch Guy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Planners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-18T17:43:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': 'Asset Allocation', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Richards', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richards', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31eb1c8c-296c-56e4-b380-36e607cf1d38', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31eb1c8c-296c-56e4-b380-36e607cf1d38'}, {'abstract': 'The new star of the time-traveling BBC series explains how he got the part and what he thinks of jokes about the older Doctor.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/doctor-who-and-the-eyebrows-of-peter-capaldi/', 'snippet': 'The new star of the time-traveling BBC series explains how he got the part and what he thinks of jokes about the older Doctor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not as rare as, say, a total solar eclipse or the alignment of the planets, but the arrival of a new star on “Doctor Who” is still a significant galactic event, at least to fans of that long-running BBC science-fiction series. Last summer Matt Smith announced that he was leaving the lead role of the enigmatic time-and-space adventurer known as the Doctor, and the BBC said that he would be succeeded by Peter Capaldi, the Scottish actor best known to British audiences (and some Americans as well) as Malcolm Tucker, the explosively vulgar government official in the political satire “The Thick of It” and the film “In the Loop.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Doctor Who’ and the Eyebrows of Peter Capaldi', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Capaldi, Peter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iannucci, Armando', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Broadcasting Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-18T17:46:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/995bc322-784b-57e9-8854-6e76fdfb05b9', 'word_count': 1100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/995bc322-784b-57e9-8854-6e76fdfb05b9'}, {'abstract': 'One World Symphony’s 2014-15 season will include mashups of classical and contemporary music inspired by some popular television shows.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/tv-series-as-inspiration-for-symphony-season/', 'snippet': 'One World Symphony’s 2014-15 season will include mashups of classical and contemporary music inspired by some popular television shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One World Symphony’s 2014-15 season will feature mashups of classical and contemporary music inspired by three popular television shows: Fox’s “New Girl,” HBO’s “Game of Thrones” and NBC’s “Hannibal.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TV Series as Inspiration for Symphony Season', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TV Series Are Inspiration For Symphony Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'One World Symphony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-18T17:51:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b483811f-f47c-5cb0-8e87-2a7a4efe060f', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b483811f-f47c-5cb0-8e87-2a7a4efe060f'}, {'abstract': 'Mohit Pandey explains the reactive functional programming we are focusing on for our Android redesign.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/getting-groovy-with-reactive-android/', 'snippet': 'Mohit Pandey explains the reactive functional programming we are focusing on for our Android redesign.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Slow,” a word no one ever wants to see associated with their app. Delay and lag of as little as 100–200 milliseconds will be perceived by users as poor, slow performance. Keeping long running tasks on the main thread will result in that perception, the dreaded “Application Not Responding” (ANR) and a bad user experience. This leaves us developers one choice: concurrent execution of code. The recent growth in popularity of functional programming is well deserved. As things become more asynchronous, the functional model really fits well. Being able to sequence, chain and transform asynchronous operations can be considered a must. However, the mechanisms for asynchronous programming provided in Android leave much to be desired, especially when compared to the web development realm. Many of the ideas and techniques from “enterprise” Java frameworks have not yet migrated to the Android world. Practices that some would consider table stakes for web development on the JVM have just begun to trickle in on Android; dependency management and a modern build system, for example. We can even dream of one day receiving proper support for unit tests.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting Groovy With Reactive Android', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-18T18:16:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohit Pandey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pandey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/771c9f0e-206c-5286-840d-79b97bc2c892', 'word_count': 1434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/771c9f0e-206c-5286-840d-79b97bc2c892'}, {'abstract': 'Cutting driving in a move from a dirt road to a village.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/from-tree-planting-along-a-dirt-road-to-car-free-village-living/', 'snippet': 'Cutting driving in a move from a dirt road to a village.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0Some readers may have noticed a prolonged hiatus here, only half of which was because of a brief escape to Winter Harbor, Me., my mother in law’s birthplace and home. The other half was because of my family’s first move in 17 years — from a 1930s-era farm house on a dirt road to a brick home less than eight miles away, built in the 1860s in the village of Nelsonville, N.Y., by two Civil War veterans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/blogs/dotchimney/dotchimney-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'widt