, 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/188b1dd6-169c-57e9-bf01-6db76a2a6687', 'word_count': 10, 'uri': 'nyt://article/188b1dd6-169c-57e9-bf01-6db76a2a6687'}, {'abstract': 'As the Obama administration plans the character of its engagement in Iraq, Russia signals a possible “humanitarian” intervention in Ukraine. And it’s all part of a pattern.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/in-iraq-and-ukraine-will-history-repeat-itself/', 'snippet': 'As the Obama administration plans the character of its engagement in Iraq, Russia signals a possible “humanitarian” intervention in Ukraine. And it’s all part of a pattern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Worlds apart, two leaders are planning to intervene in worsening conflicts outside their borders, and citing humanitarian concerns as their rationale. In Iraq, President Barack Obama and his administration are considering how to contain the violent march of radical Islamist militants and provide help to those whom the Islamists threaten with extermination. In the conflict in eastern Ukraine, Russian President Vladimir Putin sent a convoy of 280 trucks with what the Russians describe as “humanitarian aid” for the embattled region.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/opinion/nyto-redcross/nyto-redcross-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/18/opinion/nyto-redcross/nyto-redcross-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/opinion/nyto-redcross/nyto-redcross-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1013, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'im