’s national guard, it’s worth engaging with a contrary view, so here is the HotAir blogger Jazz Shaw’s case that police militarization in some form is both inevitable and necessary:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Arms and the Cop', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aurora, Colo, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Isla Vista, Calif, Massacre (2014)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Shootings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Rodney Glen', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbine High School', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Symbionese Liberation Army', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ferguson (Mo)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-18T19:01:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bddb0e4-0d28-5c6d-a478-d7c96f7beb5d', 'word_count': 1694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bddb0e4-0d28-5c6d-a478-d7c96f7beb5d'}, {'abstract': 'Four current projects, stretching from coast to coast, hand over billboards to various artists to use as their canvas.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/18/highway-billboard-art-everywhere-anne-thompson-mickalene-thomas/', 'snippet': 'Four current projects, stretching from coast to coast, hand over billboards to various artists to use as their canvas.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Visual pollution. Sky trash. Litter on a stick. The junk mail of the American highway.” That’s how billboards are described on the website of Scenic America, a group devoted to “preserving and enhancing the visual character” of the country. But while preservationists deride the billboard, artists have long been intrigued by it for its role in American highway culture. (Besides, what artist wouldn’t want a 300-square-foot canvas with a guaranteed audience?)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/t-magazine/18billboard-chan-1/18billboard-chan-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/18/t-magazine/18billboard-chan-1/18billboard-chan-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/t-magazine/18billboard-chan-1/18billboard-chan-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/18/t-magazine/18billboard-chan-1/18billboard-chan-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', '