rosecutors (Independent Counsel)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Botsford, David L', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-19T15:35:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Montgomery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Montgomery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f086cc7-a192-5c12-93dd-847a69937322', 'word_count': 897, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f086cc7-a192-5c12-93dd-847a69937322'}, {'abstract': 'There is a debate over whether or not Red Wolves should be permitted to mate with Eastern coyotes. Is it better to leave genetic diversity to nature or should we try to preserve endangered species?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/19/should-red-wolves-be-allowed-to-mate-with-coyotes/', 'snippet': 'There is a debate over whether or not Red Wolves should be permitted to mate with Eastern coyotes. Is it better to leave genetic diversity to nature or should we try to preserve endangered species?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the past century, a new medium-size predator has appeared in the Northeast. Some call it the coywolf; others prefer ”Eastern coyote.” In times past, the animal was called ”coydog.” All monikers are somewhat correct: The most recent genetic analysis suggests that the animal is roughly one-quarter wolf and two-thirds coyote. The remainder of its genome comes from dogs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/magazine/RedWolf1/RedWolf1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/15/magazine/RedWolf1/RedWolf1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/magazine/RedWolf1/RedWolf1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/magazine/RedWolf1/RedWolf1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1216, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/magazine/RedWolf1/RedWolf1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/15/magazine/RedWolf1/RedWolf1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/magazine/RedWolf1/RedWolf1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Red Wolves Be Allowed to Mate With Coyotes?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-19T15:46:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By MOISES VELASQUEZ-MANOFF', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Moises', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'VELASQUEZ-MANOFF', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d4aa789-99b2-5edf-b843-bba22279ec89', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d4aa789-99b2-5edf-b843-bba22279ec89'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty songs with lyrics written by Bob Dylan decades ago will be released with music written by five other artists.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/19/unknown-dylan-songs-get-music-by-elvis-costello-marcus-mumford-and-others/', 'snippet': 'Twenty songs with lyrics written by Bob Dylan decades ago will be released with music written by five other artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bob Dylan’s Basement Tapes have always fascinated the singer’s fans. Some of these informal recordings, which Mr. Dylan made with the Band in 1967, while recovering from injuries sustained in a motorcycle accident the previous year, turned up on one of the earliest rock bootlegs, “Great White Wonder,” in 1969, and two dozen of them were released officially on the “The Basement Tapes” in 1975 (with a few more songs turning up on Mr. Dylan’s “Bootleg Series” compilations). Bootleg collectors have another 10 CD’s worth of material, including original songs, covers and blues and folk tunes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/arts/19artsbeat-bob/19artsbeat-bob-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/20/arts/19artsbeat-bob/19artsbeat-bob-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 798}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/arts/19artsbeat-bob/19artsbeat-bob-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 770, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/arts/19artsbeat-bob/19artsbeat-bob-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1885, 'width': 1417, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/arts/19artsbeat-bob/19artsbeat-bob-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/20/arts/19artsbeat-bob/19artsbeat-bob-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/arts/19artsbeat-bob/19artsbeat-bob-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unknown Dylan Songs Get Music by Elvis Costello, Marcus Mumford and Others', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dylan Lyrics Get Music By Illustrious Ensemble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dylan, Bob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-19T15:50:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/804c620b-e023-52c9-b3bb-0591bfb2942d', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/804c620b-e023-52c9-b3bb-0591bfb2942d'}, {'abstract': 'Unlike baseball, tennis has no generally accepted way to keep score, so each reporter’s notebook has a language all its own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/25/sports/tennis/tennis-unlike-baseball-has-no-set-way-for-keeping-score.html', 'snippet': 'Unlike baseball, tennis has no generally accepted way to keep score, so each reporter’s notebook has a language all its own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'North of the Mets-Willets Point stop of the No. 7 subway line and the Long Island Rail Road, there is a standard written language: When the second baseman, the shortstop and the first baseman combine for a double play at Citi Field, reporters in the press box and fans in the seats write “4-6-3” on their scorecards. If the next batter strikes out swinging, a “K” tells the story.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Keep Score: However You Like', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Keep Score: However You Like', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothenberg, Ben', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-19T15:52:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d7d441d-e033-56d9-b2ee-c0041900a9c8', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d7d441d-e033-56d9-b2ee-c0041900a9c8'}, {'abstract': 'I thought I’d planned for all the changes coming our way when my family moved to a new city. Then — silly me — I realized you can’t plan for the unexpected.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/19/and-then-the-dog-died-things-you-cant-plan-for-when-planning-a-move/', 'snippet': 'I thought I’d planned for all the changes coming our way when my family moved to a new city. Then — silly me — I realized you can’t plan for the unexpected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When planning my family’s move to Nashville from Atlanta, one of the things I put a lot of thought into was creating a sense of consistency in order to manage how much change and disorder our children would experience this summer. I read somewhere that children need to know they can rely on some things to stay the same, even when a big transition comes along.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/us/motherlode-howl/motherlode-howl-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 546, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/19/us/motherlode-howl/motherlode-howl-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 546}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/us/motherlode-howl/motherlode-howl-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 931, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/us/motherlode-howl/motherlode-howl-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1091, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/us/motherlode-howl/motherlode-howl-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/19/us/motherlode-howl/motherlode-howl-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/us/motherlode-howl/motherlode-howl-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'And Then The Dog Died: Things You Can’t Plan for When Planning a Move', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-