nization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac672a13-7d4d-51e6-8724-4d38faccf2c3', 'word_count': 1021, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac672a13-7d4d-51e6-8724-4d38faccf2c3'}, {'abstract': 'Some people worry about robots taking jobs from humans, but others are looking at the ways company software and hardware might be changing how humans work — sometimes for the worse.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/19/when-technology-makes-work-worse/', 'snippet': 'Some people worry about robots taking jobs from humans, but others are looking at the ways company software and hardware might be changing how humans work — sometimes for the worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When we talk about robots taking people’s jobs, we often mean it almost literally — we envision if not the humanoid androids of science fiction, then at least machines programmed to do tasks once done by humans. But\xa0technology may also be altering Americans’ working lives in another way, via the software and hardware that companies use to determine when and how they work.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/opinion/nyto-robot/nyto-robot-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/15/opinion/nyto-robot/nyto-robot-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/opinion/nyto-robot/nyto-robot-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1004, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/opinion/nyto-robot/nyto-robot-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2008, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/opinion/nyto-robot/nyto-robot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/15/opinion/nyto-robot/nyto-robot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/15/opinion/nyto-robot/nyto-robot-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Technology Makes Work Worse', 'kicker': 'Op-Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-19T15:58:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna North', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'North', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b71694c-c57b-5289-86e1-892ba4875395', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b71694c-c57b-5289-86e1-892ba4875395'}, {'abstract': 'Among Diamond Dallas Page’s dozen or so workouts, there was one that I steered clear of: the “Psycho Extreme Workout.” The other day, however, boredom and curiosity got the best of me.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/19/i-tried-to-work-out-like-a-pro-wrestler-and-failed/', 'snippet': 'Among Diamond Dallas Page’s dozen or so workouts, there was one that I steered clear of: the “Psycho Extreme Workout.” The other day, however, boredom and curiosity got the best of me.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much of the discussion in my recent profile of Diamond Dallas Page, the former-wrestler-turned-fitness-guru, centered on the therapeutic uses of DDP Yoga. 