nization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f73cab87-4641-5ebf-a70d-83ef7e6ee0ce', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f73cab87-4641-5ebf-a70d-83ef7e6ee0ce'}, {'abstract': 'The Paris-born actor opens up about stealing clothes (from his friends), why smoking isn’t cool and his trademark tangle of hair.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/19/louis-garrel-jealousy-rules-of-style/', 'snippet': 'The Paris-born actor opens up about stealing clothes (from his friends), why smoking isn’t cool and his trademark tangle of hair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Louis Garrel doesn’t have to rely on fashion to be stylish. The Paris-born son of bohemian artists (the director Philippe Garrel and the actress Brigitte Sy) was born that way. The actor, best known for his role in Bernardo Bertolucci’s 2003 sex-charged drama “The Dreamers,” says that his wardrobe is a patchwork of loaners from friends and collaborators. “I really don’t buy clothes,” the 31-year-old insists. Of course, sometimes he does, but not from the gilded boutiques on rue Saint-Honoré; he prefers hidden-gem shops with little signage and fewer brand names. It’s an unexpected revelation from a guy who has been shot by Bruce Weber and styled by Carine Roitfeld, appeared on the cover of European fashion bibles and been the face of Valentino’s Uomo fragrance since last year.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/t-magazine/19garrell-barna/19garrell-barna-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/19/t-magazine/19garrell-barna/19garrell-barna-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2