e-it-off-video-a-dance-critics-take/', 'snippet': 'In “Shake It Off,” Ms. Swift is like Lucille Ball or Carol Burnett, a heroine triumphing through klutziness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The controversial music video of the moment is Taylor Swift’s “Shake It Off,” which was released on Monday. The controversy seems mainly to be about three things: 1) Ms. Swift’s move to pop material (a sign, for certain fans, of selling out); 2) her dancing; and 3) the supposed racism in a section on twerking. On Twitter, the rapper Earl Sweatshirt, after admitting that he hadn’t actually watched the video, rather quickly accused Ms. Swift of “perpetuating black stereotypes.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/arts/19artsbeat-taylor1/19artsbeat-taylor1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/19/arts/19artsbeat-taylor1/19artsbeat-taylor1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/arts/19artsbeat-taylor1/19artsbeat-taylor1-jumbo.png', 'height': 619, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/arts/19artsbeat-taylor1/19artsbeat-taylor1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1201, 'width': 1987, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/19/arts/19artsbeat-taylor1/19artsbeat-taylor1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/19/arts/19artsbeat-taylor1/19artsbeat-taylor1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailw