: [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prevost', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5f6911d-7ea8-59c7-9d69-09360536c9a0', 'word_count': 1269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5f6911d-7ea8-59c7-9d69-09360536c9a0'}, {'abstract': 'Americans think we are what we aren’t.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/20/inequality-delusions/', 'snippet': 'Americans think we are what we aren’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Via the FT, a new study compares perceptions of inequality across advanced nations. The big takeaway here is that Americans are more likely than Europeans to believe that they live in a middle-class society, even though income is really much less equally distributed here than in Europe. I’ve truncated the table to show the comparison between the U.S. and France: the French think they live in a hierarchical pyramid when they are in reality mostly middle-class, Americans are the opposite.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inequality Delusions', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-20T17:17:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54cc4420-4c63-5427-a200-df73843722c5', 'word_count': 208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54cc4420-4c63-5427-a200-df73843722c5'}, {'abstract': 'It’s not often that The Times editorial board agrees with the speaker.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/20/john-boehners-right-on-executive-secrecy/', 'snippet': 'It’s not