tters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e8d667a-0435-55ab-9682-70772ab8c650', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e8d667a-0435-55ab-9682-70772ab8c650'}, {'abstract': 'It’s exciting to solve a mystery — but, as many readers know, there may be something even more exciting about not quite solving it.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/20/why-we-love-what-we-dont-understand/', 'snippet': 'It’s exciting to solve a mystery — but, as many readers know, there may be something even more exciting about not quite solving it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was a kid, I read Gaston Leroux’s novel “The Phantom of the Opera.” I don’t remember much about it — very little about the titular phantom, or Christine, or the love story popularized in the Andrew Lloyd Webber version. What I remember is the rat-catcher.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/opinion/nyto-screenshot/nyto-screenshot-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/20/opinion/nyto-screenshot/nyto-screenshot-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/opinion/nyto-screenshot/nyto-screenshot-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/20/opinion/nyto-screenshot/nyto-screenshot-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1295, 'width': 1308, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20