aron M. Kessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aaron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66da382a-3858-54b4-97fd-9e51ed2e711e', 'word_count': 1319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66da382a-3858-54b4-97fd-9e51ed2e711e'}, {'abstract': 'The light and landscape of coastal Koktebel has drawn artists for nearly a decade, but Russia’s annexation of Crimea has split the normally peaceful oasis into feuding factions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/21/world/europe/annexation-of-crimea-divides-an-artist-colony-founded-on-tolerance.html', 'snippet': 'The light and landscape of coastal Koktebel has drawn artists for nearly a decade, but Russia’s annexation of Crimea has split the normally peaceful oasis into feuding factions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KOKTEBEL, Crimea — In Soviet times, when favored artists received a government stipend to summer here on Crimea’s southern coast, a metal billboard by the beach read in bold letters: “Be quiet! Writers are working!”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Annexation of Crimea Divides an Artist Colony Founded on Tolerance', 'kicker': 'Koktebel Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Annexation of Crimea Divides an Artist Colony Founded on Tolerance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Koktebel (Crimea)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crimea (Ukraine)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Voloshin, Max (1877-1932)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-21T00:28:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fa5adce-5f96-526d-b7b6-9797d3bafeec', 'word_count': 1305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fa5adce-5f96-526d-b7b6-9797d3bafeec'}, {'abstract': 'Jocelyn Ford’s documentary “Nowhere to Call Home,” an examination of prejudice in Beijing and Tibet, hopes to stir dialogue in China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/movies/nowhere-to-call-home-examines-prejudices.html', 'snippet': 'Jocelyn Ford’s documentary “Nowhere to Call Home,” an examination of prejudice in Beijing and Tibet, hopes to stir dialogue in China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — When the Tibetan farmer Zanta’s husband died, she was forced by local custom to move in with her in-laws, who forbade her son to attend school. Instead, she packed up and moved to Beijing, where she was helped by a relative from another lifetime.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24SUBTIBETFILM1/24SUBTIBETFILM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24SUBTIBETFILM1/24SUBTIBETFILM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24SUBTIBETFILM1/24SUBTIBETFILM1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i