 '2014-08-21T13:59:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46ff8bbe-7fc0-5003-a3c0-ebbfc426cdff', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46ff8bbe-7fc0-5003-a3c0-ebbfc426cdff'}, {'abstract': 'Lauren Gunderson’s play “Bauer” delves into the mostly forgotten drama revolving around two important figures in the founding of the Guggenheim Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/theater/bauer-recalls-key-figures-in-the-guggenheims-creation.html', 'snippet': 'Lauren Gunderson’s play “Bauer” delves into the mostly forgotten drama revolving around two important figures in the founding of the Guggenheim Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somewhere in New Jersey in the early 1950s, an artist who was once famous and his wife live alone in an oceanside mansion. Bitter, aging and in ill health, he hasn’t painted for more than 10 years. One day, the couple receive a visitor, an imperious woman of a certain age. She and the painter had once been lovers. An artist herself, as well as a museum curator with considerable financial resources, she had been a zealous supporter of his career. But they’d broken up a decade earlier and hadn’t seen each other since.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24BAUER1/24BAUER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 441, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24BAUER1/24BAUER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 441}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption