ame': None, 'lastname': 'Feuer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1526596-242f-5515-94d9-77ff6f4627ca', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1526596-242f-5515-94d9-77ff6f4627ca'}, {'abstract': 'The finding provides clues to how autism develops from childhood on, and may help explain some symptoms like oversensitivity to noise or social experiences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/22/health/brains-of-autistic-children-have-too-many-synapses-study-suggests.html', 'snippet': 'The finding provides clues to how autism develops from childhood on, and may help explain some symptoms like oversensitivity to noise or social experiences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a baby’s brain develops, there is an explosion of synapses, the connections that allow neurons to send and receive signals. But during childhood and adolescence, the brain needs to start pruning those synapses, limiting their number so different brain areas can develop specific functions and are not overloaded with stimuli.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/22/science/21autism/21autism-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1048, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/22/science/21autism/21autism-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1048}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/22/science/21autism/21autism-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 587, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/22/science/21autism/21autism-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1329, 'width': 