around Carr’s friendship with his former teacher, David Kammerer (Michael C. Hall), which edged into obsession and ended with a fatal confrontation in Riverside Park. The film “is at once a lurid true-crime chronicle and a coming-out story,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “Its vision of the past is stereoscopic, affirming Allen’s erotic self-discovery even as it explores the shadows of the closet where he and his friends must dwell.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T05:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brandon K. Thorp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brandon', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Thorp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b303439-7dc3-5589-a328-495f1639f1ba', 'word_count': 718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b303439-7dc3-5589-a328-495f1639f1ba'}, {'abstract': 'Accused of corruption, a former deputy director of the Beijing Zoo stuck to his story about earning some of his extra millions of renminbi by driving a cab at night. His explanations are a long shot.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/22/beijing-zoo-official-by-day-taxi-driver-by-night-court-told/', 'snippet': 'Accused of corruption, a former deputy director of the Beijing Zoo stuck to his story about earning some of his extra millions of renminbi by driving a cab at night. His explanations are a long shot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The former deputy director of the Beijing Zoo can explain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'capti