tion': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24talk/mag-24Talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1079, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24talk/mag-24Talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1079}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24talk/mag-24Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 570, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24talk/mag-24Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1139, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24talk/mag-24Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24talk/mag-24Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24talk/mag-24Talk-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lizzy Caplan, Sex-Ed Teacher to the World', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Teaching America Sex Ed, Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Caplan, Lizzy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spitznagel, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Masters of Sex (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T14:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Eric Spitznagel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Eric', 'lastname': 'Spitznagel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/529d7bfc-4902-55c6-b2d6-c8fc934fdd7b', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/529d7bfc-4902-55c6-b2d6-c8fc934fdd7b'}, {'abstract': 'The most important hitting partner in tennis steps out of the shadows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/magazine/serena-williamss-secret-weapon.html', 'snippet': 'The most important hitting partner in tennis steps out of the shadows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sascha Bajin — Big Sascha, as he’s known in tennis circles — figures that he spends about 330 days a year with Serena Williams. Usually, when he takes time off, he says, “I tell her not to call me, not to text me, not to contact me at all.” But this summer was different. After a disappointing Wimbledon, in which Williams lost in the third round in singles and retired from a doubles match with her sister Venus, Bajin whisked her away to Croatia for two weeks. When she returned to competition, Williams won the first tournament she played. Accepting the trophy, she dedicated the victory to the 29-year-old Bajin — her longtime hitting partner, a man with perhaps the most specialized job in all of tennis.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24partners/24partners-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24partners/24partners-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 734}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24partners/24partners-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 837, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24partners/24partners-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1674, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24partners/mag-24Partners-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24partners/mag-24Partners-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24partners/mag-24Partners-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Serena Williams’s Secret Weapon', 'kicker': 'The U.S. Open Issue ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Invisible and Indispensable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steinberger, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Serena', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bajin, Sascha', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T14:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Steinberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/708024d1-fad2-5149-a758-0dc3c51045f0', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/708024d1-fad2-5149-a758-0dc3c51045f0'}, {'abstract': 'A new generation of juniors might be able to get America back to the top — if they receive the right push.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/magazine/can-us-mens-tennis-rise-again.html', 'snippet': 'A new generation of juniors might be able to get America back to the top — if they receive the right push.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For an American growing up today, it can be difficult to imagine that the United States once dominated the men’s professional tennis circuit. “It would be nice if America actually had a famous tennis player,” said Noah Rubin, an 18-year-old who will make his first appearance in the United\xad States Open this week. In 2003, the last year an American man won a Grand Slam tournament — Andy Roddick at the U.S. Open — Rubin was just starting second grade on Long Island. “Any of my friends who aren’t huge tennis fans have barely heard of Andy Roddick,” he said the other day at the club where he trains on Randalls Island. “They’re like, ‘Wasn’t he a male model?’ ” (Close, Rubin’s father said: Roddick is married to a model, Brooklyn Decker.)', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24nextgen1/24nextgen1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24nextgen1/24nextgen1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarge