e One-Page Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T14:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/72f07b6d-719e-561d-ab04-4bb827e082ec', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/72f07b6d-719e-561d-ab04-4bb827e082ec'}, {'abstract': 'My grandfather’s letters reveal something of the man who never got home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/magazine/forgotten-postcards-from-mexico-city.html', 'snippet': 'My grandfather’s letters reveal something of the man who never got home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a handful of Sundays each year, my parents would pack my brother and me into the car with coloring books and crayons and shuttle us to visit my father’s family at his older sister’s home 30 minutes away in the woods of Connecticut. I loved going there, and one reason was the model-train set in the basement. After dutifully sitting with the adults for a while, we’d scramble down the stairs to gather around the oval track, which lay sprawled on a large table. The set had working lights, crossing signals and gates, and telephone wires were strung between poles. The train’s motor whirred as it sped around the tracks — ambling across a field, over a bridge and through a tunnel — and its steady pace always lulled me into a daydream about some future, far-off journey of my own.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24lives/mag-24Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24lives/mag-24Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24lives/mag-24Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24lives/mag-24Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1155, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24lives/mag-24Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24lives/mag-24Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24lives/mag-24Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Forgotten Postcards From Mexico City', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lost Track', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manko, Vanessa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deportation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Communism (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizenship and Naturalization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toys', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T14:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vanessa Manko', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vanessa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manko', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1391ef21-6191-57d4-a652-a7bd68cfe347', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1391ef21-6191-57d4-a652-a7bd68cfe347'}, {'abstract': 'Work pants meet horse blankets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/magazine/who-made-those-bluejeans.html', 'snippet': 'Work pants meet horse blankets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the beginning, there were Levi’s. In 1853, during the heart of the gold rush, a Bavarian émigré named Levi Strauss arrived in San Francisco from New York, looking to expand his family’s East Coast dry-goods business. Among his wares were blankets, cloth by the yard and durable work pants, sometimes called “jeans pants.” Then in 1872, one of Strauss’s customers, a tailor named Jacob Davis, made him the offer that would change his fortunes — and the way Americans dressed — forever. Davis had been buying Strauss’s blue denim and duck cloth to sew “waist-overalls” and had perfected a method of reinforcing them with the same copper rivets he used on horse blankets. Unwilling to put up the $68 necessary to patent his creation, he suggested that Strauss pay the fee in exchange for a half-interest in the business.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wmt/24wmt-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wmt/24wmt-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wmt/24wmt-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wmt/24wmt-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1477, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wmt/24wmt-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wmt/24wmt-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wmt/24wmt-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made Those Bluejeans?', 'kicker': 'Innovation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Bluejeans)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rehak, Melanie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jeans (Apparel)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Levi Strauss & Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yves Saint Laurent', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T14:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melanie Rehak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rehak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43ac0040-5ff9-50dc-b9ba-a03a3789f1c2', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43ac0040-5ff9-50dc-b9ba-a03a3789f1c2'}, {'abstract': 'A recent graduate seeks advice; a volunteer committee chair questions the ballots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/magazine/my-parents-paid-for-my-education-does-that-mean-i-need-to-take-a-soul-crushing-job.html', 'snippet': 'A recent graduate seeks advice; a volunteer committee chair questions the ballots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks to the full financial support of my parents, I recently graduated from an expensive private college in the Northeast with no student debt. After graduation I secured a salaried job with benefits at a large publishing house. I found the work thoroughly soul-crushing, so I quit and began working a number of minimum-wage jobs that do not set me up for any sort of “future.” While I enjoy my current lifestyle, I fear I might be violating an obligation to my parents, since my college degree is irrelevant to these jobs. Do I have an ethical duty to find a better job even if I’m unhappy with the work? NAME WITHHELD, BOSTON', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24ethicist/24ethicist-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 510, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr