': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Cowen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d96ed2f4-4ab2-545e-a786-261d4334e841', 'word_count': 1049, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d96ed2f4-4ab2-545e-a786-261d4334e841'}, {'abstract': 'All footage of the great tap- dancer Baby Laurence is rare. But a 1967 TV appearance can be found on YouTube.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/arts/dance/a-hoofer-for-the-ages.html', 'snippet': 'All footage of the great tap- dancer Baby Laurence is rare. But a 1967 TV appearance can be found on YouTube.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All footage of the great tap- dancer Baby Laurence is rare. Though he was hugely influential in the 1940s, adapting tap to bebop, this Charlie Parker of the feet was never very famous. Apart from a documentary filmed shortly before his death in 1974, the best and only surviving documentation of his art is an astonishing album he recorded in 1959 — the only documentation, that is, besides a clip you can find on YouTube.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24VIDEOS2/24VIDEOS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 314, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24VIDEOS2/24VIDEOS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 314}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/arts/24VIDEOS2/24VIDEOS2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 535, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url