kReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/753e252d-4778-54dd-a06e-3d767265710b', 'word_count': 766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/753e252d-4778-54dd-a06e-3d767265710b'}, {'abstract': 'Jacqueline Woodson’s memoir in verse offers poems about sibling rivalry, parents and the friends who help you through.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/jacqueline-woodsons-brown-girl-dreaming.html', 'snippet': 'Jacqueline Woodson’s memoir in verse offers poems about sibling rivalry, parents and the friends who help you through.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was 14 years old when I first read Nikki Giovanni’s masterly collection of poetry, “Cotton Candy on a Rainy Day.” As with most everything I read between the ages of 12 and 16, there was so much I didn’t understand. I was the first-\xadgeneration daughter of people who came from a small country, a country so small that I had yet to meet someone from there who could not connect the dots to my family in five seconds flat. I didn’t know a thing about Jim Crow, the American South, soul food or classic rhythm and blues. Yet like most kids who love to read, I understood the feeling behind the words, if not all of the meaning of the words. So when Giovanni wrote:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24CHAMBERS/24CHAMBERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 838, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24CHAMBERS/24CHAMBERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 838}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24CHAMBERS/24CHAMBERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1