{'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36df1c6a-cab0-58bf-a565-5cf1d3ee03c4', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36df1c6a-cab0-58bf-a565-5cf1d3ee03c4'}, {'abstract': 'Vikram Chandra, novelist and computer programmer, traces the connections between these two worlds of art and technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/geek-sublime-by-vikram-chandra.html', 'snippet': 'Vikram Chandra, novelist and computer programmer, traces the connections between these two worlds of art and technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the last half-century we’ve had a popular notion that our intellectual culture is sundered in two — the literary and the scientific. “The two cultures” is the bumper-sticker phrase for this view. It dates back to a hugely influential 1959 lecture, also published in book form that year, by C. P. Snow — “a moderately able research chemist who had become a successful novelist,” in the historian Lisa Jardine’s not very adulatory description. According to Snow, on one side were the humanists, on the other the scientists, and between them lay a shameful “gulf of mutual incomprehension.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24GLEICKSUB/0824-bks-GLEICK-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24GLEICKSUB/0824-bks-GLEICK-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24GLEICKSUB/0824-bks-GLEICK-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24GLEICKSUB/0824-bks-GLEICK-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1499, 'width': 1499, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24GLEICKSUB/24GLEICKSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24GLEICKSUB/24GLEICKSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24GLEICKSUB/24GLEICKSUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Unified Theory', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Unified Theory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gleick, James', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chandra, Vikram', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T17:40:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gleick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gleick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a80f84e-4484-5bb2-b945-1342237e6580', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a80f84e-4484-5bb2-b945-1342237e6580'}, {'abstract': 'The decade-long battle to overcome the ban against James Joyce’s “Ulysses” in America extended the definition of free speech.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/the-most-dangerous-book-about-joyces-ulysses.html', 'snippet': 'The decade-long battle to overcome the ban against James Joyce’s “Ulysses” in America extended the definition of free speech.', 'lead_paragraph': '“O, rocks! she said. Tell us in plain words.” So goes Molly Bloom’s plea to Leo\xadpold to explain metempsychosis, the transmigration of souls. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24SHTEIR/24SHTEIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 589, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24SHTEIR/24SHTEIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 589}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24SHTEIR/24SHTEIR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1005, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24SHTEIR/24SHTEIR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2010, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24SHTEIR/24SHTEIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24SHTEIR/24SHTEIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24SHTEIR/24SHTEIR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Publish and Cherish', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Publish and Cherish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Most Dangerous Book: The Battle for James Joyce's Ulysses (Book)", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Birmingham, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ulysses (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joyce, James', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T17:40:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Shteir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shteir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/64a16609-7f7a-5680-9822-50f8555cc603', 'word_count': 1320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64a16609-7f7a-5680-9822-50f8555cc603'}, {'abstract': 'Galileo. Newton. Salk. Curie. Science can change the world, but has Ellie’s grandfather gone too far?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/the-fourteenth-goldfish-by-jennifer-l-holm.html', 'snippet': 'Galileo. Newton. Salk. Curie. Science can change the world, but has Ellie’s grandfather gone too far?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who doesn’t enjoy a well-drawn curmudgeon? Children’s books are rife with them. From dour Eeyore moping about the Hundred Acre Wood to the irritable Mary Poppins, they come in all shapes and species. Proudly singular, such cantankerous characters are invariably exasperating, endearing and entertaining all at the same time. And now along comes Jennifer L. Holm with a doozy. Best known for her works of historical fiction, three of which have won Newbery Honors (“Our Only May Amelia,” “Penny From Heaven,” “Turtle in Paradise”), and the graphic novel series “Babymouse,” Holm uses a surprising twist to bring us a particularly memorable grouch in her latest, “The Fourteenth Goldfish.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Be Young Again', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Be Young Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holm, Jennifer L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T17:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Edinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Edinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8c44c3d-37f2-5166-827c-d148095c2d67', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8c44c3d-37f2-5166-827c-d148095c2d67'}, {'abstract': 'Two books assess the state of public education and the realities facing instructors today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/building-a-better-teacher-and-getting-schooled.html', 'snippet': 'Two books assess the state of public education and the realities facing instructors today.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Education is not the filling of a pail, but the lighting of a fire.” The greeting-card pithiness of this maxim obscures what is in fact a useful metaphor, and in “Building a Better Teacher,” Elizabeth Green introduces us to educators who stick to their kindling. Her project is both a history of the research on effective teaching as well as a consideration of how that research might best be implemented. What emerges is the gaping chasm between what the best teachers do and how we go about evaluating what they’ve done.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24STOCKMAN/0824-bks-STOCKMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 537, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24STOCKMAN/0824-bks-STOCKMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 537}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24STOCKMAN/0824-bks-STOCKMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 916, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24STOCKMAN/0824-bks-STOCKMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1274, 'width': 1424, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24STOCKMAN/24STOCKMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24STOCKMAN/24STOCKMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/books/review/24STOCKMAN/24STOCKMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The New School', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The New School', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Elizabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keizer, Garret', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T17:40:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebastian Stockman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebastian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stockman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/08ba999a-7b36-5285-a8b8-0a86e926d355', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08ba999a-7b36-5285-a8b8-0a86e926d355'}, {'abstract': 'The characters in three books for early readers are propelled by their self-deception and passion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/kate-dicamillos-leroy-ninker-saddles-up-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'The characters in three books for early readers are propelled by their self-deception and passion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Amelia Bedelia’s cheery misinterpretations to “Don’t Let the Pigeon Drive the Bus” and its impertinent bird hoping against hope for the impossible, delusions fuel a certain stripe of hero. Early chapter books — and here we have three by much-lauded children’s book authors — offer the perfect bridge between the freewheeling dreams of picture books and the serious narratives of longer fiction. These characters come by their delusions honestly and do not force the issue. And the more we indulge in their scattershot dreams, the more we want to believe in them too.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25BIRD/0825-bks-BIRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 474, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25BIRD/0825-bks-BIRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 474}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25BIRD/0825-bks-BIRD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 809, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25BIRD/0825-bks-BIRD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1320, 'width': 1671, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25BIRD/0825-bks-BIRD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25BIRD/0825-bks-BIRD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25BIRD/0825-bks-BIRD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Are They Kidding?', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Are They Kidding?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cronin, Doreen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiCamillo, Kate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hughes, Shirley', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Van Dusen, Chris', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornell, Kevin', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vulliamy, Clara', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-22T17:40:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Betsy Bird', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Betsy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bird', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/19ea8e3f-ef75-58ef-b21b-1ad1ee1316d1', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19ea8e3f-ef75-58ef-b21b-1ad1ee1316d1'}, {'abstract': 'Raina and her younger sister work out their sibling rivalry on a long and eventful car trip to a family reunion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/books/review/sisters-by-raina-telgemeier.html', 'snippet': 'Raina and her younger sister work out their sibling rivalry on a long and eventful car trip to a family reunion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Sisters,” the latest graphic memoir by Raina Telgemeier, it is the summer before high school and Raina is stuck between a squirrelly little brother and a volatile younger sister in a van without air conditioning. They are on a weeklong drive from their home in San Francisco to a family reunion in Colorado Springs, and as temperatures rise, so do tempers. Raina and her sister, Amara, argue over everything, from what to do about a snake on the loose (“What if we shut him in a suitcase till we’re home?”) to their parents’ relationship (“You don’t think Mom and Dad are gonna split up . . . do you?”). Memories of improbable pet deaths, their father’s unemployment and the difficulties of coexisting in a small apartment are woven into the tale of their emotional and sometimes tense journey through the American West.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25WAGENEN/0825-bks-Wagenen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 886, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25WAGENEN/0825-bks-Wagenen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 886}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25WAGENEN/0825-bks-Wagenen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 694, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25WAGENEN/0825-bks-Wagenen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1387, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25WAGENEN/25WAGENEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25WAGENEN/25WAGENEN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/books/review/25WAGENEN/25WAGENEN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Road', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_hea