, 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1c719e6-d016-5e7f-9ff2-0abb5b805820', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1c719e6-d016-5e7f-9ff2-0abb5b805820'}, {'abstract': 'We would not face the barbarism of ISIS without their sponsorship of Salafi Islam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/23/opinion/isis-atrocities-started-with-saudi-support-for-salafi-hate.html', 'snippet': 'We would not face the barbarism of ISIS without their sponsorship of Salafi Islam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALONG with a billion Muslims across the globe, I turn to Mecca in Saudi Arabia every day to say my prayers. But when I visit the holy cities of Mecca and Medina, the resting place of the Prophet Muhammad, I am forced to leave overwhelmed with anguish at the power of extremism running amok in Islam’s birthplace. Non-Muslims are forbidden to enter this part of the kingdom, so there is no international scrutiny of the ideas and practices that affect the 13 million Muslims who visit each year.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saudis Must Stop Exporting Extremism', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saudis Must Stop Exporting Extremism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Husain, Ed', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic State in Iraq and Syria (ISIS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunni Muslims', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shariah (Isla