n/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the International Herald Tribune archives: In 1964, Paris celebrates the 20th anniversary of its 1944 liberation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — ‘‘Why are the bells ringing, Herr General?’’ a young German secretary asked Dietrich von Choltitz, commander of German forces in Paris, 20 years ago tonight. Turning to his guest at the final dinner of the German headquarters staff in the Hôtel Meurice, on the Rue de Rivoli, Gen. Choltitz replied: ‘‘They are tolling for us. They are announcing the arrival of the enemy armies in Paris.’’ Led by the 13-ton bell of Notre Dame Cathedral, church bells in Paris resounded again in unison tonight as they did on Aug. 24, 1944, after four years of silence under the German occupation. In the continuing ceremonies marking the anniversary of the liberation, Parisians again gathered tonight on the Place de la Concorde. The program included a giant fireworks display. Two orchestras provided music for dancing until dawn.\xa0— New York Herald Tribune, European Edition, August 25, 1964', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1964: Paris Celebrates 20th Anniversary of the Liberation', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-24T15:48:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a7bb2e6-07d6-51c5-8410-0a919bf599db', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a7bb2e6-07d6-51c5-8410-0a919bf599db'}, {'abstract': 'That film, starring Chloë Grace Moretz, took in an estimated $16.4 million at North American theaters. But a holdover film, “Guardians of the Galaxy,” was No. 1.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/with-tears-if-i-stay-leads-new-releases/', 'snippet': 'That film, starring Chloë Grace Moretz, took in an estimated $16.4 million at North American theaters. But a holdover film, “Guardians of the Galaxy,” was No. 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The summer moviegoing season blubbered its way toward an end over the weekend, as “If I Stay,” a tear-jerker starring Chloë Grace Moretz, took in an estimated $16.4 million at North American theaters. That total made “If I Stay” (Warner Bros.) the best-performing new wide-release film, but Hollywood expected ticket sales to be significantly stronger given the generosity of Warner’s marketing campaign. “If I Stay,” based on Gayle Forman’s young adult novel, cost Warner and Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer about $11 million to make.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/arts/arts/arts-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 342, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/25/arts/arts/arts-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 342}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/25/arts/arts/arts-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201