 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e9ebba5-c37b-5121-9526-84f174d6cae5'}, {'abstract': 'One of the most beautiful strokes in tennis is in danger of disappearing entirely.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/24/magazine/the-death-of-the-one-handed-backhand.html', 'snippet': 'One of the most beautiful strokes in tennis is in danger of disappearing entirely.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in January, when Stan Wawrinka won the Australian Open, it was the first time in more than four years that someone not named Federer, Nadal, Djokovic or Murray captured a grand-slam championship. This was heartening for tennis fans, who have grown tired of watching the so-called Big Four dominate the sport for the last decade. But for a small and perhaps dwindling set of tennis aesthetes, it was noteworthy for a less obvious reason. Wawrinka, a 29-year-old Swiss player, uses one of the prettiest strokes in tennis: a singular display of agility, balance and precision timing known as the one-handed backhand. It is far superior for slice, or underspin; it is also much better for drop shots and volleys and for retrieving difficult-to-reach balls. Good players can conjure exquisite angles with it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wawrinka2/mag-24Wawrinka-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wawrinka2/mag-24Wawrinka-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 563}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/24/magazine/24wawrinka2/mag-24Wawrinka-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 961, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '