s nonverbal, but what about the words that go into passing it from person to person, generation to generation? Tatsumi Hijikata, who founded the Japanese movement art Butoh in the 1960s, left behind a wealth of words when he died, in 1986, texts that can be as unsettling to read as Butoh is to watch. In an essay, the filmmaker and critic Nanako Kurihara describes the “strange, equivocal” images that populated his writings: “rotting space,” “dribbling candy.” A corporeal response to the emotional wreckage of postwar Japan, Butoh channeled those images through the flesh.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/arts/MINA/MINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/26/arts/MINA/MINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/arts/MINA/MINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/arts/MINA/MINA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/arts/MINA/MINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/26/arts/MINA/MINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/arts/MINA/MINA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'su