, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-26T00:32:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Banker White', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Banker', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'White', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cc92d1d-38ed-514e-b20d-a41d288a76c9', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cc92d1d-38ed-514e-b20d-a41d288a76c9'}, {'abstract': 'Reporters should have the right to witness executions in Oklahoma from start to end, a federal suit filed in Oklahoma City on Monday by the American Civil Liberties Union contends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/26/us/oklahoma-suit-seeks-reporting-access-at-executions.html', 'snippet': 'Reporters should have the right to witness executions in Oklahoma from start to end, a federal suit filed in Oklahoma City on Monday by the American Civil Liberties Union contends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reporters should have the right to witness executions in Oklahoma from start to end, a federal suit filed in Oklahoma City on Monday by the American Civil Liberties Union contends. The suit was prompted by the bungled execution of Clayton D. Lockett on April 29, where witnesses were not allowed to watch as officials spent 51 minutes struggling to insert catheters into Mr. Lockett’s veins and where witnesses had their view blocked again after Mr. Lockett began to writhe and moan. “At the moment when public oversight was most important,” the suit states, citing the First Amendment and Oklahoma law, “the state unlawfully shrouded its execution proceedings in secrecy.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oklahoma: Suit Seeks Reporting Access at Executions ', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Plains', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oklahoma: Suit Seeks Reporting Access at Executions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eckholm, Erik', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Civil Liberties Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lockett, Clayton Darrell', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Witnesses', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-26T00:37:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7afc239b-cc9f-5733-bc3d-c4384fa87d43', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7afc239b-cc9f-5733-bc3d-c4384fa87d43'}, {'abstract': 'The number of middle school and high school students in the United States who say they have tried e-cigarettes but not traditional cigarettes increased by about 60 percent from 2012 to 2013.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/26/us/e-cigarette-use-said-to-rise-among-tobacco-free-youths.html', 'snippet': 'The number of middle school and high school students in the United States who say they have tried e-cigarettes but not traditional cigarettes increased by about 60 percent from 2012 to 2013.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The number of middle school and high school students in the United States who say they have tried e-cigarettes but not traditional cigarettes increased by about 60 percent from 2012 to 2013, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention said Monday. An estimated 263,000 middle and high school students used e-cigarettes but had never smoked regular cigarettes, up from about 160,000 in 2012 and about 79,000 in 2011. Youth smoking rates dropped by half from 1997 to 2011, according to federal data, but the pace of the decline has slowed. Many health advocates worry that e-cigarettes may lead teenagers to use conventional cigarettes. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'E-Cigarette Use Said to Rise Among Tobacco-Free Youths', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'E-Cigarette Use Said to Rise Among Tobacco-Free Youths', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tavernise, Sabrina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Cigarettes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-26T00:39:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sabrina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tavernise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_i