: 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a704442-20b6-5247-9294-3211db9f53d8', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a704442-20b6-5247-9294-3211db9f53d8'}, {'abstract': 'An academic paper finds that people’s answers change over the months that the government surveys them, likely understating the official jobless rate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/26/upshot/a-new-reason-to-question-the-official-unemployment-rate.html', 'snippet': 'An academic paper finds that people’s answers change over the months that the government surveys them, likely understating the official jobless rate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Labor Department’s monthly jobs report has been the subject of some wacky conspiracy theories. None was wackier than the suggestion from Jack Welch, the former General Electric chief executive, that government statisticians were exaggerating job growth during President Obama’s 2012 re-election campaign. Both Republican and Democratic economists dismissed those charges as silly.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/business/26up-leonhardt/26up-leonhardt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/26/business/26up-leonhardt/26up-leonhardt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/business/26up-leonhardt/26up-leonhardt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/26/business/26up-leonhardt/26up-leonhardt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy'