': 'persons', 'value': 'Bennett, Genevieve', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinch, Russell', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-27T21:34:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a81de28-7fa5-5afc-bf76-1226b55a9f61', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a81de28-7fa5-5afc-bf76-1226b55a9f61'}, {'abstract': 'Wall vases so pretty that flowers are optional.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/28/garden/improving-on-nature.html', 'snippet': 'Wall vases so pretty that flowers are optional.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Keiichi Tanaka’s ceramic wall vases were not holding botanicals, they would easily stand alone as sculpture. “I’m inspired by the old tools and handicrafts hanging on walls of old farmhouses,” Mr. Tanaka said, adding that he sought forms “that would look beautiful even without flowers and trees.” He makes the vases by hand in his home studio in Kawagoe, near Tokyo. He achieves the aged metallic look by applying a custom blended metal-infused glaze and firing each piece at 2,200 degrees Fahrenheit, then applying a black pigment and firing again at a lower temperature. His Linear vase ($310) and Circular vase ($280) are available from March in San Francisco. Information: 415-931-7433 or marchsf.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/garden/28CURR6/28CURR6-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/28/garden/28CURR6/28CURR6-articleLarge-