hey use on a daily basis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One ambitious start-up wants to reroute the plumbing of the consumer Internet. Now it has the money to try to make it happen. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/business/28bits-ifttt/28bits-ifttt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 552, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/28/business/28bits-ifttt/28bits-ifttt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 552}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/business/28bits-ifttt/28bits-ifttt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 943, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/business/28bits-ifttt/28bits-ifttt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1771, 'width': 1924, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/business/28bits-ifttt/28bits-ifttt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/28/business/28bits-ifttt/28bits-ifttt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/business/28bits-ifttt/28bits-ifttt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With $30 Million More in Hand, IFTTT Looks to the Internet of Things', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cash for Service Linking Devices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'IFTTT', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T15:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Isaac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14f1ad36-2e06-52d3-ac7a-450b246104b5', 'word_count': 719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14f1ad36-2e06-52d3-ac7a-450b246104b5'}, {'abstract': 'From designing to hand stitching, inmates from three different institutions collaborate on the Project Pietà label.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/28/peru-prison-fashion-project-pieta/', 'snippet': 'From designing to hand stitching, inmates from three different institutions collaborate on the Project Pietà label.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, the French-born, Peru-based textile specialist Thomas Jacob accompanied a friend on a trip to the San Jorge prison in Lima. “I was struck by the dignity of the inmates,” the 27-year-old recalls. “They didn’t have a mind for pain, they just accepted their fate with sobriety and humility.” He located two similar correctional facilities near the country’s capital — Santa Monica in Chorrillos and San Pedro in Lurigancho — and established a system that would enable the inmates at each to work together making clothes. The nascent fashion collective took its name, Project Pietà, from a masterpiece by Michelangelo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/t-magazine/28peru_pieta/28peru_pieta-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/28/t-magazine/28peru_pieta/28peru_pieta-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/t-magazine/28peru_pieta/28peru_pieta-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/t-magazine/28peru_pieta/28peru_pieta-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 472, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/t-magazine/28peru_pieta/28peru_pieta-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/28/t-magazine/28peru_pieta/28peru_pieta-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/28/t-magazine/28peru_pieta/28peru_pieta-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Inspiring Fashion Collective Springs From Peru’s Prison System', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T15:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeanine Celeste Pang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeanine', 'middlename': 'Celeste', 'lastname': 'Pang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfa9158d-b480-5f87-833c-07d2cf79f18d', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfa9158d-b480-5f87-833c-07d2cf79f18d'}, {'abstract': 'The filmmaker David Lynch, who was first a painter, has his first United States retrospective, at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/arts/design/museum-show-for-david-lynch-who-began-as-a-visual-artist.html', 'snippet': 'The filmmaker David Lynch, who was first a painter, has his first United States retrospective, at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — David Lynch’s rooftop painting studio, perched high in the Hollywood Hills, is littered with the byproduct of work. Paintings with crude, childlike figures doing menacing things lean up against the walls, unfinished drawings are strewn over his huge desk, and the floor is carpeted with cigarette butts. While the dark visual sensibility of his film work — “Eraserhead” (1977), “The Elephant Man” (1980), “Blue Velvet” (1986), “Wild at Heart” (1990), “Mulholland Drive” (2001), and his TV series “Twin Peaks” (1990-91) — has permeated the public consciousness and widely influenced other filmmakers, writers and artists (including Cindy Sherman and Gregory Crewdson), Mr. Lynch’s own visual art is almost unknown. Yet painting is where he started, enrolled as an advanced student at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts in Philadelphia in 1966 and ’67, and it is the medium he continues to work in most actively. His first United States museum retrospective, “David Lynch: The Unified Field,” opens at the Pennsylvania Academy on Sept. 13.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/art