 to Say Ouch', 'kicker': 'The Haggler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When a Customer Has Two Reasons to Say Ouch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customer Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hair', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ideal Image Development Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Laser (Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T17:51:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Segal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Segal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/31f82394-d390-5187-8a82-64d0d9ce84b5', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31f82394-d390-5187-8a82-64d0d9ce84b5'}, {'abstract': '“Outlaw Pete,” based on a song of the same name, is for “for anybody who loves a good Western,” said his publisher.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/28/a-book-for-children-and-others-by-bruce-springsteen/', 'snippet': '“Outlaw Pete,” based on a song of the same name, is for “for anybody who loves a good Western,” said his publisher.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Springsteen has joined what is by now a long parade of rock stars (including Keith Richards, three of the Beatles, and Jimmy Buffett) who have written children’s books – or at least, what look like children’s books. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Book for Children, and Others, by Bruce Springsteen', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Book by Springsteen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springsteen, Bruce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T17:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e36d76c5-996d-5d34-ab70-be0ca108af17', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e36d76c5-996d-5d34-ab70-be0ca108af17'}, {'abstract': 'New York’s one restaurant devoted to the cooking of Moldova offers genuine details and food, and can make even a wedding crasher feel welcome.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/03/dining/hungry-city-moldova-in-midwood-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'New York’s one restaurant devoted to the cooking of Moldova offers genuine details and food, and can make even a wedding crasher feel welcome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Branches twine above. Balkan folk-punk pulses below. The mural on the back wall is a postcard writ large, of a cave monastery a millennium old, transplanted from the cliffs along the Dniester River to a utilitarian strip of Midwood, Brooklyn. The tablecloths are red and the chairs hooded in brocade, and every table seems to seat at least eight. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Invitation Necessary', 'kicker': 'Hungry City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Invitation Necessary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mishan, Ligaya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midwood (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Moldova (Brooklyn, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T17:57:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33bc6a2d-2a29-5118-abfe-7c8f21a6d556', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33bc6a2d-2a29-5118-abfe-7c8f21a6d556'}, {'abstract': 'A study seems to rule out assimilation as the reason for the sudden disappearance of the Paleo-Eskimo population that thrived alone for more than 4,000 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/science/study-offers-clues-to-arctic-mystery-paleo-eskimos-abrupt-extinction.html', 'snippet': 'A study seems to rule out assimilation as the reason for the sudden disappearance of the Paleo-Eskimo population that thrived alone for more than 4,000 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seven hundred years ago, the Dorset people disappeared from the Arctic. The last of the Paleo-Eskimos, the Dorset had dominated eastern Canada and Greenland for centuries, hunting seal and walrus through holes in the ice and practicing shamanistic rituals with ornate carvings and masks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/us/ARCTIC2/ARCTIC2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/29/us/ARCTIC2/ARCTIC2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 518}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/u