': {'original': 'By Joshua A. Krisch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Krisch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75cc6cae-1cdd-5bed-b58c-f694c3c45222', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75cc6cae-1cdd-5bed-b58c-f694c3c45222'}, {'abstract': '“The Disasters of War, 1800-1914,” a French museum show, illustrates how depictions of war have changed through the centuries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/arts/design/horror-is-a-constant-as-artists-depict-war.html', 'snippet': '“The Disasters of War, 1800-1914,” a French museum show, illustrates how depictions of war have changed through the centuries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LENS, France — The nightmare images come one after another: Three women, half-clothed, one with her legs spread open, lie on the floor, apparently raped; the naked body of a decapitated man hangs from a tree branch, his severed head stuck on a shorter branch; a man hauls a dead woman by her legs, her dress flipped up, exposing her underwear.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31LOUVRE3/31JPLOUVRE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31LOUVRE3/31JPLOUVRE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31LOUVRE3/31JPLOUVRE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31LOUVRE3/31JPLOUVRE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'he