on of expert ranking data provided by Fantasypros.com, the algorithm finds natural tiers and clusters within the data. See the charts here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantasy Football: Analyzing Draft Tiers With Advanced Statistics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fantasy Sports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T18:40:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Boris Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Boris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7e82d39-5531-55cb-963e-854d3926f9cf', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7e82d39-5531-55cb-963e-854d3926f9cf'}, {'abstract': 'Monrovia has become, in a few short weeks, a major focal point of the epidemic, with the outbreak overwhelming the government of President Ellen Johnson Sirleaf.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/world/africa/in-liberias-capital-an-ebola-outbreak-like-no-other.html', 'snippet': 'Monrovia has become, in a few short weeks, a major focal point of the epidemic, with the outbreak overwhelming the government of President Ellen Johnson Sirleaf.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONROVIA, Liberia — Some people are swimming in and out of the Ebola quarantine zone in this seaside capital. One man slips out every day to reach his job at a Western embassy. Another has turned his living room into a tollbooth, charging others to escape through his apartment at the edge of the cordoned area. Countless others have used a different method: bribing their way out with fees that soldiers determine according to a person’s appearance, circumstances and even gender.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Ebola Grips Liberia’s Capital, a Quarantine Sows Social Chaos', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Ebola Grips City, Quarantine Adds to Chaos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Liberia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Onishi, Norimitsu', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson Sirleaf, Ellen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ebola Virus', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Monrovia (Liberia)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T18:49:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Norimitsu Onishi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Norimitsu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Onishi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21d7ba95-d75f-510d-a8e7-8fa7a831bbbc', 'word_count': 2289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21d7ba95-d75f-510d-a8e7-8fa7a831bbbc'}, {'abstract': 'Bethany Collins, Kevin Beasley and Abigail DeVille display their works in “Material Histories: Artists in Residence 2013-14” at the Studio Museum in Harlem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/arts/design/artists-in-residence-display-work-at-studio-museum-in-harlem.html', 'snippet': 'Bethany Collins, Kevin Beasley and Abigail DeVille display their works in “Material Histories: Artists in Residence 2013-14” at the Studio Museum in Harlem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Hammons’s “African American Flag” — with its Pan-African red and black stripes and green field of black stars — floats high over the sidewalk outside the Studio Museum in Harlem. Originally created nearly a quarter-century ago, it has become an identifying emblem for a museum dedicated to nurturing the careers of artists of African descent. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stuff of Life, Urgently Altered', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Stuff of Life, Urgently Altered', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Studio Museum in Harlem', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammons, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beasley, Kevin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Bethany', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeVille, Abigail', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T18:51:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8abae9dc-436e-564f-8692-139bd2a3bda1', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8abae9dc-436e-564f-8692-139bd2a3bda1'}, {'abstract': 'A pitch for considering a lifetime’s worth of carbon dioxide emissions when examining power plants in the context of climate change.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/28/accounting-for-the-expanding-carbon-shadow-from-coal-burning-plants/', 'snippet': 'A pitch for considering a lifetime’s worth of carbon dioxide emissions when examining power plants in the context of climate change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steven Davis of the University of California, Irvine, and Robert Socolow of Princeton (best known for his work dividing the climate challenge into carbon “wedges”) have written “Commitment accounting of CO2\xa0emissions,”\xa0a valuable new paper\xa0in\xa0Environmental Research Letters showing the value of shifting from tracking annual emissions of carbon dioxide from power plants to weighing the full amount of carbon dioxide that such plants, burning coal or gas, could emit during their time in service.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/12sino-coal/12sino-coal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/12/world/12sino-coal/12sino-coal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/12sino-coal/12sino-coal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/12sino-coal/12sino-coal-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1257, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/12sino-coal/12sino-coal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/12/world/12sino-coal/12sino-coal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 't