Weekend (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cross, Joseph (1986- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dolby, Tom (1975- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T21:16:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d872631-f851-509d-921a-43d5d5386bf1', 'word_count': 202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d872631-f851-509d-921a-43d5d5386bf1'}, {'abstract': 'The move comes as a result of the state’s retreat from Common Core, a set of reading and math standards adopted by more than 40 states.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/education/oklahoma-common-core-no-child-left-behind.html', 'snippet': 'The move comes as a result of the state’s retreat from Common Core, a set of reading and math standards adopted by more than 40 states.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a result of its retreat from Common Core, a set of reading and math standards adopted by more than 40 states, Oklahoma was notified Thursday that it lost its waiver from key provisions of No Child Left Behind, the signature Bush-era education law.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oklahoma Loses Waiver From No Child Left Behind Provisions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oklahoma: Schools Lose Exemption From No Child Left Behind Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'No Child Left Behind Act', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fallin, Mary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T21:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ed7d08e-6a3e-57f3-b1cf-97085662e97b', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ed7d08e-6a3e-57f3-b1cf-97085662e97b'}, {'abstract': 'A psychiatrist and his philosopher brother discuss how mental illness reflects culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/suspicious-minds-by-joel-gold-and-ian-gold.html', 'snippet': 'A psychiatrist and his philosopher brother discuss how mental illness reflects culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joel Gold first observed the Truman Show delusion — in which people believe they are the involuntary subjects of a reality television show whose producers are scripting the vicissitudes of their lives — on Halloween night 2003 at Bellevue Hospital, where he was the chief attending psychiatrist. “Suspicious Minds,” which he wrote with his brother, Ian, an associate professor of philosophy and psychology at McGill University, is an attempt to use this delusion, which has been observed by many clinicians, to pose questions that have gone out of fashion in psychiatry over the last half-century: Why does a mentally ill person have the delusions he or she has? And, following the lead of the medical historian Roy Porter, who once wrote that “every age gets the lunatics it deserves,” what can we learn about ourselves and our times from examining the content of madness?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31GREENBERG/0814-bks-GREENBERG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31GREENBERG/0814-bks-GREENBERG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31GREENBERG/0814-bks-GREENBERG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31GREENBERG/0814-bks-GREENBERG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1384, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31GREENBERG/0814-bks-GREENBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31GREENBERG/0814-bks-GREENBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31GREENBERG/0814-bks-GREENBERG-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Delusions We Deserve', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Delusions We Deserve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Suspicious Minds: How Culture Shapes Madness (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gold, Ian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gold, Joel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T21:19:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Greenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e80ef0f0-3400-50ac-995b-94ed54eb34a5', 'word_count': 927, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e80ef0f0-3400-50ac-995b-94ed54eb34a5'}, {'abstract': 'Kieron Barry’s “Tomorrow in the Battle,” at Stageworks/Hudson, follows powerful, self-destructive Londoners in a romantic triangle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/theater/kieron-barrys-tomorrow-in-the-battle-a-love-triangle.html', 'snippet': 'Kieron Barry’s “Tomorrow in the Battle,” at Stageworks/Hudson, follows powerful, self-destructive Londoners in a romantic triangle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HUDSON, N.Y. — Ah, the sudsy thrill of watching shallow people drowning in deep waters! This dubious pleasure is one I associate with summer — with glossy movies viewed furtively in air-conditioned theaters and books about the randy rich read through sunglasses on beach towels.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29TOMORROW/29TOMORROW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29TOMORROW/29TOMORROW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/2