yt://article/6d672869-568b-5cf6-8944-4f8b32ac4dae'}, {'abstract': '“The Naked Room” presents an up-close view of children grappling with emotional trauma in a psychiatric hospital in Mexico City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/movies/the-naked-room-filmed-in-a-psychiatrists-office.html', 'snippet': '“The Naked Room” presents an up-close view of children grappling with emotional trauma in a psychiatric hospital in Mexico City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The title of “The Naked Room” (“El Cuarto Desnudo”) doesn’t lie: This documentary from Nuria Ibáñez takes place almost entirely in an office at a children’s psychiatric hospital in Mexico City. Nearly all of what we see consists of patients, typically framed in head-and-shoulders shots, sharing their thoughts. Asking persistent questions, the doctors remain unseen. Parents occasionally appear but mostly speak from off camera.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29NAKED/29NAKED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29NAKED/29NAKED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29NAKED/29NAKED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29NAKED/29NAKED-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1049, 'width': 1865, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29NAKED/29NAKED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29NAKED/29NAKED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29NAKED/29NAKED-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Troubled Children, Baring Their Psychic Pain', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Naked Room (El Cuarto Desnudo)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico City (Mexico)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Naked Room (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T21:37:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Kenigsberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenigsberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86ed8a21-6490-588e-943b-64d3ef5b9239', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86ed8a21-6490-588e-943b-64d3ef5b9239'}, {'abstract': '“The Notebook (Le Grand Cahier),” based on a novel by Agota Kristof, is a flinty parable of brutality and resilience: World War II seen through the eyes of twin boys.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/movies/the-notebook-le-grand-cahier-from-janos-szasz.html', 'snippet': '“The Notebook (Le Grand Cahier),” based on a novel by Agota Kristof, is a flinty parable of brutality and resilience: World War II seen through the eyes of twin boys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is something irresistible — and also something questionable — about the artistic impulse to filter fictional accounts of real-world horror through the eyes of children. The juxtaposition of innocence and cruelty, of a naïve ignorance of political causes with a vivid understanding of their human effect, can refresh and deepen our perception of past atrocities. But there are risks, too: of sentimentality, of vagueness and of a wide-eyed naïveté that mystifies events we have an ethical duty to see as clearly as possible. For every “Pan’s Labyrinth,” which mines archetypes and fairy tales to unearth the hidden truth of Spanish fascism, there is “The Boy in the Striped Pajamas,” which turns the Holocaust into maudlin kiddie-kitsch.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29THENOTEBOOK/29THENOTEBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 288, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29THENOTEBOOK/29THENOTEBOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 288}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29THENOTEBOOK/29THENOTEBOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29THENOTEBOOK/29THENOTEBOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 984, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'capt