,” a comedy based on Charlotte Roche’s notorious novel.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Overshare” doesn’t begin to cover the behavior of Carla Juri’s teenage character, Helen, in Wetlands.” Within minutes, we learn about Helen’s body odor, a raunchy snack-stand tryst and her unusual approach to public toilet seats. The coming-of-age comedy, which adapts Charlotte Roche’s scatological memoirlike novel, is a mother’s nightmare, but Ms. Juri brings a fresh sense of mischief and a touching vulnerability to the role.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31SNAPSHOT/31SNAPSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 2010, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31SNAPSHOT/31SNAPSHOT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 2010}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31SNAPSHOT/31SNAPSHOT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 306, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31SNAPSHOT/31SNAPSHOT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 612, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31SNAPSHOT/31SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31SNAPSHOT/31SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31SNAPSHOT/31SNAPSHOT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Always Let Them See You Sweat', 'kicker': 'Snapshot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Always Let Them See You Sweat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Juri, Carla', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rapold, Nicolas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wnendt, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Wetlands (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T21:51:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Nicolas', 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2aaa658-62cc-556b-a069-f701744ae547', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2aaa658-62cc-556b-a069-f701744ae547'}, {'abstract': 'The National Ballet of Canada will perform Christopher Wheeldon’s “Alice” at David H. Koch Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/arts/dance/young-romance-is-part-of-wheeldons-wonderland.html', 'snippet': 'The National Ballet of Canada will perform Christopher Wheeldon’s “Alice” at David H. Koch Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just a few years back, it seemed as if evening-length ballets with detailed story lines were a thing of the past, a relic of the pre-postmodern imagination. Athleticism and abstraction were in; plot points and well-defined characters were out. Thus, the announcement, in 2010, that the Royal Ballet, with the National Ballet of Canada, had commissioned Christopher Wheeldon to create a new ballet based on “Alice in Wonderland” was greeted with a flurry of interest, and some eye rolling. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31ALICE1/31ALICE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31ALICE1/31ALICE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31ALICE1/31ALICE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31ALICE1/31ALICE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31ALICE1/31ALICE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31ALICE1/31ALICE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/arts/31ALICE1/31ALICE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Alice’ in All Its Teenage Subconscious', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Alice’ in All Its Teenage Subconscious', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Alice's Adventures in Wonderland (Ballet)", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wheeldon, Christopher', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Ballet of Canada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-28T21:51:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marina Harss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/345933fd-e366-5635-a63a-4f4ff36f74b9', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/345933fd-e366-5635-a63a-4f4ff36f74b9'}, {'abstract': 'Kevin Kline plays the swashbuckling actor Erroll Flynn in his waning years in “The Last of Robin Hood.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/movies/the-last-of-robin-hood-on-errol-flynns-final-years.html', 'snippet': 'Kevin Kline plays the swashbuckling actor Erroll Flynn in his waning years in “The Last of Robin Hood.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Last of Robin Hood” takes place during the final two years of Errol Flynn’s life, when his stardom and health had waned, but his ardor for girls had not. As played by Kevin Kline with an almost disconcerting low-key openness, Flynn trades on his name recognition with an earnest, self-deprecating manner, though that is itself a form of entitlement. A go-between reaches out first to 15-year-old Beverly Aadland (Dakota Fanning), who is invited to a play audition in a country house borrowed for the purpose, which is not to audition. (In 1943 Flynn was acquitted of statutory rape in a case whose notoriety stuck.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/29/arts/29LA