tions', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T05:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Cumming-Bruce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cumming-Bruce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/863be2e4-44bf-5371-86fd-45c198065406', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/863be2e4-44bf-5371-86fd-45c198065406'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/arts/television/whats-on-tv-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '1:30 P.M. (Cinemax) UP IN THE AIR (2009) George Clooney, above, his smile as bright as ever, portrays Ryan Bingham, a corporate assassin — he fires people for a living — and frequent flier who reaches a crossroads when an upstart (Anna Kendrick) tries to change the rules of the game in this film from Jason Reitman. Vera Farmiga is Alex, the fast-moving executive who, for the first time, causes Bingham to rethink his life. “\xa0‘Up in the Air’ is an assertively, and unapologetically, tidy package, from its use of romance to instill some drama into the narrative (the book introduces disease instead) and the mope-rock tunes that Mr. Reitman needlessly overuses,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times about this adaptation of the Walter Kirn novel. Still, one of the film’s pleasures is that its actresses “share the frame with Mr. Clooney as equals, not props.” The film, chosen by Ms. Dargis as a critic’s pick, received Oscar nominations for its three leads and Mr. Reitman, as well as a best picture nod.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T05:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf070e5d-c95d-54e6-90ff-5a73f65f5ce6', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf070e5d-c95d-54e6-90ff-5a73f65f5ce6'}, {'abstract': 'The success of Oudin, who reached the U.S. Open quarterfinals in 2009 when she was just 17, loomed again with the sensation caused by the 15-year-old Bellis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/29/sports/tennis/cici-belliss-run-elicits-memories-of-another-young-american-melanie-oudin.html', 'snippet': 'The success of Oudin, who reached the U.S. Open quarterfinals in 2009 when she was just 17, loomed again with the sensation caused by the 15-year-old Bellis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although turn-back-the-clock runs by Jimmy Connors, Andre Agassi and Andy Roddick have captured fans’ attention at past United States Opens, the imagination typically runs wilder with an unexpected arrival, and the hysteria that follows is exponentially greater when that arrival — like CiCi Bellis this year — is an American. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None