}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:42:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28caa590-bcb1-5b76-9267-9dde486448e6', 'word_count': 742, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28caa590-bcb1-5b76-9267-9dde486448e6'}, {'abstract': 'A look at past reviews of the letters of Sylvia Plath, Flannery O’Connor and Saul Bellow.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/priority-mail.html', 'snippet': 'A look at past reviews of the letters of Sylvia Plath, Flannery O’Connor and Saul Bellow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Throughout the years, authors’ collected letters have often featured in our pages (this week, Henry Giardina reviews “The Animals: Love Letters Between Christopher Isherwood and Don Bachardy”). Assessing Sylvia Plath’s “Letters Home” in 1975, Maureen Howard said the author’s dispatches “can be read like a novel,” including those Plath sent while studying at Smith College: “The letters are like little stories; here she is, Cinderella waltzing in her silver dress at a debutante party, or talking about life and love with handsome boys at any number of Ivy League colleges, or stunning her professors (all of them ‘dears’) with another villanelle.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0831-OpenBooks/0831-OpenBooks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0831-OpenBooks/0831-OpenBooks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 264}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0831-OpenBooks/0831-OpenBooks-jumbo.jpg'