eb19d2739', 'word_count': 1232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/684db640-1f72-51c8-9c05-e36eb19d2739'}, {'abstract': 'A restless executive subjects his family to an itinerant existence on three continents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/home-leave-by-brittani-sonnenberg.html', 'snippet': 'A restless executive subjects his family to an itinerant existence on three continents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elise, the mother in Brittani Sonnenberg’s debut novel, “Home Leave,” is one of those unknowable blondes often glimpsed in business-class lounges. Neither delayed flights to Singapore nor fussy children can rattle her poise, because looking serene while traveling is what she does. Elise’s husband, Chris Kriegstein, is a former high school basketball star who was mostly benched come senior year of college. He is competitive but not ambitious in the way of today’s Zuckerbergian entrepreneurs. Rather than start his own company, he stumps for someone else’s in conference rooms in Seoul, Bombay, Dubai — anywhere that’s not Chariton, Ind.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HUSTAD/31HUSTAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 783, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HUSTAD/31HUSTAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 783}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HUSTAD/31HUSTAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 995, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HUSTAD/31HUSTAD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 995, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HUSTAD/31HUSTAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HUSTAD/31HUSTAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HUSTAD/31HUSTAD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Expatriate Acts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Expatriate Acts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hustad, Megan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sonnenberg, Brittani', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:45:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Megan Hustad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Megan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hustad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33d5063f-f769-5c5a-ae8b-23aa6f701b33', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33d5063f-f769-5c5a-ae8b-23aa6f701b33'}, {'abstract': 'A frail humanity survives the unspeakable in Richard Flanagan’s novel of the Burma-Thailand Railway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/richard-flanagans-narrow-road-to-the-deep-north.html', 'snippet': 'A frail humanity survives the unspeakable in Richard Flanagan’s novel of the Burma-Thailand Railway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The title of Richard Flanagan’s sixth novel comes from a 17th-century Japanese classic, a little book by the poet Basho that mixes a prose travel narrative with haiku in its account of a long journey on foot. Basho went north from present-\xadday Tokyo through a mountainous land of often shattering beauty. Yet his walk was marked by moments of terrible loneliness, and he seemed to travel under a kind of compulsion, without a defined goal or purpose. Basho walks because he must, and in reading him the old cliché comes alive: Life is a journey. Whether that journey has any meaning, whether there’s anything beyond putting one foot in front of the other . . . well, that’s another question entirely.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0814-bks-GORRA/0814-bks-GORRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0814-bks-GORRA/0814-bks-GORRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 752}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0814-bks-GORRA/0814-bks-GORRA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 817, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0814-bks-GORRA/0814-bks-GORRA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1767, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0814-bks-GORRA/0814-bks-GORRA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0814-bks-GORRA/0814-bks-GORRA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/0814-bks-GORRA/0814-bks-GORRA-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bridge to Nowhere', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bridge to Nowhere', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flanagan, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:45:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gorra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfcdaf9c-36dc-52d8-998f-7f9257ce18af', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfcdaf9c-36dc-52d8-998f-7f9257ce18af'}, {'abstract': 'A celebrated British author embarks on a strange new career in these linked novellas by Alessandro Baricco.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/alessandro-bariccos-mr-gwyn-and-three-times-at-dawn.html', 'snippet': 'A celebrated British author embarks on a strange new career in these linked novellas by Alessandro Baricco.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is the line between creating and copying? How does the subject of a portrait, like a particle in a physics experiment, change and transform under the portraitist’s watchful gaze? How do writers hide and reveal themselves in their work, leaving sly traces that only their intimates might recognize? These questions lie at the heart of “Mr. Gwyn” and “Three Times at Dawn,” two clever interlocking novellas by the Italian writer Alessandro Baricco. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31DONADIO/0831-bks-DONADIO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 329, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31DONADIO/0831-bks-DONADIO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 329}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31DONADIO/0831-bks-DONADIO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 561, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31DONADIO/0831-bks-DONADIO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 823, 'width': 1501, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31DONADIO/0831-bks-DONADIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31DONADIO/0831-bks-DONADIO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31DONADIO/0831-bks-DONADIO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'His Penetrating Gaze', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'His Penetrating Gaze', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donadio, Rachel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baricco, Alessandro', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldstein, Ann (1949- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:46:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Donadio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6de9937d-666f-5205-9827-442f1bfc8c0d', 'word_count': 1102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6de9937d-666f-5205-9827-442f1bfc8c0d'}, {'abstract': 'A Russian immigrant, deported from the United States, attempts to reunite with his family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/the-invention-of-exile-by-vanessa-manko.html', 'snippet': 'A Russian immigrant, deported from the United States, attempts to reunite with his family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ambitiousness of Vanessa Manko’s debut novel is well represented by its title, which Homer might have blushed to use. “The Invention of Exile” spans four decades and three continents to tell the story of an epic love frustrated but never destroyed by political antagonism between nations, namely the United States and the brand-new Soviet Union. Its beleaguered hero, Austin Voronkov, debarks on Ellis Island (where his given name, Ustin, is quickly and permanently Americanized) in 1913; despite his engineering background, he takes a proletarian job at the Remington gun factory in Bridgeport, Conn. The reasons for his emigration are never divulged, though the fact that he “didn’t believe that workers and trained engineers were equal” suggests he has picked an opportune time to get out of Russia.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/31DEE/31DEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/31DEE/31DEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/31DEE/31DEE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/31DEE/31DEE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/31DEE/31DEE-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/31DEE/31DEE-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/31DEE/31DEE-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Love and Visas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love and Visas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manko, Vanessa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:47:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Dee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7c9d6d7-40b4-5821-bf53-e185a9b7dc6b', 'word_count': 1127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7c9d6d7-40b4-5821-bf53-e185a9b7dc6b'}, {'abstract': 'A tribute to an Asian superhero from the 1940s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/the-shadow-hero-by-gene-luen-yang.html', 'snippet': 'A tribute to an Asian superhero from the 1940s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hank Chu is your run-of-the-mill \xadChinese-American drenched in filial piety and surrounded by shelves of simple carbohydrates in his family’s grocery store. When we meet him, in the 1930s, he’s content to be a facsimile of his father, passing his days, item by item, behind the counter. The Chus sweep, they stock, they eat and if they’re lucky, one day they’ll die. Hank’s mother, Hua Chu, is the only one with enough chutzpah to desire more. She escapes the Chu family “Groundhog Day” by taking a job with a Mrs. Olson, who could be Miss Daisy, Cruella de Vil or any flavor of distinguished Caucasian woman with garden party headwear and a big body sedan. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HAUNGALT/0831-bks-HAUNG-alt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 288, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HAUNGALT/0831-bks-HAUNG-alt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 288}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HAUNGALT/0831-bks-HAUNG-alt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HAUNGALT/0831-bks-HAUNG-alt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 1746, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HAUNGALT/31HAUNGALT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HAUNGALT/31HAUNGALT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31HAUNGALT/31HAUNGALT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Out of His Shell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out of His Shell', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yang, Gene Luen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liew, Sonny', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:47:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eddie Huang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eddie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bf3f87a-c3ad-58ad-a035-91899b11bb63', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bf3f87a-c3ad-58ad-a035-91899b11bb63'}, {'abstract': 'Cultural influence has kept pace with South Korea’s growth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/the-birth-of-korean-cool-by-euny-hong.html', 'snippet': 'Cultural influence has kept pace with South Korea’s growth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1985, a 12-year-old Euny Hong moved with her family from suburban Chicago to Seoul, South Korea. Not just any place in Seoul, South Korea, but a neighborhood known as Apgujeong — the wealthiest, most exclusive cluster of addresses in the Gangnam district. The Hongs, in short, went “Gangnam Style” 27 years before it was a thing. And when it comes to South Korean history — as with meme superstardom — three decades is a long time. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31CHOI/31CHOI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 440, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31CHOI/31CHOI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 440}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31CHOI/31CHOI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31CHOI/31CHOI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1501, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31CHOI/31CHOI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31CHOI/31CHOI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31CHOI/31CHOI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seoul Survivors', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seoul Survivors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Choi, Mary H K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hong, Euny', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Birth of Korean Cool: How One Nation Is Conquering the World Through Pop Culture (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:48:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary H. K. Choi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'K', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1ac5b37-612c-5c37-b8ec-488c74b1fddf', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1ac5b37-612c-5c37-b8ec-488c74b1fddf'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Timberg traces his personal journey from disfigured Vietnam veteran to author and journalist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/robert-timbergs-blue-eyed-boy.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Timberg traces his personal journey from disfigured Vietnam veteran to author and journalist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January 1967 a land mine blew up under the not-quite-armored-enough Amphibian Tractor, or Amtrac, carrying United States Marine Lt. Robert Timberg. He was on a routine mission to deliver pay to front-line units in Vietnam. The gas tank carried 456 gallons of fuel with little protection from underneath. If it exploded, anyone inside would be, as the Marines said, “fricasseed.” So the passengers sat on the top or clung to the sides. Timberg was watching the road ahead, he writes, when “I felt myself lifted from the top of the Amtrac, as if in the eye of a hurricane, except in place of wind and rain I was being carried aloft by flames.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Long Road to Recovery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Long Road to Recovery', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Timberg, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:48:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Dickey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dickey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e67c910f-c040-5c10-97c2-9aa899649ac9', 'word_count': 1049, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e67c910f-c040-5c10-97c2-9aa899649ac9'}, {'abstract': 'In a nameless city, women are abducted, tattooed with crude maps and released.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/the-city-under-the-skin-by-geoff-nicholson.html', 'snippet': 'In a nameless city, women are abducted, tattooed with crude maps and released.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cast includes women who’ve been abducted, hooded, forcibly tattooed and released with no explanation. A bum, a stripper, a life coach, a real estate agent. They have nothing in common but the maps inked onto their backs — evidence of an unskilled hand, the maps illegible and crude. Playing the leads: a killer, Wrobleski; a hired thug, Billy Moore; his precocious 12-year-old daughter, Carla; a real estate mogul; and one cranky driver. In addition, two innocents turned amateur detectives — think “the Bloodhound Gang” (see PBS’s “3-2-1 Contact,” not the band) — who commit themselves, with no prelude or wherewithal, to solving the mystery of the tattooed women. And, as backdrop, a nameless city that feels like New York in about 50 years, but isn’t New York. Just some run-down urban mecca: corrupt, filthy, mysterious, cinematic. Put it all together and you’ve got Geoff Nicholson’s new crime novel, “The City Under the Skin,” which compels the way a good crime novel should: by making you turn the pages at a clip. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31SUBMAAXEL/31SUBMAAXEL-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31SUBMAAXEL/31SUBMAAXEL-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31SUBMAAXEL/31SUBMAAXEL-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31SUBMAAXEL/31SUBMAAXEL-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1332, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31SUBMAAXEL/31SUBMAAXEL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31SUBMAAXEL/31SUBMAAXEL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31SUBMAAXEL/31SUBMAAXEL-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bodies of Evidence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bodies of Evidence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nicholson, Geoff', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:48:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fiona Maazel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fiona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maazel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9de09fdf-47ea-5cef-adaa-25f33dde4486', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9de09fdf-47ea-5cef-adaa-25f33dde4486'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COMMAND AND CONTROL: Nuclear Weapons, the Damascus Accident, and the Illusion of Safety,  by Eric Schlosser. (Penguin, $18.) How do you deploy weapons of mass destruction without being destroyed by them? That’s the question at the heart of Schlosser’s disquieting examination of nuclear risk, which weaves a riveting account of an accident at a nuclear missile silo in rural Arkansas with a crisp historical narrative covering more than 70 years.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:49:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a80ed79b-afb9-5c22-aff1-d58a56cc2fea', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a80ed79b-afb9-5c22-aff1-d58a56cc2fea'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GEEK SUBLIME: The Beauty of Code, the Code of Beauty,  by Vikram Chandra. (Graywolf, paper, $16.) With great subtlety and depth, Chandra, who is both a novelist and a programmer, traces the connections between art and technology.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:49:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/01bbff2e-e9b8-5f04-aa4c-f14bfffc72d3', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01bbff2e-e9b8-5f04-aa4c-f14bfffc72d3'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Ryan, the Wisconsin Republican whose first book, “The Way Forward,” is No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has opened up about his father’s alcoholism and early death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/07/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Ryan, the Wisconsin Republican whose first book, “The Way Forward,” is No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has opened up about his father’s alcoholism and early death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whistle-Stop: Representative Paul Ryan, the Wisconsin Republican who was Mitt Romney’s running mate in 2012, has been on the road lately to promote his first book, “The Way Forward,” which enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 5. In it, Ryan offers voters — oops, readers! — a softer, more personal account of his life, discussing among other things his courtship of his wife and opening up for the first time about his father’s alcoholism and early death. “Before I lost him to a heart attack,” Ryan writes, “whiskey had washed away some of the best parts of the man I knew.” None of this, of course, means the book isn’t full of your basic agitprop. Running a column about it last month, one Texas newspaper used the succinct headline “Ryan Blames Everything on Left.” So is this the first volley in a presidential campaign? Ryan has been noncommittal in interviews, saying he’ll wait until 2015 to decide. And despite the book’s calculated stagecraft and careful red-meat rhetoric (“I believe the conservative vision can appeal to the majority of our fellow citizens”), there’s at least one good reason to think he’s telling the truth: His whistle-stop book tour includes no scheduled appearances in Iowa or New Hampshire.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31ryan/31ryan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 275, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31ryan/31ryan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 275, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31ryan/31ryan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31ryan/31ryan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/books/review/31ryan/31ryan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T14:50:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d0e770a-a889-5df4-8557-06ac20b51ed0', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d0e770a-a889-5df4-8557-06ac20b51ed0'}, {'abstract': 'Private developers have built more than 100 mixed-income buildings over the past decade, mostly in Manhattan and gentrified parts of Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/realestate/affordable-housing-in-new-yorks-luxury-buildings.html', 'snippet': 'Private developers have built more than 100 mixed-income buildings over the past decade, mostly in Manhattan and gentrified parts of Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brandon Deese lives just a few blocks from where he grew up, but it might as well be another world. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/realestate/31COVER/31cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/realestate/31COVER/31cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/realestate/31COVER/31cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/realestate/31COVER/31cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/realestate/31COVER/31COVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/realestate/31COVER/31COVER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/realestate/31COVER/31COVER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Living in the Mix', 'kicker': None, 'con