ork', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ed19731-ac6b-5d3b-97d8-b34e6de2f16e', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ed19731-ac6b-5d3b-97d8-b34e6de2f16e'}, {'abstract': 'As summer fades, women are adjusting to autumn with a deeper palette.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/fashion/bill-cunningham-transition-to-fall.html', 'snippet': 'As summer fades, women are adjusting to autumn with a deeper palette.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Crossroad As summer fades, women are adjusting to autumn with a deeper palette, from fir-tree greens to burgundies, charcoal and black. This summer will be remembered for its cool weather, calling for jackets and cardigans. The curiosities were the popularity of city shorts and one-piece playsuits, and of gladiator sandals. Lace shorts gained momentum for a second year. And the summer was not without whimsy.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Cunningham | Transition to Fall', 'kicker': 'On the Street', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crossroad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cunningham, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-29T23:40:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Cunningham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cunningham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59c92945-d344-532d-873d-6dd6e6a0e8e7', 'word_count': 67, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59c92945-d344-532d-873d-6dd6e6a0e8e7'}, {'abstract': 'Children, drivers and other residents have learned how to prepare for huge dust storms, sometimes called haboobs, that can turn day into night in seconds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/30/us/swirls-of-dust-and-drama-punctuating-life-in-the-southwest.html', 'snippet': 'Children, drivers and other residents have learned how to prepare for huge dust storms, sometimes called haboobs, that can turn day into night in seconds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHOENIX — The best way to explain a haboob is to say it is a tsunami of sand, in the sense that there is no stopping it or outrunning it. It is a supreme spectacle. The fierce winds that precede it make the leaves on palm trees stand as if they are hands waving an effusive goodbye, the sky darkens and the world takes the color of caramel as the dust swallows everything in its path.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/30/us/HABOOB/HABOOB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/30/us/HABOOB/HABOOB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/30/us/HABOOB/HABOOB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/30/us/HABOOB/HABOOB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0,