 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper and Michael R. Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1edc410-0a51-5063-93cb-18ecdc0f4357', 'word_count': 1225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1edc410-0a51-5063-93cb-18ecdc0f4357'}, {'abstract': 'More and more top-25 teams are facing off in the first couple weeks of the college football season, partly because of the influence of television and the new four-team playoff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/30/sports/ncaafootball/feast-of-season-openers-worthy-of-top-billing.html', 'snippet': 'More and more top-25 teams are facing off in the first couple weeks of the college football season, partly because of the influence of television and the new four-team playoff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday, No. 9 South Carolina was routed at home by No. 21 Texas A&M, 52-28, and newly ascendant Mississippi, ranked 18th, blasted formerly ascendant Boise State, 35-13, in Atlanta. On Saturday, No. 12 Georgia will host No. 16 Clemson; No. 1 Florida State will play Oklahoma State in Arlington, Tex.; and last year’s top midmajor, Central Florida, will play Penn State in Ireland — now that the volcano gods have forestalled a feared Icelandic eruption, permitting the teams to travel.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/30/sports/Y-JP-MATCHUP/Y-JP-MATCHUP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 