 None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b303a8c0-06a3-549f-867b-842f31e0c490', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b303a8c0-06a3-549f-867b-842f31e0c490'}, {'abstract': 'By the time he was 10, Scott Giordano knew that he wanted to be a chef. “I couldn’t stay out of the kitchen,” he said. Last May, he opened the Poached Pear Bistro, which he owns with his brother, Marc, in Point Pleasant Beach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/nyregion/a-review-of-the-poached-pear-bistro-in-point-pleasant-beach.html', 'snippet': 'By the time he was 10, Scott Giordano knew that he wanted to be a chef. “I couldn’t stay out of the kitchen,” he said. Last May, he opened the Poached Pear Bistro, which he owns with his brother, Marc, in Point Pleasant Beach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And still I think of the pear pizzetta appetizer. What a marvel: pizza dough cooked so expertly in a 500-degree convection oven that its light and flaky texture resembled that of pastry dough, with slivers of fresh pear, shaved radicchio, walnuts toasted to release oils that tasted like butter, and a thin layer of tangy Gorgonzola cheese running like lava across the surface of the whole, misshapen, messy pie. A drizzle of port wine, reduced until it resembled honey, united all the disparate elements. ', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/nyregion/31DINENJ2/31DINENJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/nyregion/31DINENJ2/31DINENJ2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/nyregion/31DINENJ2/31DINENJ2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy'