nhandle city. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/us/31ttCulture/31ttCulture-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 532, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/us/31ttCulture/31ttCulture-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 532}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/us/31ttCulture/31ttCulture-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 908, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/us/31ttCulture/31ttCulture-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1595, 'width': 1799, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/us/31ttCulture/31ttCulture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/08/31/us/31ttCulture/31ttCulture-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/us/31ttCulture/31ttCulture-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Community Makes Itself Known, With Discretion', 'kicker': 'Texas Monthly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Community Makes Itself Known, With Discretion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lubbock (Tex)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Monthly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-30T22:45:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robyn Ross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f34281e8-68a9-5683-bf61-a11262c70163', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f34281e8-68a9-5683-bf61-a11262c70163'}, {'abstract': 'Ahead of the 2015 legislative session, momentum seems to be building for more two-year institutions to get a chance to offer four-year degrees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/us/for-two-year-colleges-a-chance-to-grant-more-four-year-degrees.html', 'snippet': 'Ahead of the 2015 legislative session, momentum seems to be building for more two-year institutions to get a chance to offer four-year degrees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AUSTIN, Tex. — Since 2003, when South Texas College, Midland College and Brazosport College received permission to offer bachelor’s degrees, several other community colleges have tried to acquire the same authority.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Two-Year Colleges, a Chance to Grant More Four-Year Degrees', 'kicker': 'The Texas Tribune', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Two-Year Colleges, a Chance to Grant More Four-Year Degrees', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Community Colleges', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Tribune', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-30T22:50:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Reeve Hamilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Reeve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hamilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f99aeae0-3c2b-5f9b-86b8-7c68b25bc930', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f99aeae0-3c2b-5f9b-86b8-7c68b25bc930'}, {'abstract': '“James Brown Performance Compilation,” has two screenings on Sunday as part of a Film Society of Lincoln Center series on the godfather of soul.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/movies/out-of-sight-not-james-brown.html', 'snippet': '“James Brown Performance Compilation,” has two screenings on Sunday as part of a Film Society of Lincoln Center series on the godfather of soul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Brown’s 18-minute performance in the 1964 concert film “The T.A.M.I. Show,” in which he upstaged the Rolling Stones, is a performance as electrifying as Michael Jackson’s 1983 moonwalk dance moves, which it foreshadowed. It can be revisited in the “James Brown Performance Compilation,” which has two screenings on Sunday at the Walter Reade Theater, as part of the Film Society of Lincoln Center series James Brown: The Hardest Working Man in Show Business. Here is the godfather of soul in his limber prime, with his group the Famous Flames, performing a medley of “Out of Sight,” “Prisoner of Love,” “Please, Please, Please” (the high point) and “Night Train.” In the throes of passion, he feigns a collapse from which his musicians revive him, and executes a one-legged precursor of Jackson’s moonwalk. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out of Sight? Not James Brown', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out of Sight? Not James Brown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None