h and Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ab5698e-0676-5d83-a1dc-62e06f8085f9', 'word_count': 1261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ab5698e-0676-5d83-a1dc-62e06f8085f9'}, {'abstract': 'Some research suggests that exposure to soil bacteria could treat not just physical ailments but mental ones, too.', 'web_url': 'https://op-talk.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/08/31/the-secret-to-happiness-can-be-found-by-digging-in-the-dirt/', 'snippet': 'Some research suggests that exposure to soil bacteria could treat not just physical ailments but mental ones, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By now, many of us are familiar with the “hygiene hypothesis” — the idea that an environment that’s too clean may actually increase our risk of disease. This hypothesis usually gets discussed in terms of ailments like allergies or autoimmune disorders, but some research shows that dirt might be good for\xa0our mental health, too.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/opinion/nyto-dirt/nyto-dirt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/08/31/opinion/nyto-dirt/nyto-dirt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/08/31/opinion/nyto-dirt/nyto-dirt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',