comments from people who offered their own contributions or shared thoughts on how they viewed the arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Critics for The New York Times plumbed the depths of their disciplines this summer to play the role of curator as part of the Imagine This series. Their task was to create their own dream shows, festivals or exhibitions, and the results were diverse: a Shakespearean battle royale, an art museum of the mind, eclectic music festivals and an exhibition of video games designed by women. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/30/arts/brantley/brantley-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/07/30/arts/brantley/brantley-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/30/arts/brantley/brantley-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/30/arts/brantley/brantley-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/30/arts/brantley/brantley-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/07/30/arts/brantley/brantley-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/07/30/arts/brantley/brantley-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Critics Play Favorites, Readers Line Up to Join', 'kicker': 'Imagine This', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Critics Play Favorites, Readers Line Up to Join', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Suellentrop, Chris', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-31T21:21:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Roston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7d4f6f5-bcda-5671-aa99-0d6e88a257f1', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7d4f6f5-bcda-5671-aa99-0d6e88a257f1'}, {'abstract': 'A lost chapter of an earlier version of the 1964 book “Charlie and the Chocolate Factory” was recently published for the first time in The Guardian.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/01/books/want-more-wonka-a-lost-chapter-is-found.html', 'snippet': 'A lost chapter of an earlier version of the 1964 book “Charlie and the Chocolate Factory” was recently published for the first time in The Guardian.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It appears that Roald Dahl, below, once planned for Charlie Bucket to have a lot more company and a somewhat wilder adventure in “Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.” A lost chapter of an earlier version of this 1964 book that was found after Dahl’s death in 1990 was recently published for the first time in The Guardian. It reveals more temptations for a larger cast of characters and some variations on character names. The chapter opens with eight children — including Charlie, escorted by his mother instead of his Grandpa Joe — entering the Vanilla Fudge room. At this point, two other children, Miranda Grope and Augustus Pottle, who later became Augustus Gloop, have already fallen into the chocolate river. Inside the room, the visitors see a mountain of chocolate where hundreds of workers (later called Oompa-Loompas) are chipping away chunks of fudge that are then loaded onto wagons and transported through a dark hole in the wall. Two children, Tommy Troutbeck and Wilbur Rice, disobey Willy Wonka and jump into the wagons. They are quickly swept away through the hole into the “Pounding and Cutting Room.” Wonka tells the parents that this room is where the fudge is pounded “against the floor until it is all nice and smooth and thin.” After that, he says, “a whole lot of knives come down and go chop chop chop, cutting it up into neat little squares, ready for the shops.” The chapter did not make the book because it was “deemed too wild, subversive and insufficiently moral for the tender minds of British children almost 50 years ago,” The Guardian said. “Charlie and the Chocolate Factory” has sold more than 20 million copies worldwide in 55 languages, according to RoaldDahl.com, and has been made into two movies and a musical.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS02/ARTS02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS02/ARTS02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 796}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS02/ARTS02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 773, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS02/ARTS02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1630, 'width': 1229, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS02/ARTS02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS02/ARTS02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS02/ARTS02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Want More Wonka? A Lost Chapter Is Found', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Want More Wonka? A Lost Chapter Is Found', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dahl, Roald', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Guardian (British Newspaper)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-08-31T21:27:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Lori Holcomb-Holland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holcomb-Holland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/950450db-616a-5970-b72f-3831b2a80fcf', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/950450db-616a-5970-b72f-3831b2a80fcf'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Swift is to step in as an adviser to contestants on NBC’s “The Voice” this fall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/01/arts/television/taylor-swift-joins-mentors-on-the-voice.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Swift is to step in as an adviser to contestants on NBC’s “The Voice” this fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Players gonna play, haters gonna hate. Those and other words of wisdom from Taylor Swift’s latest Billboard-topping song, “Shake It Off,” might come in handy when she steps in as an adviser to contestants on NBC’s “The Voice” this fall. Ms. Swift, 24, left, is to appear as a mentor to competitors in its seventh season, Us Weekly reported. She’ll join previously announced guest mentors, including Gavin Rossdale, Stevie Nicks, Little Big Town and Alicia Keys, who are to work with members from teams led by the judges, Adam Levine, Blake Shelton, Gwen Stefani and Pharrell Williams. Ms. Swift, however, will work with competitors from all four teams, much as Chris Martin of Coldplay did in Season 6, according to US Weekly. NBC would not comment on the news. “The Voice” is scheduled to return on Sept. 22, and Ms. Swift is set to release her fifth studio album, “1989,” on Oct. 27.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS03/ARTS03-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 794, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS03/ARTS03-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 794}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/09/01/arts/ARTS03/ARTS03-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 774, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo'